window.OSH_CONTENT={"legalReference":[{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-i","title":"Chapter I — Preliminary (Sections 1–2)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s1","title":"Section 1 — Short title, commencement and application","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 1 · Chapter I <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">1. (1) This Act may be called the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) It shall come into force on such date as the Central Government may, by notification appoint; and different dates may be appointed for different provisions of this Code and any reference in any such provision to the commencement of this Code shall be construed as a reference to the coming into force of that provision.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) It shall not apply to the offices of the Central Government, offices of the State Government and any ship of war of any nationality:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the Code shall apply in case of contract labour employed through contractor in the offices of the Central Government or in the offices of the State Government, where, the Central Government or, as the case may be, the State Government is the principal employer.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.1; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 1<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Names the Code and lets the Central Government bring provisions into force on different dates.</p><p><strong>Exclusions.</strong> The Code does not apply to the offices of the Central and State Governments, or to ships of war. It <strong>does</strong> apply to contract labour engaged through a contractor in those offices, where the Government is the principal employer.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Government offices using contractors must apply the Code&#39;s contract labour provisions as principal employer.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Sub-section (1) reads &#39;This Act may be called…&#39;; reproduced as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.1. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Code does not apply to offices of the Central or State Government or to any ship of war (s.1(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">It applies to contract labour employed through a contractor in Government offices where the Government is the principal employer (proviso to s.1(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s2","title":"Section 2 — Definitions","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 2 · Chapter I <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">2. (1) In this Code, unless the context otherwise requires,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) &quot;adolescent&quot; shall have the same meaning as assigned to it in clause (i) of section 2 of the Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) &quot;adult&quot; means a person who has completed his eighteenth year of age;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) &quot;agent&quot; when used in relation to a mine, means every person, whether appointed as such or not, who, acting or purporting to act on behalf of the owner, takes part in the management, control, supervision or direction of such mine or of any part thereof;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) &quot;appropriate Government&quot; means—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in relation to, establishments [other than those specified in sub-clause (ii)] carried on by or under the authority of the Central Government or concerning any such controlled industry as may be specified in this behalf by the Central Government or the establishment of railways including metro railways, mines, oil field, major ports, air transport service or telecommunication service, banking company or any insurance company (by whatever name called) established by a Central Act or a corporation or other authority established by a Central Act or a Central public sector undertaking or subsidiary companies set up by the Central public sector undertakings or autonomous bodies owned or controlled by the Central Government, including establishment of contractors for the purposes of such establishment, corporation or other authority, Central public sector undertakings, subsidiary companies or autonomous bodies, as the case may be, the Central Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Provided that in the case of Central Public Sector Undertakings the appropriate Government shall continue to be the Central Government even if the holding of the Central Government reduces to less than fifty per cent. equity of the Central Government in that Public Sector Undertakings after the commencement of this Code; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) in relation to a factory, motor transport undertaking, plantation, newspaper establishment and establishment relating to beedi and cigar including the establishments not specified in clause (i), the concerned State Government where it is situated.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Explanation.—For the removal of doubts it is hereby clarified that State Government shall be the appropriate Government in respect of occupational safety, health and working conditions in a factory situated in that State;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) &quot;audio-visual production&quot; means audio-visual produced wholly or partly in India and includes—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) animation, cartoon depiction, audio-visual advertisement;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) digital production or any of the activities in respect of making thereof; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) features films, non-feature films, television, web-based serials, talk shows, reality shows and sport shows;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) &quot;audio-visual worker&quot; means a person, who is employed, directly or through any contractor, in or in connection with the audio-visual production to work as an artist including actor, musician, singer, anchor, news reader, dancer, dubbing artist or stunt person or to do any work, skilled, unskilled, manual, supervisory, technical, artistic or otherwise, and his remuneration with respect to such employment in or in connection with the production of audio-visual does not exceed, where remuneration is by way of monthly wages or where such remuneration is by way of lump sum, in each case, such amount as may be notified by the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) &quot;banking company&quot; means a banking company as defined in clause (c) of section 5 of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 and includes the Export-Import Bank of India, the Industrial Reconstruction Bank of India, the Small Industries Development Bank of India established under section 3 of the Small Industries Development Bank of India Act, 1989, the Reserve Bank of India, the State Bank of India, a corresponding new bank constituted under section 3 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act, 1970, a corresponding new bank constituted under section 3 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act, 1980;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) &quot;building or other construction work&quot; means the construction, alteration, repairs, maintenance or demolition in relation to buildings, streets, roads, railways, tramways, airfields, irrigation, drainage, embankment and navigation works, flood control works (including storm water drainage works), generation, transmission and distribution of power, water works (including channels for distribution of water), oil and gas installations, electric lines, internet towers, wireless, radio, television, telephone, telegraph and overseas communications, dams, canals, reservoirs, watercourses, tunnels, bridges, viaducts, aqua-ducts, pipelines, towers, cooling towers, transmission towers and such other work as may be specified in this behalf by the Central Government, by notification, but does not include building or other construction work which is related to any factory or mine and the building or other construction work where such work is for own residential purposes of an individual or group of individuals for their own residence and the total cost of such work does not exceed rupees fifty lakhs or such higher amount and employing more than such number of workers as may be notified by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) &quot;building worker&quot; means a person who is employed to do any highly skilled, skilled, semi-skilled or unskilled, manual, technical or clerical work for hire or reward, whether the terms of such employment are express or implied, in connection with any building or other construction work, but does not include any such person who is employed mainly in a managerial or supervisory or administrative capacity;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) &quot;cargo&quot; includes anything carried or to be carried in a ship or other vessel, or vehicle;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) &quot;Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator&quot; means a Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator appointed under sub-section (5) of section 34;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) &quot;competent person&quot;, means a person or an institution recognised as such by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator for the purposes of carrying out tests, examinations and inspections required to be done in an establishment having regard to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) the qualifications and experience of the person and facilities available at his disposal; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) the qualifications and experience of the persons employed in such institution and facilities available therein:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Provided that in case of mines the competent person includes such other person who is authorised by the manager referred to in section 67 to supervise or perform any work, or to supervise the operation of machinery, plant or equipment and is responsible for such duties assigned to him and also includes a shot firer or blaster;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) &quot;contract labour&quot; means a worker who shall be deemed to be employed in or in connection with the work of an establishment when he is hired in or in connection with such work by or through a contractor, with or without the knowledge of the principal employer and includes inter-State migrant worker but does not include a worker (other than part time employee) who is regularly employed by the contractor for any activity of his establishment and his employment is governed by mutually accepted standards of the conditions of employment (including engagement on permanent basis), and gets periodical increment in the pay, social security coverage and other welfare benefits in accordance with the law for the time being in force in such employment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(n) &quot;contractor&quot;, in relation to an establishment, means a person, who—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) undertakes to produce a given result for the establishment, other than a mere supply of goods or articles of manufacture to such establishment, through contract labour; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) supplies contract labour for any work of the establishment as mere human resource,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">and includes a sub-contractor;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(o) &quot;controlled industry&quot; means any industry the control of which by the Central Government has been declared under any Central Act in the public interest;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(p) &quot;core activity of an establishment&quot; means any activity for which the establishment is set up and includes any activity which is essential or necessary to such activity:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that the following shall not be considered as essential or necessary activity, if the establishment is not set up for such activity, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) sanitation works, including sweeping, cleaning, dusting and collection and disposal of all kinds of waste;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) watch and ward services including security services;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) canteen and catering services;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) loading and unloading operations;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) running of hospitals, educational and training Institutions, guest houses, clubs and the like where they are in the nature of support services of an establishment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(vi) courier services which are in nature of support services of an establishment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(vii) civil and other constructional works, including maintenance;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(viii) gardening and maintenance of lawns and other like activities;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ix) housekeeping and laundry services, and other like activities, where these are in nature of support services of an establishment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(x) transport services including, ambulance services;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(xi) any activity of intermittent nature even if that constitutes a core activity of an establishment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(q) &quot;day&quot; means a period of twenty-four hours beginning at mid-night;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(r) &quot;District Magistrate&quot;, in relation to any mine, means the District Magistrate or the Deputy Commissioner, as the case may be, who is vested with the executive powers of maintaining law and order in the revenue district in which the mine is situated:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that in case of a mine, which is situated partly in one district and partly in another, the District Magistrate for the purpose shall be the District Magistrate authorised in this behalf by the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(s) &quot;dock work&quot; means any work in or within the vicinity of any port in connection with, or required for, or incidental to, the loading, unloading, movement or storage of cargoes into or from ship or other vessel, port, dock, storage place or landing place, and includes—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) work in connection with the preparation of ships or other vessels for receipt or discharge of cargoes or leaving port;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) all repairing and maintenance processes connected with any hold, tank structure or lifting machinery or any other storage area on board the ship or in the docks; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) chipping, painting or cleaning of any hold, tank, structure or lifting machinery or any other storage area on board the ship or in the docks;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(t) &quot;employee&quot; means,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in respect of an establishment, a person (other than an apprentice engaged under the Apprentices Act, 1961) employed on wages by an establishment to do any skilled, semi-skilled, unskilled, manual, operational, supervisory, managerial, administrative, technical, clerical or any other work, whether the terms of employment be express or implied; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) a person declared to be an employee by the appropriate Government,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">but does not include any member of the Armed Forces of the Union:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that notwithstanding anything contained in this clause, in case of a mine a person is said to be &quot;employed&quot; in a mine who works as the manager or who works under appointment by the owner, agent or manager of the mine or with the knowledge of the manager, whether for wages or not—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(a) in any mining operation (including the concomitant operations of handling and transport of minerals up to the point of dispatch and of gathering sand and transport thereof to the mine);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(b) in operations or services relating to the development of the mine including construction of plant therein but excluding construction of buildings, roads, wells and any building work not directly connected with any existing or future mining operations;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(c) in operating, servicing, maintaining or repairing any part of any machinery used in or about the mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(d) in operations, within the premises of the mine, of loading for dispatch of minerals;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(e) in any office of mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(f) in any welfare, health, sanitary or conservancy services required to be provided under this Code relating to mine, or watch and ward, within the premises of the mine excluding residential area; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(g) in any kind of work, whatsoever, which is preparatory or incidental to, or connected with, mining operations;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(u) &quot;employer&quot; means a person who employs, whether directly or through any person, or on his behalf, or on behalf of any person, one or more employees in his establishment and where the establishment is carried on by any Department of the Central Government or the State Government, the authority specified, by the head of such Department, in this behalf or where no authority, is so specified, the head of the Department and in relation to an establishment carried on by a local authority, the Chief Executive of that authority, and includes,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in relation to an establishment which is a factory, the occupier of the factory;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) in relation to mine, the owner of the mine, agent or manager referred to in section 67;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) in relation to any other establishment, the person who, or the authority which has ultimate control over the affairs of the establishment and where said affairs are entrusted to a manager or managing director, such manager or managing director;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) contractor; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) legal representative of a deceased employer;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) &quot;establishment&quot; means—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) a place where any industry, trade, business, manufacturing or occupation is carried on in which ten or more workers are employed; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) motor transport undertaking, newspaper establishment, audio-video production, building and other construction work or plantation, in which ten or more workers are employed; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) factory, for the purpose of Chapter II, in which ten or more workers are employed, notwithstanding the threshold of workers provided in clause (w); or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) a mine or port or vicinity of port where dock work is carried out:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Provided that in sub-clauses (i) and (ii), the threshold of worker specified therein shall not be applicable in case of such establishment or class of establishments, in which such hazardous or life threatening activity is being carried on, as may be notified by the Central Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Provided further that notwithstanding any threshold provided in the definition of factory in clause (w), for the purposes of Chapter II, the establishment specified in sub-clause (i) or sub-clause (ii) or sub-clause (iii) shall be deemed to be the establishment within the meaning of this clause though the number of employees employed are ten or more;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(w) &quot;factory&quot; means any premises including the precincts thereof—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) whereon twenty or more workers are working, or were working on any day of the preceding twelve months, and in any part of which a manufacturing process is being carried on with the aid of power, or is ordinarily so carried on; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) whereon forty or more workers are working, or were working on any day of the preceding twelve months, and in any part of which a manufacturing process is being carried on without the aid of power, or is ordinarily so carried on,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">but does not include a mobile unit belonging to the armed forces of the Union, railways running shed or a hotel, restaurant or eating place:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that where under any law for the time being in force in a State immediately before the commencement of this Code, the number of workers specified is more or less than the number specified in clause (i) or clause (ii), then, the number specified under the law of the State shall prevail in that State till it is amended by the competent Legislature.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Explanation I.—For computing the number of workers for the purposes of this clause all the workers (in different groups and relays) in a day shall be taken into account.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Explanation II.—For the purposes of this clause, the mere fact that an Electronic Data Processing Unit or a Computer Unit is installed in any premises or part thereof, shall not be construed as factory if no manufacturing process is being carried on in such premises or part thereof;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(x) &quot;family&quot;, when used in relation to a worker, means—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) spouse;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) children including adopted children of the worker who are dependent upon him and have not completed the age of eighteen years; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) parents, grand-parents, widowed daughter and widowed sister dependent upon such worker.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this clause, such dependents shall not be included who are, for the time being, getting such income from such sources, as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(y) &quot;godown&quot; means any warehouse or other place, by whatever name called, used for the storage of any article or substance required for any manufacturing process which means any process for, or incidental to, making, finishing or packing or otherwise treating any article or substance with a view to its use, sale, transport, delivery or disposal as finished products;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(z) &quot;hazardous&quot; means involving danger or potential danger;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(za) &quot;hazardous process&quot; means any process or activity in relation to an industry or plantation specified in the First Schedule where, unless special care is taken, raw materials used therein or the intermediate or finished products, bye-products, hazardous substances, wastes or effluents thereof or spraying of any pesticides, insecticides or chemicals used therein, as the case may be, would—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) cause material impairment to the health of the persons engaged in or connected therewith, or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) result in the pollution of the general environment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zb) &quot;hazardous substance&quot; means any substance or such quantity of the substance as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government or preparation of which by reason of its chemical or physio-chemical properties or handling is liable to cause physical or health hazards to human being or may cause harm to other living creatures, plants, micro-organisms, property or the environment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zc) &quot;industrial premises&quot; means any place or premises (not being a private dwelling house), including the precincts thereof, in which or in any part of which any industry, trade, business, occupation or manufacturing is being ordinarily carried on with or without the aid of power and includes a godown attached thereto;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zd) &quot;industry&quot; means any systematic activity carried on by co-operation between an employer and worker (whether such worker is employed by such employer directly or by or through any agency, including a contractor) for the production, supply or distribution of goods or services with a view to satisfy human wants or wishes (not being wants or wishes which are merely spiritual or religious in nature), whether or not,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) any capital has been invested for the purpose of carrying on such activity; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) such activity is carried on with a motive to make any gain or profit,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">but does not include—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(a) institutions owned or managed by organisations wholly or substantially engaged in any charitable, social or philanthropic services; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(b) any activity of the appropriate Government relatable to the sovereign functions of the appropriate Government including all the activities carried on by the Departments of the Central Government dealing with defence research, atomic energy and space; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(c) any domestic service; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(d) any other activity as may be notified by the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ze) &quot;Inspector-cum-Facilitator&quot; means an Inspector-cum-Facilitator appointed under sub-section (1) of section 34;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zf) &quot;inter-State migrant worker&quot; means a person who is employed in an establishment and who—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) has been recruited directly by the employer or indirectly through contractor in one State for employment in such establishment situated in another State; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) has come on his own from one State and obtained employment in an establishment of another State (hereinafter called destination State) or has subsequently changed the establishment within the destination State,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">under an agreement or other arrangement for such employment and draws wages not exceeding the amount of rupees eighteen thousand per month or such higher amount as may be notified by the Central Government from time to time;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zg) &quot;machinery&quot; means any article or combination of articles assembled, arranged or connected and which is used or intended to be used for converting any form of energy to perform work, or which is used or intended to be used, whether incidental thereto or not, for developing, receiving, storing, containing, confining, transforming, transmitting, transferring or controlling any form of energy;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zh) &quot;major port&quot; means a major port as defined in clause (8) of section 3 of the Indian Ports Act, 1908;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zi) &quot;manufacturing process&quot; means any process for—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) making, altering, repairing, ornamenting, finishing, packing, oiling, washing, cleaning, breaking up, demolishing, or otherwise treating or adapting any article or substance with a view to its use, sale, transport, delivery or disposal; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) pumping oil, water, sewage or any other substance; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) generating, transforming or transmitting power; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) composing, printing, printing by letter press, lithography, offset, photogravure screen printing, three Dimensional or four Dimensional printing, prototyping, flexography or other types of printing process or book binding; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) constructing, reconstructing, repairing, refitting, finishing or breaking up ships or vessels; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(vi) preserving or storing any article in cold storage; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(vii) such other processes as the Central Government may notify;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zj) &quot;medical officer&quot; means the medical officer appointed under sub-section (1) of section 42;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zk) &quot;metro railway&quot; means the metro railway as defined in sub-clause (i) of clause (1) of section 2 of the Metro Railways (Operation and Maintenance) Act, 2002;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zl) &quot;mine&quot; means any excavation where any operation for the purpose of searching for or obtaining minerals has been or is being carried on and includes—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) all borings, bore holes, oil wells and accessory crude conditioning plants, including the pipe conveying mineral oil within the oilfields;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) all shafts, in or adjacent to and belonging to a mine, whether in the course of being sunk or not;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) all levels and inclined planes in the course of being driven;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) all open cast workings;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) all conveyors or aerial ropeways provided for bringing into or removal from a mine of minerals or other articles or for the removal of refuse therefrom;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(vi) all adits, levels, planes, machinery, works, railways, tramways and sidings in or adjacent to and belonging to a mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(vii) all protective works being carried out in or adjacent to a mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(viii) all workshops and stores situated within the precincts of a mine and under the same management and used primarily for the purposes connected with that mine or a number of mines under the same management;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ix) all power stations, transformer sub-stations, converter stations, rectifier stations and accumulator storage stations for supplying electricity solely or mainly for the purpose of working the mine or a number of mines under the same management;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(x) any premises for the time being used for depositing sand or other material for use in a mine or for depositing refuse from a mine or in which any operations in connection with such sand refuse or other material is being carried on, being premises exclusively occupied by the owner of the mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(xi) any premises in or adjacent to and belonging to a mine on which any process ancillary to the getting, dressing or preparation for sale of minerals or coke is being carried on;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(xii) a mine owned by the Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zm) &quot;minerals&quot; means all substances which can be obtained from the earth by mining, digging, drilling, dredging, hydraulicing, quarrying or by any other operation and includes mineral oils (such as natural gas and petroleum);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zn) &quot;motor transport undertaking&quot; means a motor transport undertaking employing motor transport worker and engaged in carrying passengers or goods or both by road for hire or reward, and includes a private carrier;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zo) &quot;motor transport worker&quot; means a person who is employed in a motor transport undertaking directly or through an agency, whether for wages or not, to work in a professional capacity on a transport vehicle or to attend the duties in connection with the arrival, departure, loading or unloading of such transport vehicle and includes a driver, conductor, cleaner, station staff, line checking staff, booking clerk, cash clerk, depot clerk, time-keeper, watchman or attendant, but does not include any such person—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) who is employed in a factory;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) to whom the provisions of any other law for the time being in force regulating the conditions of service of persons employed in shops or commercial establishments apply;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zp) &quot;newspaper&quot; means any printed periodical work containing public news or comments on public news and includes such other class of printed periodical work as may, from time to time, be notified in this behalf by the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zq) &quot;newspaper establishment&quot; means an establishment under the control of any person or body of persons, whether incorporated or not, for the production or publication of one or more newspapers or for conducting any news agency or syndicate and includes following newspaper establishments which shall be deemed to be one establishment, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) two or more newspaper establishments under common control;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) two or more newspaper establishments owned by an individual and his or her spouse unless it is shown that such spouse is a sole proprietor or partner or a shareholder of a corporate body on the basis of his or her own individual funds;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) two or more newspaper establishments publishing newspapers bearing the same or similar title and in the same language in any place in India or bearing the same or similar title but in different languages in the same State or Union territory.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Explanation 1.—For the purposes of sub-clause (i) two or more establishments shall be deemed to be under common control where—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(a) (i) the newspaper establishments are owned by a common individual or individuals;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(ii) the newspaper establishments are owned by firms, if such firms have a substantial number of common partners;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(iii) the newspaper establishments are owned by bodies corporate, if one body corporate is a subsidiary of the other body corporate, or both are subsidiaries of a common holding company or a substantial number of their equity shares are owned by the same person or group of persons, whether incorporated or not;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(iv) one establishment is owned by a body corporate and the other is owned by a firm, if a substantial number of partners of the firm together hold a substantial number of equity shares of the body corporate;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(v) one is owned by a body corporate and the other is owned by a firm having bodies corporate as its partners if a substantial number of equity shares of such bodies corporate are owned, directly or indirectly, by the same person or group of persons, whether incorporated or not, or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(b) there is functional integrality between concerned newspaper establishments.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Explanation 2.—For the purposes of this clause,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) different departments, branches and centres of newspaper establishments shall be treated as parts thereof;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) a printing press shall be deemed to be a newspaper establishment if the principal business thereof is to print newspaper;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zr) &quot;notification&quot; means a notification published in the Gazette of India or the Official Gazette of a State, as the case may be, and the expression &quot;notify&quot; with its grammatical variations and cognate expressions shall be construed accordingly;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zs) &quot;occupier&quot; of a factory means the person who has ultimate control over the affairs of the factory:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in the case of a firm or other association of individuals, any one of the individual partners or members thereof;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) in the case of a company, any one of the directors, except any independent director within the meaning of sub-section (6) of section 149 of the Companies Act, 2013;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) in the case of a factory owned or controlled by the Central Government or any State Government, or any local authority, the person or persons appointed to manage the affairs of the factory by the Central Government, the State Government or the local authority or such other authority as may be prescribed by the Central Government,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">shall be deemed to be the occupier:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided further that in the case of a ship which is being repaired, or on which maintenance work is being carried out, in a dry dock which is available for hire, the owner of the dock shall be deemed to be the occupier for all purposes except the matters as may be prescribed by the Central Government which are directly related to the condition of ship for which the owner of ship shall be deemed to be the occupier;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zt) &quot;office of the mine&quot; means an office at the surface of the mine concerned;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zu) &quot;open cast working&quot; means a quarry, that is to say, an excavation where any operation for the purpose of searching for or obtaining minerals has been or is being carried on, not being a shaft or an excavation which extends below superjacent ground;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zv) &quot;ordinarily employed&quot; with reference to any establishment or part thereof, means the average number of persons employed per day in the establishment or part thereof during the preceding calendar year obtained by dividing the number of man days worked by the number of working days excluding rest days and other non-working days;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zw) &quot;owner&quot;, in relation to a mine, means any person who is the immediate proprietor or lessee or occupier of the mine or of any part thereof and in case of a mine the business whereof is being carried on by a liquidator or receiver, such liquidator or receiver; but does not include a person who merely receives a royalty, rent or fine from the mine, or is merely the proprietor of the mine, subject to any lease grant or licence for the working thereof, or is merely the owner of the soil and not interested in the minerals of the mine; but any contractor or sub-lessee for the working of a mine or any part thereof shall be subject to this Code in like manner as if he were an owner but not so as to exempt the former from any liability;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zx) &quot;plantation&quot; means—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(a) any land used or intended to be used for—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(i) growing tea, coffee, rubber, cinchona or cardamom which admeasures five hectares or more;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(ii) growing any other plant, which admeasures five hectares or more and in which persons are employed or were employed on any day of the preceding twelve months, if, after obtaining the approval of the Central Government, the State Government, by notification, so directs.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">Explanation.—Where any piece of land used for growing any plant referred to in this sub-clause admeasures less than five hectares and is contiguous to any other piece of land not being so used, but capable of being so used, and both such pieces of land are under the management of the same employer, then, for the purposes of this sub-clause, the former piece of land shall be deemed to be a plantation, if the total area of both such pieces of land admeasures five hectares or more; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(b) any land which the State Government may, by notification, declare and which is used or intended to be used for growing any plant referred to in sub-clause (a), notwithstanding that it admeasures less than five hectares:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Provided that no such declaration shall be made in respect of such land which admeasures less than five hectares immediately before the commencement of this Code; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(c) offices, hospitals, dispensaries, schools and any other premises used for any purpose connected with any plantation within the meaning of sub-clause (a) and sub-clause (b); but does not include factory on the premises;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zy) &quot;prescribed&quot; means prescribed by rules made by the appropriate Government under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zz) &quot;principal employer&quot;, where the contract labour is employed or engaged, means—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in relation to any office or Department of the Government or a local authority, the head of that office or Department or such other officer as the Government or the local authority, may specify in this behalf;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) in a factory, the owner or occupier of the factory and where a person has been named as the manager of the factory, the person so named;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) in a mine, the owner or agent of the mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) in relation to any other establishment, any person responsible for the supervision and control of the establishment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zza) &quot;producer&quot;, in relation to audio-visual production, means the company, firm or other person by whom the arrangements necessary for producing such audio-visual (including the raising of finances and engaging audio-visual workers for producing audio-visual) are undertaken.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this clause, the expressions &quot;company&quot; and &quot;firm&quot; have the same meaning as respectively assigned to them in the Companies Act, 2013 and the Indian Partnership Act, 1932;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzb) &quot;qualified medical practitioner&quot; means a medical practitioner who possesses any recognised medical qualification as defined in clause (i) of section 2 of the Indian Medical Council Act, 1956 and who is enrolled on a Indian Medical Register as defined in clause (e) and on a State Medical Register as defined in clause (l) of the said section;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzc) &quot;railway&quot; means the railway as defined in clause (31) of section 2 of the Railways Act, 1989;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzd) &quot;relay&quot; means a set of two or more persons carrying out the same kind of work during different periods of the day and each such period is called a &quot;shift&quot;;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zze) &quot;sales promotion employees&quot; means any person by whatever name called employed or engaged in any establishment for hire or reward to do any work relating to promotion of sales or business, or both, but does not include any such person who,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) being employed or engaged in a supervisory capacity, draws wages exceeding eighteen thousand rupees per mensem or an amount as may be notified by the Central Government from time to time; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) is employed or engaged mainly in a managerial or administrative capacity.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzf) &quot;Schedule&quot; means the Schedule appended to this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzg) &quot;serious bodily injury&quot; means any injury which involves, or in all probability will involve, the permanent loss of any part or section of a body or the use of any part or section of a body, or the permanent loss of or injury to the sight or hearing or any permanent physical incapacity or the fracture of any bone or one or more joints or bones of any phalanges of hand or foot;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzh) &quot;standards&quot;, &quot;regulations&quot;, &quot;rules&quot;, &quot;bye-laws&quot; and &quot;orders&quot; respectively means standards, regulations, rules, bye-laws and orders made or declared, as the case may be, under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzi) &quot;telecommunication service&quot; means the telecommunication service as defined in clause (k) of sub-section (1) of section 2 of the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India Act, 1997;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzj) &quot;wages&quot; means all remuneration whether by way of salaries, allowances or otherwise, expressed in terms of money or capable of being so expressed which would, if the terms of employment, express or implied, were fulfilled, be payable to a person employed in respect of his employment or of work done in such employment, and includes,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) basic pay;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) dearness allowance; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) retaining allowance, if any,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">but does not include—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(a) any bonus payable under any law for the time being in force, which does not form part of the remuneration payable under the terms of employment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(b) the value of any house-accommodation, or of the supply of light, water, medical attendance or other amenity or of any service excluded from the computation of wages by a general or special order of the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(c) any contribution paid by the employer to any pension or provident fund, and the interest which may have accrued thereon;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(d) any conveyance allowance or the value of any travelling concession;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(e) any sum paid to the employed person to defray special expenses entailed on him by the nature of his employment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(f) house rent allowance;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(g) remuneration payable under any award or settlement between the parties or order of a court or Tribunal;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(h) any overtime allowance;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) any commission payable to the employee;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(j) any gratuity payable on the termination of employment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(k) any retrenchment compensation or other retirement benefit payable to the employee or any ex gratia payment made to him on the termination of employment:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that, for calculating the wages under this clause, if payments made by the employer to the employee under sub-clauses (a) to (i) exceeds one-half, or such other per cent. as may be notified by the Central Government, of the all remuneration calculated under this clause, the amount which exceeds such one-half, or the per cent. so notified, shall be deemed as remuneration and shall be accordingly added in wages under this clause:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided further that for the purpose of equal wages to all genders and for the purpose of payment of wages, the emoluments specified in sub-clauses (d), (f), (g) and (h) shall be taken for computation of wages.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Explanation.—Where an employee is given in lieu of the whole or part of the wages payable to him, any remuneration in kind by his employer, the value of such remuneration in kind which does not exceed fifteen per cent. of the total wages payable to him, shall be deemed to form part of the wages of such employee;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzk) &quot;week&quot; means a period of seven days beginning at midnight on Saturday night or such other night as may be approved in writing for a particular area by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzl) &quot;worker&quot; means any person employed in any establishment to do any manual, unskilled, skilled, technical, operational, clerical or supervisory work for hire or reward, whether the terms of employment be express or implied, and includes working journalists and sales promotion employees, but does not include any such person—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) who is subject to the Air Force Act, 1950, or the Army Act, 1950, or the Navy Act, 1957; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) who is employed in the police service or as an officer or other employee of a prison; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) who is employed mainly in a managerial or administrative capacity; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) who is employed in a supervisory capacity drawing wage exceeding eighteen thousand rupees per month or an amount as may be notified by the Central Government from time to time;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzm) &quot;Working Journalist&quot; means a person whose principal avocation is that of a journalist and who is employed as such, either whole-time or part-time, in, or in relation to, one or more newspaper establishment, or other establishment relating to any electronic media or digital media such as newspaper or radio or other likemedia and includes an editor, a leader-writer, news editor, sub-editor, feature-writer, copy-tester, reporter, correspondent, cartoonist, news-photographer and proof-reader, but does not include any such person who is employed mainly in a managerial, supervisory or administrative capacity;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) For the purposes of this Code, a person working or employed in or in connection with mine is said to be working or employed—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) &quot;below ground&quot; if he is working or employed—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in a shaft which has been or is in the course being sunk; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) in any excavation which extends below superjacent ground; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) &quot;above ground&quot; if he is working in an opencast working or in any other manner not specified in clause (a).</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.2; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 2<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The definitions decide coverage and who is responsible.</p><p><strong>Appropriate Government</strong> (cl. (d)).</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>Central Government:</strong> its own establishments; railways, metro railways, mines, oil fields, major ports, air transport, telecom, banking and insurance companies; Central PSUs and their subsidiaries; Central autonomous bodies; and contractors working for them. A Central PSU stays with the Centre even if Government equity falls below 50%.</li><li><strong>State Government:</strong> factories, motor transport undertakings, plantations, newspaper establishments, beedi and cigar establishments, and everything else. The Explanation confirms that the State is the appropriate Government for safety, health and working conditions <strong>in any factory</strong> in the State.</li></ul><p><strong>Establishment</strong> (cl. (v)). A place with <strong>10 or more workers</strong>, motor transport, newspapers, audio-visual production, building work or plantations with 10 or more workers, and any mine, port or dock work. The 10-worker threshold does not apply to notified hazardous or life-threatening activities.</p><p><strong>Factory</strong> (cl. (w)). Premises with <strong>20 or more workers</strong> using power, or <strong>40 or more</strong> without power, on any day in the preceding 12 months. Hotels, restaurants and eating places, and railway running sheds, are excluded. Different thresholds under prior State law continue until amended.</p><p><strong>Worker vs employee.</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><em>Employee</em> (cl. (t)) includes managerial and supervisory staff.</li><li><em>Worker</em> (cl. (zzl)) excludes managerial or administrative staff and supervisors earning above <strong>₹18,000 a month</strong> (or a notified amount); it includes working journalists and sales promotion employees.</li></ul><p><strong>Contract labour</strong> (cl. (m)) and <strong>contractor</strong> (cl. (n)) include a pure labour supplier. A contractor&#39;s own regular staff on permanent terms are not contract labour. <strong>Core activity</strong> (cl. (p)) excludes listed support services (sanitation, security, canteen, loading, housekeeping, transport and similar) unless the establishment is set up for them.</p><p><strong>Inter-State migrant worker</strong> (cl. (zf)). Recruited in one State for work in another, or moved on his own, with wages up to <strong>₹18,000 a month</strong> (or a notified higher amount).</p><p><strong>Wages</strong> (cl. (zzj)). The same 50% add-back as the other Codes.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>Check the appropriate Government</strong> for each site. Factories are always State-regulated for OSH. Central PSUs and listed sectors follow the Central Rules.</li><li><strong>Headcount:</strong> count &#39;workers&#39;, not &#39;employees&#39;, for most thresholds. Use the &#39;ordinarily employed&#39; average (cl. (zv)) where a provision says so.</li><li><strong>Contract labour</strong> includes manpower supply; review vendor contracts for core-activity issues (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\" data-ref=\"osh.s57\">Section 57</a>).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Several definitions refer to repealed Acts (for example, the Factories Act-era State thresholds saved by the proviso to cl. (w)); how far State thresholds survive depends on State law, which is outside scope.</li><li>Gratuity treatment in the 50% test is disputed; working treatment as in <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.faq.gratuity-fifty-percent\" data-ref=\"cow.faq.gratuity-fifty-percent\">Is gratuity counted when applying the 50% rule?</a>.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.2. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The State Government is the appropriate Government for occupational safety, health and working conditions in a factory situated in the State (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">An &#39;establishment&#39; includes a place where any industry, trade, business, manufacturing or occupation is carried on with ten or more workers (s.2(1)(v)(i)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A &#39;factory&#39; has twenty or more workers with power, or forty or more without power, on any day of the preceding twelve months (s.2(1)(w)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">&#39;Worker&#39; excludes supervisors drawing wages above ₹18,000 a month or a notified amount (s.2(1)(zzl)(iv)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">An inter-State migrant worker draws wages not exceeding ₹18,000 a month or a notified higher amount (s.2(1)(zf)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Wages include the excess of specified exclusions above one-half of all remuneration (first proviso to s.2(1)(zzj)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Whether gratuity counts in the 50% computation is disputed; working treatment: included.</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Interpretation</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">Disputed</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">See cow.s2 and the Code on Wages FAQ on the 50% rule.</div></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s1\">Section 1 — Short title, commencement and application</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\">Section 57 — Prohibition of employment of contract labour</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/irc.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-ii","title":"Chapter II — Registration (Sections 3–5)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s3","title":"Section 3 — Registration of certain establishments","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 3 · Chapter II <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">3. (1) Every employer of any establishment,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) which comes into existence after the commencement of this Code; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) to which this Code shall apply,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">shall, within sixty days from the date of such applicability of this Code, make an application electronically to the registering officer appointed by the appropriate Government (hereinafter referred to as the registering officer) for the registration of such establishment:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the registering officer may entertain any such application for registration after the expiry of such period on payment of such late fees as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Every application under sub-section (1) shall be submitted to the registering officer in such manner, in such form, containing such particulars including the information relating to the employment of inter-State migrant workers and shall be accompanied by such fees as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) After the receipt of an application under sub-section (1), the registering officer shall register the establishment and issue a certificate of registration electronically to the employer thereof in such form and within such time and subject to such conditions as may be prescribed by the Central Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that if the registering officer fails to register an establishment under the application so made or to entertain the application within the prescribed period, then, such establishment shall be deemed to have been registered under this Code immediately on the expiration of such period and the electronic certificate of registration shall be auto generated and the responsibility of such failure shall be on the registering officer.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Any change in the ownership or management or in any particulars referred to in sub-section (2) which occurs after the registration of an establishment under this Code, shall be intimated by the employer electronically to the registering officer within thirty days of such change in such form as may be prescribed by the Central Government and thereafter the registering officer shall make amendment in the certificate of registration electronically in such manner as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) The employer of an establishment shall, within thirty days of the closing of the establishment—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) inform the closing of such establishment; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) certify payment of all dues to the workers employed in such establishment,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">to the registering officer in such manner as may be prescribed by the Central Government and the registering officer shall, on receiving such information and certificate remove such establishment from the register of establishments maintained by him and cancel the registration certificate of the establishment within sixty days from the receipt of such information :</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that if the registering officer fails to cancel the registration certification of the establishment under this sub-section within such sixty days, then, the registration certificate of such establishment shall be deemed to have been cancelled under this Code immediately on the expiration of such period of sixty days and the cancellation of registration certificate shall be auto generated and the responsibility of such failure shall be on the registering officer.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) If an employer of an establishment—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) has obtained the registration of his establishment by misrepresentation or suppression of any material fact, or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) has obtained the registration of his establishment so fraudulently or otherwise that the registration has become useless or ineffective to run the establishment,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">then, in case of clause (a) such misrepresentation or suppression of any material fact shall be deemed to be the contravention of the provisions of this Code for prosecution of the employer under section 94 without affecting the registration and running of the establishment and in case of clause (b) the registering officer may, after giving an opportunity to the employer of the establishment to be heard, revoke the registration by an order and such process for revocation shall be completed by the registering officer within sixty days from coming into his notice the facts specified in clause (b).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(7) No employer of an establishment who—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) has not registered the establishment under this section; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) has not preferred appeal under section 4 against the cancellation of the registration certificate of the establishment under sub-section (5) or revocation of the registration of the establishment under sub-section (6) or the appeal so preferred has been dismissed,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">shall employ any employee in the establishment.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(8) Notwithstanding anything contained in this Code, where any establishment, to which this Code applies, has already been registered under any—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) Central Labour law; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) any other law which may be notified by the Central Government and which applies to the establishment which is in existence at the time of the commencement of this Code,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">shall be deemed to have been registered under the provisions of this Code, subject to the condition that the registration holder provides the details of registration to the concerned registering officer within such time and in such form as may be prescribed.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.3; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 15<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Who registers.</strong> Every employer of an establishment to which the Code applies and which came into existence after commencement must apply electronically within <strong>60 days</strong> of the Code applying (late applications are accepted with a fee).</p><p><strong>Certificate.</strong> Issued electronically. If the registering officer does not act within the prescribed period, registration is <strong>deemed</strong> and the certificate is auto-generated.</p><p><strong>Changes and closure.</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Changes in ownership, management or particulars: notify within <strong>30 days</strong>.</li><li>On closure: within <strong>30 days</strong>, inform the officer and certify payment of all workers&#39; dues. The registration is cancelled within 60 days, otherwise cancellation is deemed.</li></ul><p><strong>Misrepresentation and fraud.</strong> Misrepresentation is prosecuted under s.94 without affecting the registration. Registration obtained fraudulently may be revoked after a hearing.</p><p><strong>No registration, no employment.</strong> An unregistered establishment may not employ anyone (s.3(7)).</p><p><strong>Existing registrations</strong> under Central labour laws are deemed registrations, if details are provided as prescribed.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 3):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>apply on the Shram Suvidha Portal in <strong>Form I</strong>; certificate in <strong>Form III</strong> within 7 days, or deemed;</li><li>establishments already registered under other Central labour laws must update their particulars in Form I <strong>within six months</strong> of the Rules coming into force;</li><li>update changes within 30 days; display the certificate; quote the registration number;</li><li>on closure (other than mines), file <strong>Form II</strong> within 30 days with a certificate of payment of dues; cancellation in Form IV.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rule 3(6) contains a proviso about cancellation that does not obviously fit the sub-rule on updating particulars; reproduced in the Rules as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 3 — Central sphere: registration in Form I; certificate in Form III; closure in Form II; cancellation in Form IV.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">New establishments apply for registration electronically within sixty days of the Code applying (s.3(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Registration is deemed if the registering officer does not act within the prescribed period (proviso to s.3(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No employer may employ any employee in an establishment that is not registered (s.3(7)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Establishments registered under any Central labour law are deemed registered, subject to providing details as prescribed (s.3(8)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: establishments registered under other Central labour laws update particulars in Form I within six months of the Rules coming into force (rule 3(6)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s4\">Section 4 — Appeal</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s5\">Section 5 — Notice by employer of commencement and cessation of operation</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\">Section 94 — General penalty for offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s3\">Section 3 — Registration and cancellation of an establishment</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s4","title":"Section 4 — Appeal","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 4 · Chapter II <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">4. (1) Any person aggrieved by an order made under section 3 may, within thirty days from the date on which the order is communicated to him, prefer an appeal to an appellate officer who shall be a person notified in this behalf by the appropriate Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the appellate officer may entertain the appeal after the expiry of the said period of thirty days, if he is satisfied that the appellant was prevented by sufficient cause from filing the appeal in time.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) On receipt of an appeal under sub-section (1), the appellate officer shall, after giving the appellant an opportunity of being heard, dispose of the appeal within a period of thirty days from the date of receipt of such appeal.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.4; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 16<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Appeals against registration orders under s.3 go to the notified appellate officer within <strong>30 days</strong> (later for sufficient cause). The appeal must be decided within 30 days after a hearing.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Diarise the 30-day window after any refusal, cancellation or revocation.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.4. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Appeal within thirty days; decided within thirty days of receipt (s.4).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\">Section 3 — Registration of certain establishments</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s5","title":"Section 5 — Notice by employer of commencement and cessation of operation","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 5 · Chapter II <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">5. (1) No employer of an establishment being factory or mine or relating to contract labour or building or other construction work shall use such establishment to commence the operation of any industry, trade, business, manufacturing or occupation thereon without sending notice of such purpose in such form and manner and to such authority and within such time as may be prescribed and shall also intimate the cessation thereof to the said authority in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The notice or intimation under sub-section (1) shall be given electronically.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.5; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 16<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Employers of factories, mines, contract labour establishments and building or other construction work must give <strong>electronic notice</strong> before commencing operations, and intimate cessation, as prescribed.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 4):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>contract labour and building work establishments: <strong>Form VI</strong> within <strong>30 days</strong> of commencement or cessation, to the registering officer and the Inspector-cum-Facilitator; a cessation notice must certify payment of dues and that the premises are free of hazardous chemicals;</li><li>mines: at least <strong>30 days&#39; prior notice</strong> in <strong>Form VII</strong> of commencement, reopening, cessation or abandonment.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 4 — Central sphere: notice of commencement and cessation; Forms VI and VII.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Factories, mines, contract labour establishments and building work need electronic notice of commencement and intimation of cessation (s.5).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: Form VI within thirty days of commencement or cessation for contract labour and building work; mines give thirty days&#39; prior notice in Form VII (rule 4).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\">Section 3 — Registration of certain establishments</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-iii","title":"Chapter III — Duties of Employer and Employees, etc. (Sections 6–15)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s6","title":"Section 6 — Duties of employer","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.appointment-letter"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 6 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">6. (1) Every employer shall,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) ensure that workplace is free from hazards which cause or are likely to cause injury or occupational disease to the employees;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) comply with the occupational safety and health standards declared under section 18 or the rules, regulations, bye-laws or orders made under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) provide such annual health examination or test free of costs to such employees of such age or such class of employees of establishments or such class of establishments, as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) provide and maintain, as far as is reasonably practicable, a working environment that is safe and without risk to the health of the employees;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) ensure the disposal of hazardous and toxic waste including disposal of e-waste;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) issue a letter of appointment to every employee on his appointment in the establishment, with such information and in such form as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government and where an employee has not been issued such appointment letter on or before the commencement of this Code, he shall, within three months of such commencement, be issued such appointment letter;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) ensure that no charge is levied on any employee, in respect of anything done or provided for maintenance of safety and health at workplace including conduct of medical examination and investigation for the purpose of detecting occupational diseases;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) relating to factory, mine, dock work, building or other construction work or plantation, ensure and be responsible for the safety and health of employees, workers and other persons who are on the work premises of the employer, with or without his knowledge, as the case may be.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Without prejudice to the generality of the provisions of sub-section (1), the duties of an employer shall particularly in respect of factory, mines, dock, building or other construction work or plantation include—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the provision and maintenance of plant and systems of work in the workplace that are safe and without risk to health;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the arrangements in the workplace for ensuring safety and absence of risk to health in connection with the use, handling, storage and transport of articles and substances;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the provision of such information, instruction, training and supervision as are necessary to ensure the health and safety of all employees at work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) the maintenance of all places of work in the workplace in a condition that is safe and without risk to health and the provision and maintenance of such means of access to, and egress from, such places as are safe and without such risk;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) the provision, maintenance or monitoring of such working environment in the workplace for the employees that is safe, without risk to health as regards facilities and arrangements for their welfare at work.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.6; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 16<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>General duties of every employer</strong> (sub-s. (1)):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>keep the workplace free from hazards causing injury or occupational disease;</li><li>comply with the standards, rules, regulations and orders;</li><li>provide prescribed <strong>annual health examinations</strong> free of cost;</li><li>maintain a safe working environment;</li><li>dispose of hazardous and toxic waste, including e-waste;</li><li>issue an <strong>appointment letter</strong> to every employee (existing employees within three months of commencement);</li><li>charge employees nothing for safety and health measures;</li><li>in factories, mines, docks, building work and plantations, be responsible for the safety of everyone on the premises.</li></ul><p><strong>Particular duties</strong> (sub-s. (2)) in those sectors: safe plant and systems of work; safe handling of substances; information, training and supervision; safe access and egress; and a safe working environment with welfare arrangements.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>Appointment letters</strong> are a universal duty. Central sphere: use the rule 6 format (identity, Aadhaar with consent, UAN or ESI number, employment type, skill category, wages and allowances, social security, maternity benefits for women).</li><li><strong>Existing employees</strong> needed letters within three months of 21 November 2025, that is by about <strong>21 February 2026</strong>.</li><li><strong>Central sphere: annual health examination</strong> (rule 5): free examination for employees aged <strong>40 or more</strong> in dock work and building work, certified in Form VIII (ESIC facilities may be used).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rule 5 prescribes annual health examinations only for dock work and building work (and mines under rule 109). Other classes depend on further prescription.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 5 — Central sphere: annual health examination (dock work, building work).</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 6 — Central sphere: appointment letter format.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Appointment letter: what it must contain (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Appointment letter: what it must contain (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rule 6 format. The duty in s.6(1)(f) applies to every establishment; the format is prescribed by the appropriate Government.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Identity</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Name; date of birth; father&#39;s or mother&#39;s name; Aadhaar number (with consent)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (i)–(iv)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employer</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Labour Identification Number of the establishment</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (v)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Social security IDs</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">UAN and/or ESI insurance number (if available)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (vi)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Job</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Designation; type of employment (regular, fixed term, contractual); skill category; date of joining; broad duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (vii)–(x), (xiv)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Pay</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Wages/basic pay and DA; other allowances including accommodation</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (xi)–(xii)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Benefits</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">EPF and ESI applicability; maternity benefits (women employees)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (xiii), (xv)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Deadline for existing staff</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Within three months of commencement (about 21 February 2026)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.6(1)(f)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.6(1)(f); <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 6</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Every employee must receive an appointment letter; existing employees within three months of commencement (s.6(1)(f)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No charge may be levied on employees for safety and health measures, including medical examinations (s.6(1)(g)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: free annual medical examination for dock and building workers aged forty or more, certified in Form VIII (rule 5).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: no employee may be employed without an appointment letter in the prescribed format (rule 6).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s13\">Section 13 — Duties of employee</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\">Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s97\">Section 97 — Punishment for contravention of certain provisions</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s7","title":"Section 7 — Duties and responsibilities of owner, agent and manager in relation to mine","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 7 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">7. (1) The owner and agent of every mine shall jointly and severally be responsible for making financial and other provisions and for taking such other steps as may be necessary for compliance with the provisions of this Code and the rules, regulations, bye-laws and orders made thereunder, relating to mine.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) In the event of any contravention by any person whosoever of any of the provisions of this Code or of the rules, regulations, bye-laws or orders made thereunder, relating to mine, except those which specifically require any person to do any act or thing or prohibit any person from doing an act or thing, besides the person who contravenes, then, each of the following persons shall also be deemed to be guilty of such contravention unless he proves that he had used due diligence to secure compliance with the provisions and had taken reasonable means to prevent such contravention, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the official or officials appointed to perform duties of supervision in respect of the provisions contravened;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the manager of the mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the owner and agent of the mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) the person appointed, if any, to carry out the responsibility under section 24.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) It shall not be a defence in any proceedings brought against the owner or agent of a mine under this section that the manager and other officials have been appointed in accordance with the provisions of this Code or that a person to carry the responsibility under section 24 has been appointed.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.7; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 17<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>In a mine, the owner and agent are jointly and severally responsible for compliance. On any contravention, supervising officials, the manager, the owner and agent, and any person appointed under s.24 are deemed guilty unless they prove due diligence. Appointing a manager is no defence for the owner or agent.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mine owners and agents should document the systems and resources they provide for compliance; due diligence is the only defence.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.7. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Owner and agent of a mine are jointly and severally responsible for compliance (s.7(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Officials, manager, owner and agent are deemed guilty of a contravention unless they prove due diligence (s.7(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s67\">Section 67 — Managers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s107\">Section 107 — Prosecution of owner, agent or manager of mine</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s108\">Section 108 — Exemption of owner, agent or manager of mine or occupier of factory from liability in certain cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s8","title":"Section 8 — Duties of designers, manufacturers, importers or suppliers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 8 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">8. (1) Every person who designs, manufactures, imports or supplies any article for use in any establishment shall—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) ensure so far as is reasonably practicable, that the article is so designed and constructed in the establishment as to be safe and without risk to the health of the workers when properly used;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) carry out or arrange for the carrying out of such tests and examination in the establishment as may be considered necessary for the effective implementation of the provisions of clause (a);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) take steps as may be necessary to ensure that adequate information will be available—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in connection with the use of the article in any establishment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) about the use for which such article is designed and tested; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) about any conditions necessary to ensure that the article, when put to such use, shall be safe, and without risk to the health of the workers:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">Provided that where an article is designed or manufactured outside India, then it shall be obligatory on the part of the importer to see—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(A) that the article conforms to the same standards of such article manufactured in India; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(B) if the standards adopted in the country outside India for the manufacture of such article is above the standards adopted in India, that the article conforms to such standards in such country;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(C) if there is no standard of such article in India, then, the article conforms to the standard adopted in the country from where it is imported at its national level.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The designer, manufacturer, importer or supplier shall also comply with such duties as the Central Government may, in consultation with the National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board referred to in sub-section (1) of section 16, by regulations specify.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Every person, who undertakes to design or manufacture any article and substance for use in any factory, may carry out or arrange for the carrying out of necessary research with a view to the discovery and, so far as is reasonably, practicable, the elimination or minimisation of any risks to the health or safety of the workers to which the design or manufacture of article and substance may give rise to such risk.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Nothing contained in sub-sections (1) and (2) shall be construed to require a person to repeat the testing, examination or research which has been carried out otherwise than by him or at his instance in so far as it is reasonable for him to rely on the results thereof for the purposes of the said sub-sections.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Any duty imposed on any person by sub-sections (1) and (2) shall extend only to things done in the course of business carried on by him and to matters within his control.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) Every person,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) who erects or installs any article for use in a factory, shall ensure, so far as practicable, that such article so erected or installed does not make it unsafe or a risk to health when that article is used by the persons in such factory;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) who manufactures, imports or supplies any substance for use in any factory shall—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) ensure, so far as practicable, that such substance when used in the factory does not make it unsafe or a risk to health of persons working in such factory;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) carry out or arrange for carrying out of such tests and examination in relation to such substance as may be necessary;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) take such steps as are necessary to secure that the information about the results of tests carried out in connection with the use of the substance as referred to in sub-clause (ii) is available in a factory along with conditions necessary to ensure its safe use and no risks to health;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) who undertakes the manufacture of any substance for use in any factory shall carry out or arrange for carrying out of any necessary research with a view to discover and, so far as practicable, to ensure the elimination or minimisation of any risks to health or safety to which the substance may give rise out of such manufacture or research;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(7) For the purposes of this section, an article and substance is not to be regarded as properly used, if they are used without regard to any information or advice relating to their use which has been made available by the person who has designed, manufactured, imported or supplied the article and substance.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purpose of this section—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) &quot;article&quot; shall include plant and machinery;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) &quot;substance&quot; means any natural or artificial substance whether in a solid or liquid form or in the form of a gas or vapour; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) &quot;substance for use in any factory&quot; means such substance, whether or not intended for use by persons working in a factory.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.8; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 18<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Designers, manufacturers, importers and suppliers of articles for use in establishments must:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>ensure, so far as reasonably practicable, the article is safe when properly used;</li><li>arrange tests and examinations;</li><li>make information available on use, design purpose and conditions for safe use.</li></ul><p><strong>Imported articles</strong> must meet Indian standards, or the foreign standard if higher; if India has no standard, the standard of the country of origin. Similar duties apply to installers and to suppliers of substances for factories. Misuse contrary to supplied information is not &#39;proper use&#39;.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Procurement: require conformity evidence and safe-use information from suppliers, especially for imported machinery.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.8. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Designers, manufacturers, importers and suppliers must ensure articles are safe when properly used, and provide information (s.8(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Imported articles must conform to Indian standards, or a higher foreign standard (proviso to s.8(1)(c)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\">Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s9","title":"Section 9 — Duties of architect, project engineer and designer","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 9 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">9. (1) It shall be the duty of the architect, project engineer or designer responsible for any building or other construction work or the design of any project or part thereof relating to such building or other construction work to ensure that, at the planning stage, due consideration is given to the safety and health aspects of the building workers and employees who are employed in the erection, operation and execution of such projects and structures as the case may be.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Adequate care shall be taken by the architect, project engineer and other professionals involved in the project referred to in sub-section (1), not to include anything in the design which would involve the use of dangerous structures or other processes or materials, hazardous to health or safety of building workers and employees during the course of erection, operation and execution as the case may be.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) It shall also be the duty of the professionals, involved in designing the buildings structures or other construction projects, to take into account the safety aspects associated with the maintenance and upkeep of the structures and buildings where maintenance and upkeep may involve such hazards as may be notified by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.9; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 19<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Architects, project engineers and designers of building or construction projects must consider workers&#39; safety and health at the planning stage, avoid hazardous structures, processes or materials in the design, and account for the safety of maintenance work.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Developers should brief design consultants on these duties and keep design-stage safety reviews on file.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.9. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Architects, project engineers and designers must consider workers&#39; safety and health at the planning stage (s.9(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s78\">Section 78 — Prohibition of employment of certain persons in certain building or construction</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s10","title":"Section 10 — Notice of certain accident","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.incident-reporting"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 10 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">10. (1) Where at any place in an establishment, an accident occurs which causes death, or which causes any bodily injury by reason of which the person injured is prevented from working for a period of forty-eight hours or more immediately following the accident or which is of such nature as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government, then,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) employer or owner or agent or manager referred to in section 67 of such establishment if it is mine; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) employer or manager in relation to such establishment if it is factory or relates to dock work; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the employer of a plantation or an establishment relating to building or other construction or any other establishment,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">shall send notice thereof to such authorities, in such manner and within such time, as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where a notice given under sub-section (1) relates to an accident causing death in a plantation or an establishment relating to building or other construction work or any other establishment, the authority to whom the notice is sent shall make an inquiry into the occurrence within two months of the receipt of the notice or if there is no such authority, the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall cause the Inspector-cum-Facilitator to make an inquiry within the said period.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.10; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 20<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Where an accident causes death, or injury keeping the person from work for <strong>48 hours or more</strong>, the employer (or mine owner, agent or manager; factory or dock manager) must send notice to the prescribed authorities. For deaths in plantations, building work and other establishments, the authority must inquire within two months.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 7):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>death:</strong> notice in <strong>Form XI forthwith</strong>, and by phone and electronically to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, Inspector-cum-Facilitator, District Magistrate or SDO, the police station and the victim&#39;s family;</li><li><strong>48-hour injury:</strong> Form XI electronically within <strong>12 hours after</strong> the 48 hours are complete;</li><li>a later death after notice: notify forthwith and confirm in writing within 12 hours.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 7 — Central sphere: notice of accidents and dangerous occurrences; Form XI.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rules 7–8. Also report to the competent authority under the SS Code within 7 days where applicable (SS s.73).</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-timeline\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: Form XI to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator; phone and electronic intimation to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, District Magistrate/SDO, police station and family</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Dangerous occurrence (listed)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Within 12 hours: intimation to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator and District Magistrate/SDO, injury or not</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(3)–(4)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Injury, 48 hours off work</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Form XI electronically within 12 hours after the 48 hours are complete</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(2)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Later death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith by phone and electronically; written confirmation within 12 hours of the death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">proviso to rule 7(3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Third Schedule disease</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: notice electronically or by speed post to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 8(1)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.10–12; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 7–8</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Accidents causing death or 48 hours&#39; or more incapacity must be notified to the prescribed authorities (s.10(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: fatal accidents notified forthwith in Form XI and by phone; 48-hour injuries within 12 hours after the 48 hours (rule 7(1)–(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s11\">Section 11 — Notice of certain dangerous occurrences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s12\">Section 12 — Notice of certain diseases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s33\">Section 33 — Maintenance of registers, records and filing of returns</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s73\">Section 73 — Reports of fatal accidents and serious bodily injuries</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s11","title":"Section 11 — Notice of certain dangerous occurrences","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.incident-reporting"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 11 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">11. Where in an establishment there is any dangerous occurrence of such nature, (whether causing any bodily injury or disability, or not) the employer shall send notice thereof to such authorities, and in such form and within such time, as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.11; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 20<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Dangerous occurrences of a prescribed nature must be notified, whether or not anyone is injured.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 7(3)–(4)): intimate the Inspector-cum-Facilitator and the District Magistrate or SDO <strong>within 12 hours</strong>. Listed occurrences include bursting of pressure plant, crane or hoist collapse, fire, explosion or toxic release, structural or scaffold collapse, chemical spillage, electrical flash-over burns, and mine-specific events.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 7(3)–(4) — Central sphere: list of dangerous occurrences.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rules 7–8. Also report to the competent authority under the SS Code within 7 days where applicable (SS s.73).</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-timeline\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: Form XI to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator; phone and electronic intimation to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, District Magistrate/SDO, police station and family</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Dangerous occurrence (listed)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Within 12 hours: intimation to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator and District Magistrate/SDO, injury or not</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(3)–(4)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Injury, 48 hours off work</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Form XI electronically within 12 hours after the 48 hours are complete</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(2)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Later death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith by phone and electronically; written confirmation within 12 hours of the death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">proviso to rule 7(3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Third Schedule disease</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: notice electronically or by speed post to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 8(1)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.10–12; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 7–8</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Prescribed dangerous occurrences must be notified whether or not they cause injury (s.11).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: dangerous occurrences intimated within twelve hours (rule 7(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s12","title":"Section 12 — Notice of certain diseases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.incident-reporting"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 12 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">12. (1) Where any worker in an establishment contracts any disease specified in the Third Schedule, the employer of the establishment shall send notice thereof to such authorities, and in such form and within such time, as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) If any qualified medical practitioner attends on a person, who is or has been employed in an establishment, and who is, or is believed by the qualified medical practitioner, to be suffering from any disease specified in the Third Schedule, the medical practitioner shall without delay send a report in writing to the office of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator in such form and manner and within such time as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) If any qualified medical practitioner fails to comply with the provisions of sub-section (2), he shall be punishable with penalty which may extend to ten thousand rupees.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.12; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 20<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The employer must notify the prescribed authorities when a worker contracts a <strong>Third Schedule</strong> disease. A medical practitioner attending such a case must report to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator; failure attracts a penalty of up to <strong>₹10,000</strong>.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 8): notice of disease forthwith, electronically or by speed post, in the prescribed format.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 8 — Central sphere: notice of disease format.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rules 7–8. Also report to the competent authority under the SS Code within 7 days where applicable (SS s.73).</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-timeline\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: Form XI to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator; phone and electronic intimation to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, District Magistrate/SDO, police station and family</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Dangerous occurrence (listed)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Within 12 hours: intimation to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator and District Magistrate/SDO, injury or not</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(3)–(4)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Injury, 48 hours off work</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Form XI electronically within 12 hours after the 48 hours are complete</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(2)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Later death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith by phone and electronically; written confirmation within 12 hours of the death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">proviso to rule 7(3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Third Schedule disease</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: notice electronically or by speed post to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 8(1)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.10–12; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 7–8</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employers must notify Third Schedule diseases (s.12(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A medical practitioner failing to report is liable to a penalty up to ₹10,000 (s.12(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: notice of disease forthwith (rule 8(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch3\">The Third Schedule — List of notifiable diseases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s13","title":"Section 13 — Duties of employee","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 13 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">13. Every employee at workplace shall,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) take reasonable care for the health and safety of himself and of other persons who may be affected by his acts or omissions at the workplace;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) comply with the safety and health requirements specified in the standards;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) co-operate with the employer in meeting the statutory obligations of the employer under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) if any situation which is unsafe or unhealthy comes to his attention, as soon as practicable, report such situation to his employer or to the health and safety representative and in case of mine, agent or manager referred to in section 67, safety officers or an official for his workplace or section thereof, as the case may be, who shall report it to the employer in the manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) not wilfully interfere with or misuse or neglect any appliance, convenience or other thing provided at workplace for the purpose of securing the health, safety and welfare of workers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) not do, wilfully and without reasonable cause, anything, likely to endanger himself or others; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) perform such other duties as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.13; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 20<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Every employee must:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>take reasonable care of his own and others&#39; safety;</li><li>comply with the standards;</li><li>co-operate with the employer;</li><li>report unsafe or unhealthy situations;</li><li>not misuse or interfere with safety appliances;</li><li>not endanger himself or others.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 9): an employee reports unsafe conditions to the employer, health and safety representative, safety officer or official, who reports to the employer immediately. Build this into induction and the reporting system.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Employee offences are dealt with in <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s106\" data-ref=\"osh.s106\">Section 106</a>.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 9 — Central sphere: reporting of unsafe conditions.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employees must take reasonable care, comply with standards and report unsafe situations (s.13).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s14\">Section 14 — Rights of employee</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s15\">Section 15 — Duty not to interfere with or misuse things</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s106\">Section 106 — Offences by employees</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s14","title":"Section 14 — Rights of employee","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 14 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">14. (1) Every employee in an establishment shall have the right to obtain from the employer information relating to employee&#39;s health and safety at work and represent to the employer directly or through a member of the Safety Committee as constituted under section 22, if constituted by the employer for such purpose, regarding inadequate provision for protection of his safety or health in connection with the work activity in the workplace, and if not satisfied, to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where the employee referred to in sub-section (1) in any workplace has reasonable apprehension that there is a likelihood of imminent serious personal injury or death or imminent danger to health, he may bring the same to the notice of his employer directly or through a member of the Safety Committee referred to in sub-section (1) and simultaneously bring the same to the notice of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The employer or any employee referred to in sub-section (1) shall take immediate remedial action if he is satisfied about the existence of such imminent danger and send a report forthwith of the action taken to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) If the employer referred to in sub-section (3) is not satisfied about the existence of any imminent danger as apprehended by his employees, he shall, nevertheless, refer the matter forthwith to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator whose decision on the question of the existence of such imminent danger shall be final.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.14; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 21<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Employees have the right to:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>obtain safety and health information from the employer;</li><li>represent on inadequate protection, directly or through the Safety Committee, and then to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</li></ul><p><strong>Imminent danger.</strong> An employee who reasonably apprehends imminent serious injury, death or danger to health may notify the employer and, simultaneously, the Inspector-cum-Facilitator. The employer must act at once and report. If the employer disagrees that danger exists, it must refer the matter to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator, whose decision is final.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 10): after any imminent-danger report, the employer takes immediate remedial action and reports the action taken to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator, whether or not the employee is satisfied.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 10 — Central sphere: action on imminent danger.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employees may report imminent danger to the employer and the Inspector-cum-Facilitator; the employer must act or refer the question (s.14(2)–(4)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: the employer reports remedial action to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator (rule 10).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s13\">Section 13 — Duties of employee</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\">Section 22 — Safety Committee and safety officers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s89\">Section 89 — Right of workers to warn about imminent danger</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s15","title":"Section 15 — Duty not to interfere with or misuse things","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 15 · Chapter III <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">15. No person shall intentionally or recklessly interfere with, damage or misuse anything which is provided in the interest of health, safety or welfare under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.15; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 21<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No person may intentionally or recklessly interfere with, damage or misuse anything provided for health, safety or welfare.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Applies to everyone, including contractors and visitors.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.15. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No person may interfere with or misuse safety, health or welfare provisions (s.15).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s13\">Section 13 — Duties of employee</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-iv","title":"Chapter IV — Occupational Safety and Health (Sections 16–22)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s16","title":"Section 16 — National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 16 · Chapter IV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">16. (1) The Central Government shall, by notification, constitute the National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board (hereinafter in this Code referred to as the National Board) to discharge the functions conferred on it by or under this Code and to advise the Central Government on the matters relating to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) standards, rules and regulations to be declared or framed under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) implementation of the provisions of this Code and the standards, rules and regulations relating thereto;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the issues of policy and programme relating to occupational safety and health referred to it, from time to time, by the Central Government; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) any other matter in respect of this Code referred to it, from time to time, by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The National Board shall consist of—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) Secretary, Ministry of Labour and Employment—Chairperson ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) Director General, Factory Advice Service and Labour Institutes, Mumbai— Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) Director General, Mines Safety, Dhanbad—Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) Chief Controller of Explosives, Nagpur—Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) Chairman, Central Pollution Control Board, New Delhi—Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) Chief Labour Commissioner (Central), New Delhi—Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) Principal Secretaries dealing with labour matters of four States (by rotation as the Central Government may deem fit)—Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) Director General, Employee&#39;s State Insurance Corporation, New Delhi— Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) Director General, Health Services, New Delhi—Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) five representatives of employers—Member ex officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) five representatives of employees—Member ex-officio;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) a representative of professional body associated with the matter for which standards, rules, policies being framed—Member;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) five eminent persons connected with the field of Occupational Safety and Health, or representatives from reputed research institutions or similar other discipline—Member;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(n) special invitees from the State Government or the Government of Union territory for seeking inputs in specific matters or industry or sector which is predominant in that State or Union territory—Member;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(o) Joint Secretary, Ministry of Labour and Employment—Member Secretary ex officio.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The terms of office of the Members referred to in clauses (g), (j), (k), (l) and (m) of sub-section (2) shall be of three years and the procedure for their nomination, and discharge of their functions shall be such as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) The Central Government may, in consultation with the National Board, determine the number, nature and categories of other officers and employees required to assist the National Board in the efficient discharge of its functions and terms and conditions of service of such officers and employees of the National Board shall be such as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) The Central Government may constitute as many technical committees or advisory committees consisting of such number of members having such qualifications as may be prescribed by the Central Government, to assist the National Board in discharge of its function specified in sub-section (1).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) The National Board shall consult the State Governments whose Principal Secretaries are the Members of the National Board as required under clause (g) of sub-section (2) of section 16 and in case of specific issues relating to plantation, factories and like other issues, the State Government concerned may be invited by the National Board as special invitee for obtaining their inputs on such issues.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.16; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 21<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government constitutes the <strong>National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</strong> to advise on standards, rules, regulations, implementation and policy. Members include Government officials, five employer and five employee representatives, and experts. Technical committees may assist it.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>No direct employer duty. The National Board&#39;s advice shapes standards under s.18.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 11–12 — National Board and technical committees.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board advises the Central Government (s.16(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s17\">Section 17 — State Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\">Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s17","title":"Section 17 — State Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 17 · Chapter IV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">17. (1)The State Government shall constitute a Board to be called the State Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board (hereinafter referred to as &quot;State Advisory Board&quot;) to advise the State Government on such matters arising out of the administration of this Code as may be referred to it by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The constitution, procedure and other matters relating to State Advisory Board shall be such as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The State Government may constitute as many technical committees or advisory committees of the State Advisory Board including site appraisal committees, consisting of such number of members and having such qualifications as may be prescribed, to assist the State Government or State Advisory Board in discharge of their functions relating to the area falling within their respective jurisdictions.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.17; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 22<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Each State constitutes a State Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board, and may set up committees, including site appraisal committees.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>No direct employer duty. State rules govern the State Board (outside scope).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter; Central Rules not applicable. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Each State constitutes a State Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board (s.17(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s16\">Section 16 — National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s83\">Section 83 — Constitution of site appraisal committee</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s18","title":"Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 18 · Chapter IV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">18. (1) The Central Government shall declare, by notification, standards on occupational safety and health for workplaces relating to factory, mine, dock work, beedi and cigar, building and other construction work and other establishments.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) In particular and without prejudice to the generality of the power to declare standards to be followed under sub-section (1), such standards shall relate to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) physical, chemical, biological and any other hazards to be dealt with for the working life of employee to ensure to the extent feasible on the basis of the best available evidence or functional capacity, that no employee will suffer material impairment of health or functional capacity even if such employee has regular exposure to such hazards;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the norms—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) appraising the hazards to employees and users to whom such hazards are exposed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) relating to relevant symptoms and appropriate energy treatment and proper conditions and precautions of safe use or exposure;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) for monitoring and measuring exposure of employees to hazards;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) for medical examination and other tests which shall be made available, by the employer or at his cost, to the employees exposed to hazards; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) for hazard evaluation procedures like safety audit, hazard and operability study, fault free analysis, event free analysis and such other requirements;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) medical examination including criteria for detection and reporting of occupational diseases to be extended to the employees even after he ceases to be in employment, if he is suffering from an occupational disease which arises out of or in the course of employment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) such aspects of occupational safety and health relating to workplaces which the Central Government considers necessary on the report of the authority designated by such Government for such purpose;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) such safety and health measures as may be required having regard to the specific conditions prevailing at the workplaces relating to mine, factory, building and other construction work, beedi and cigar, dock work or any other establishments notified; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) matters specified in the Second Schedule to this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Notwithstanding anything contained in section 131, the Central Government may, on the basis of the recommendation of the National Board and after notifying its intention so to do for not less than forty-five days&#39;, by notification, amend the Second Schedule.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) The State Government may, with the prior approval of the Central Government, by notification amend the standards made under sub-section (1) and sub-section (2) for the establishment for which it is the appropriate Government situated in the State.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.18; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 23<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government declares <strong>occupational safety and health standards</strong> by notification for factories, mines, docks, beedi and cigar, building work and other establishments. They cover hazards, exposure norms, monitoring, medical examinations (including after employment ends, for occupational disease), hazard evaluation (safety audit, HAZOP and similar) and the matters in the <strong>Second Schedule</strong>. States may amend standards for their establishments with the Centre&#39;s approval.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Track notified standards for your sector; they set the technical benchmark for compliance under s.6.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Whether standards under s.18 have been notified was not checked; the Central Rules contain sector-specific requirements.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.18. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Central Government declares occupational safety and health standards by notification (s.18(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Standards cover the matters in the Second Schedule (s.18(2)(f)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Whether standards under s.18 have been notified was not checked.</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Interpretation</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Not searched.</div></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch2\">The Second Schedule — Matters for occupational safety and health standards</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s16\">Section 16 — National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s19","title":"Section 19 — Research related activities","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 19 · Chapter IV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">19. It shall be the duty of such institutions in the field of occupational safety and health as the Central or State Government may notify to conduct research, experiments and demonstrations relating to occupational safety and health and thereafter submit their recommendations to the Central Government or the State Government, as the case may be:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the State Government shall consult National Board before notifying conduct of research, experiments and demonstration relating to occupational safety and health.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.19; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 24<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Notified institutions conduct research and demonstrations on occupational safety and health and report to the Government. States consult the National Board first.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>No direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.19. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Notified institutions conduct OSH research (s.19).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s16\">Section 16 — National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s20","title":"Section 20 — Safety and occupational health surveys","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 20 · Chapter IV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">20. (1) At any time during the normal working hours of an establishment or at any other time as he may deem necessary,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator in the case of factory or mine; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the Director General of Factory Advice Service and Labour Institute in the case of factory; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the Director General of Mines Safety in the case of mine; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) the Director General of Health Services in the case of factory or mine; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) such other officer as may be authorised by the appropriate Government in the case of any other establishment or class of establishments,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">after giving notice in writing to the employer, conduct survey of the factory or mine or such other establishment or class of establishments and such employer shall afford all facilities for such survey, including facilities for the examination and testing of plant and machinery and collection of samples and other data relevant to the survey.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this sub-section, the expression &quot;employer&quot; includes manager for the factory or in the case of any other establishment or class of establishments such person who is for the time being responsible for the safety and the occupational health of such other establishment or class of establishments, as the case may be.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) For the purpose of facilitating surveys under sub-section (1) every worker shall, if so required by the person conducting the survey, present himself to undergo such medical examination as may be considered necessary by such person and furnish all information in his possession which is relevant to the survey.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Any time spent by a worker for undergoing medical examination or furnishing information under sub-section (2) shall, for the purpose of calculating wages and extra wages for overtime work, be deemed to be working hour for him.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this section, the report submitted to the appropriate Government by the person conducting the survey under sub-section (1) shall be deemed to be a report submitted by an Inspector-cum-Facilitator under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.20; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 24<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitators, the DGFASLI, DGMS, DGHS or authorised officers may conduct <strong>safety and occupational health surveys</strong> on written notice. The employer must give all facilities. Workers must undergo medical examination if required, and the time counts as working time.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Cooperate with surveys; time spent by workers counts as working hours for wages and overtime.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.20. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Authorities may survey establishments after written notice; employers must afford facilities (s.20(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Time spent by workers on survey medical examinations counts as working time (s.20(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s34\">Section 34 — Appointment of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s21","title":"Section 21 — Collection of statistics and portal for inter-State migrant workers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 21 · Chapter IV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">21. (1) For the purposes of this Code, the Central Government and the State Government shall collect, compile and analyse occupational safety and health statistics in such form and manner as may be prescribed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The Central Government and the State Governments shall maintain the database or record, for inter-State migrant workers, electronically or otherwise in such portal and in such form and manner as may be prescribed by the Central Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that an inter-State migrant worker may register himself as an inter-State migrant worker on such portal on the basis of self-declaration and Aadhaar:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that the workers who have migrated from one State to any other State and are self-employed in that other State may also register themselves on that portal.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this sub-section, the expression &quot;Aadhaar&quot; shall have the same meaning as assigned to it in clause (a) of section 2 of the Aadhaar (Targeted Delivery of Financial and Other Subsidies, Benefits and Services) Act, 2016.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.21; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 24<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central and State Governments collect OSH statistics, and maintain a database or portal of <strong>inter-State migrant workers</strong>. Migrant workers may self-register with Aadhaar; self-employed migrants may also register.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 13): employers furnish statistics of inter-State migrant workers and their OSH electronically on the designated portal, as specified by order.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 13 — Central sphere: statistics of inter-State migrant workers.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A database or portal of inter-State migrant workers is maintained; workers may self-register with Aadhaar (s.21(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: employers furnish inter-State migrant worker statistics on the designated portal (rule 13).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s60\">Section 60 — Facilities to inter-State migrant workers</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s22","title":"Section 22 — Safety Committee and safety officers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.welfare-thresholds"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 22 · Chapter IV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">22. (1) The appropriate Government may, by general or special order, require any establishment or class of establishments to constitute in the prescribed manner a Safety Committee consisting of representatives of employers and workers engaged in such establishment in such manner that the number of representatives of workers on the Committee shall not be less than the number of representatives of the employer and the representatives of the workers shall be chosen in such manner and for such purpose as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) In every establishment which is a—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) factory wherein five hundred workers or more; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) factory carrying on hazardous process wherein two hundred fifty workers or more; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) building or other construction work wherein two hundred fifty workers or more; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) mine wherein one hundred workers or more, are ordinarily employed,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">the employer shall also appoint such number of safety officers, who shall possess such qualifications and perform such duties, as may be prescribed by appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.22; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 25<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Safety Committee.</strong> The appropriate Government may, by order, require establishments to constitute a Safety Committee with at least as many worker representatives as employer representatives.</p><p><strong>Safety officers</strong> must be appointed where ordinarily employed workers number:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>500 or more</strong> in a factory;</li><li><strong>250 or more</strong> in a factory carrying on a hazardous process;</li><li><strong>250 or more</strong> in building or other construction work;</li><li><strong>100 or more</strong> in a mine.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 14–21):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>Safety Committee</strong> in every establishment with <strong>500 or more workers</strong>; three-year tenure; meets at least quarterly (monthly in mines); employer acts on recommendations within <strong>15 days</strong>; equal sides, at most 20 members; women represented in proportion; worker members nominated by the negotiating union or council, or chosen by workers.</li><li><strong>Safety officers:</strong> dock work 500+ and building work 250+ on a sliding scale (building work: 1 up to 500 workers, 2 up to 1,000 and so on); mines 100+ (one per 500 workers); prescribed qualifications.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Factory safety officers are a State matter where the State is the appropriate Government (all factories, per s.2(1)(d)); State rules were not reviewed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 14–21 — Central sphere: Safety Committee composition and functions; safety officers for docks, building work and mines.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Welfare and safety staffing thresholds\"><figcaption><strong>Welfare and safety staffing thresholds</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Counts are workers &#39;ordinarily employed&#39; (s.2(1)(zv)) unless stated. Central Rules figures apply to the establishment types the rule names.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">More than 50 workers</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Crèche (rule 58); lunchroom and rest room in factories and mines (rule 56)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(2)(iii), (3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">100 or more workers (including contract labour)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Canteen</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(1)(v); rule 53</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">100 or more (mine)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Safety officer (one per 500)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(d); rule 20</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">250 or more</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Welfare officer (factories, mines, plantations); safety officer in hazardous-process factories and building work</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(b)–(c); s.24(2)(iv); rules 18, 57</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">500 or more</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Safety officer (factories, docks); Safety Committee (Central Rules)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(a); rules 14, 18</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">More than 500</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Ambulance room (factories, mines, building work)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(2)(i); rule 55</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.22, 24; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 14–20, 53–58</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Safety officers are required in factories with 500+ workers, hazardous-process factories and building work with 250+, and mines with 100+ (s.22(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: a Safety Committee in every establishment with 500 or more workers; recommendations acted on within 15 days (rule 14).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: building work safety officers on a scale starting at one up to 500 workers (rule 18(1)(b)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s14\">Section 14 — Rights of employee</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-v","title":"Chapter V — Health, Safety and Working Conditions (Sections 23–23)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s23","title":"Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 23 · Chapter V <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">23. (1) The employer shall be responsible to maintain in his establishment such health, safety and working conditions for the employees as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Without prejudice to the generality of the power conferred under sub-section (1), the Central Government may prescribe for providing all or any of the following matters in the establishment or class of establishments, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) cleanliness and hygiene;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) ventilation, temperature and humidity;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) environment free from dust, noxious gas, fumes and other impurities;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iv) adequate standard of humidification, artificially increasing the humidity of the air, ventilation and cooling of the air in work rooms;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) potable drinking water;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vi) adequate standards to prevent overcrowding and to provide sufficient space to employees or other persons, as the case may be, employed therein;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vii) adequate lighting;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(viii) sufficient arrangement for latrine and urinal accommodation to male, female and transgender employee separately and maintaining hygiene therein;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ix) effective arrangements for treatment of wastes and effluents; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(x) any other arrangement which the Central Government considers appropriate.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.23; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 25<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The employer must maintain the health, safety and working conditions <strong>prescribed by the Central Government</strong>, which may cover:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>cleanliness and hygiene;</li><li>ventilation, temperature and humidity;</li><li>freedom from dust, gas and fumes;</li><li>humidification and cooling;</li><li>drinking water;</li><li>overcrowding and space;</li><li>lighting;</li><li>separate latrines and urinals for male, female and transgender employees;</li><li>treatment of waste and effluents.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central Rules</strong> (rules 22–48), sector by sector (factories, mines, building work, beedi and cigar, motor transport, docks, plantations):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>drinking water marked and replenished daily;</li><li>separate latrines for men, women, transgender persons and persons with disabilities;</li><li>lighting, space and waste treatment standards.</li></ul><p>Apply the part of the Rules for your sector.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Sections 23(1) and 24(1) say &#39;as may be prescribed by the <strong>Central Government</strong>&#39;, not by the appropriate Government. On that wording, the Central Rules on health, working conditions and welfare (rules 22–63) may apply even where a State is the appropriate Government (for example, factories). This is an interpretation question; State rules were not reviewed.</li><li>The Ministry&#39;s FAQ (as on 13.03.2026) states that Central rules under ss.23 and 24 apply to all ports and docks across India. That is guidance, not law.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 22–48 — Health, safety and working conditions by type of establishment.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Health, safety and working conditions are as prescribed by the Central Government (s.23(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Central Rules prescribe cleanliness, ventilation, dust control, drinking water, lighting, latrines and waste treatment by sector (rules 22–48).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Whether the Central Rules under s.23 apply to establishments for which a State is the appropriate Government is an interpretation question.</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Interpretation</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Reasoned from the wording of s.23(1); not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-vi","title":"Chapter VI — Welfare Provisions (Sections 24–24)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s24","title":"Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.welfare-thresholds"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 24 · Chapter VI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">24. (1) The employer shall be responsible to provide and maintain in his establishment such welfare facilities for the employees as may be prescribed by the Central Government, including,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) adequate and suitable facilities for washing to male and female employees separately;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) bathing places and locker rooms for male, female and transgender employees separately;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) place of keeping clothing not worn during working hours and for the drying of wet clothing;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iv) sitting arrangements for all employees obliged to work in a standing position;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) facilities of canteen in an establishment for employees thereof, wherein one hundred or more workers including contract labourers are ordinarily employed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vi) in case of mines, medical examination of the employees employed or to be employed in the mines, before their employment and at specific intervals;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vii) adequate first-aid boxes or cupboards with contents readily accessible during all working hours; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(viii) any other welfare measures which the Central Government considers, under the set of circumstances, as required for decent standard of life of the employees.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Without prejudice to the generality of the powers referred to under sub-section (1), the Central Government may also prescribe for the following matters, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) ambulance room in every factory, mine, building or other construction work wherein more than five hundred workers are ordinarily employed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) medical facilities at the operating centres and halting stations, uniforms, raincoats and other like amenities for protection from rain or cold for motor transport workers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) adequate, suitable and separate shelters or rest-rooms for male, female and transgender employees and lunch-room in every factory and mine wherein more than fifty workers are ordinarily employed and in motor transport undertaking wherein employee is required to halt at night;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iv) the appointment of welfare officer in every factory, mine or plantation wherein two hundred and fifty or more workers are ordinarily employed and the qualification, conditions of service and duties of such welfare officer;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) for providing by the employer temporary living accommodation, free of charges and within the work site or as near to it as may be possible, to all building workers employed by him and for causing removal or demolition of such temporary living accommodation and for returning by the employer the possession of any land obtained by him for such purpose from Municipal Board or any other local authority;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vi) for payment by the principal employer the expenses incurred on providing the accommodation to the contractor, where the building or other construction work is done through the contractor;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vii) any other matter which may be prescribed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The Central Government may make rules to provide for the facility of creche having suitable room or rooms for the use of children under the age of six years of the employees at suitable location and distance either separately or along with common facilities in establishments wherein more than fifty workers are ordinarily employed:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that an establishment can avail common crèche facility of the Central Government, State Government, municipality or private entity or provided by non-Governmental organisation or by any other organisation or group of establishments may pool their resources for setting up of common crèche in the manner as they may agree for such purpose.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.24; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 25<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The employer must provide the <strong>welfare facilities prescribed by the Central Government</strong>, including:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>separate washing facilities;</li><li>bathing places and lockers for male, female and transgender employees;</li><li>clothing storage;</li><li>seating for standing work;</li><li>a <strong>canteen</strong> where <strong>100 or more</strong> workers (including contract labour) are ordinarily employed;</li><li>medical examinations in mines;</li><li>first-aid boxes.</li></ul><p>The Centre may also prescribe:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>an <strong>ambulance room</strong> (factories, mines and building work with <strong>more than 500</strong> workers);</li><li><strong>rest rooms and lunch rooms</strong> (factories and mines with <strong>more than 50</strong>);</li><li>a <strong>welfare officer</strong> (factories, mines and plantations with <strong>250 or more</strong>);</li><li>temporary accommodation for building workers.</li></ul><p><strong>Crèche.</strong> Rules may require a crèche where <strong>more than 50 workers</strong> are ordinarily employed; common crèches are allowed.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central Rules</strong> (rules 49–59):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>canteen at 100+ workers;</li><li>ambulance room above 500;</li><li>lunchroom above 50;</li><li>welfare officers from 250 (one more per additional 500; women welfare officers in proportion);</li><li>crèche above 50, with detailed safety standards (CCTV, police verification of staff, evacuation plan and others);</li><li><strong>quarterly mock drills</strong> (annual in plantations).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Sections 23(1) and 24(1) say &#39;as may be prescribed by the <strong>Central Government</strong>&#39;, not by the appropriate Government. On that wording, the Central Rules on health, working conditions and welfare (rules 22–63) may apply even where a State is the appropriate Government (for example, factories). This is an interpretation question; State rules were not reviewed.</li><li>The Ministry&#39;s FAQ (as on 13.03.2026) states that Central rules under ss.23 and 24 apply to all ports and docks across India. That is guidance, not law.</li><li>The crèche threshold here is &#39;more than fifty workers&#39;; under the Code on Social Security it is &#39;fifty employees&#39; (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s67\" data-ref=\"ssc.s67\">Section 67</a>). Apply the stricter where both apply.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 49–59 — Welfare facilities: washing, bathing, lockers, seating, canteen, first aid, ambulance room, shelters, welfare officer, crèche, mock drills.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Welfare and safety staffing thresholds\"><figcaption><strong>Welfare and safety staffing thresholds</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Counts are workers &#39;ordinarily employed&#39; (s.2(1)(zv)) unless stated. Central Rules figures apply to the establishment types the rule names.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">More than 50 workers</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Crèche (rule 58); lunchroom and rest room in factories and mines (rule 56)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(2)(iii), (3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">100 or more workers (including contract labour)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Canteen</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(1)(v); rule 53</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">100 or more (mine)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Safety officer (one per 500)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(d); rule 20</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">250 or more</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Welfare officer (factories, mines, plantations); safety officer in hazardous-process factories and building work</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(b)–(c); s.24(2)(iv); rules 18, 57</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">500 or more</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Safety officer (factories, docks); Safety Committee (Central Rules)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(a); rules 14, 18</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">More than 500</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Ambulance room (factories, mines, building work)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(2)(i); rule 55</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.22, 24; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 14–20, 53–58</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Canteen facilities where one hundred or more workers including contract labour are ordinarily employed (s.24(1)(v)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Welfare officers may be prescribed in factories, mines and plantations with 250 or more workers (s.24(2)(iv)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Crèche rules may apply where more than fifty workers are ordinarily employed; common crèches allowed (s.24(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central Rules: quarterly mock drills (annually in plantations) (rule 59).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central Rules: crèche where more than fifty workers are ordinarily employed, with listed safety standards (rule 58).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s67\">Section 67 — Creche facility</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-vii","title":"Chapter VII — Hours of Work and Annual Leave with Wages (Sections 25–32)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s25","title":"Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 25 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">25. (1) No worker shall be required or allowed to work, in any establishment or class of establishment for more than—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) eight hours in a day; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the period of work in each day under clause (a) shall be so fixed, as not to exceed such hours, with such intervals and spread overs , as may be notified by the appropriate Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that subject to clause (a) in the case of mines,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) the persons employed below ground in a mine shall not be allowed to work for more than such hours as may be notified by the Central Government in any day;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) no work shall be carried on below ground in any mine except by a system of shifts so arranged that the period of work for each shift is not spread over more than the daily maximum hours as notified under clause (i);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) no person employed in a mine shall be allowed to be present in any part of a mine below ground except during the periods of work shown in respect of him in the register maintained under clause (a) of section 33:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that subject to clause (a) that the hours of work in case of motor transport worker shall include—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) the time spent in work done during the running time of the transport vehicle;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) the time spent in subsidiary work; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) period of mere attendance at terminals of less than fifteen minutes.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this sub-section—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) &quot;running time&quot; in relation to a working day means the time from the moment a transport vehicle starts functioning at the beginning of the working day until the moment when the transport vehicle ceases to function at the end of the working day, excluding any time during which the running of the transport vehicle is interrupted for a period exceeding such duration as may be prescribed by the Central Government during which period the persons who drive, or perform any other work in connection with the transport vehicle are free to dispose of their time as they please or are engaged in subsidiary work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) &quot;subsidiary work&quot; means the work in connection with a transport vehicle, its passengers or its load which is done outside the running time of the transport vehicle, including in particular—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) the work in connection with accounts, paying of cash, signing of registers, handover of service sheets, the checking of tickets and other similar work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) taking over and garaging of the transport vehicles;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) travelling from the place where a person signs on to the place where he takes over the transport vehicle and from the place where he leaves the transport vehicle to the place where he signs off;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) work in connection with the upkeep and repair of the transport vehicle; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) the loading and unloading of the transport vehicle;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) &quot;period of mere attendance&quot; means the period during which a person remains at his post solely in order to reply to possible calls or to resume action at the time fixed in the duty schedule.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1), the hours of work for working journalist shall, subject to a maximum of one hundred and forty-four hours of work during any period of four consecutive weeks and a period of not less than twenty-four consecutive hours of rest during any period of seven consecutive days, be such as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-sections (1) and (2), a sales promotion employee or the working journalist,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) in addition to such holidays, casual leave or other kinds of leave as may be prescribed by the Central Government, shall be granted, if requested for—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) earned leave on full wages for not less than one-eleventh of the period spent on duty;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) leave on medical certificate on one-half of the wages for not less than one-eighteenth of the period of service;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) may accumulate earned leave up to such maximum limit as may be prescribed by the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) shall be entitled for the limit up to which the earned leave may be either encashed or availed of at a time by him and the reasons for which such limit may be exceeded shall be such as may be prescribed by the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) shall,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) when he voluntarily relinquishes his post or retires from service; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) when his services are terminated for any reason whatsoever (not being termination as punishment),</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">be entitled to cash compensation, subject to such conditions and restrictions as may be prescribed by the Central Government (including conditions by way of specifying the maximum period for which such cash compensation shall be payable), in respect of the earned leave earned by him and not availed of;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) who dies while in service, his heirs shall be entitled to cash compensation for the earned leave earned by him and not availed of his heirs shall be paid the cash compensation in respect of any period of earned leave for which he or his heirs, is or are entitled to cash compensation under clause (iv) or clause (v), which shall be an amount equal to the wages due to him for such period.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Notwithstanding anything contained in this section, the working hours of an adolescent worker shall be regulated in accordance with the provisions of the Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.25; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 27<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Daily hours.</strong> No worker may be required or allowed to work more than <strong>8 hours a day</strong>. Intervals and spread-over are as notified by the appropriate Government.</p><p><strong>Mines.</strong> Below-ground hours are notified by the Central Government; shift work only; presence below ground only during registered periods.</p><p><strong>Motor transport.</strong> Working hours include running time, subsidiary work, and attendance at terminals of less than 15 minutes.</p><p><strong>Working journalists.</strong> At most 144 hours in four weeks, with 24 consecutive hours&#39; rest in seven days.</p><p><strong>Sales promotion employees and working journalists</strong> get earned leave of at least 1/11 of duty, half-pay medical leave of 1/18 of service, accumulation and encashment as prescribed, and cash compensation for unused leave on leaving or death.</p><p><strong>Adolescents.</strong> Hours are governed by the Child and Adolescent Labour Act, 1986.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 64–66):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>48 hours a week</strong> maximum (rule 64(1));</li><li>motor transport: up to 15 minutes&#39; interruption counts in running time;</li><li>detailed hours and leave for working journalists and sales promotion employees (rules 65–66).</li></ul><p>The 8-hour daily limit is in the Code itself. Schedules with longer days (for example, four 12-hour days) depend on the notified spread-over and the weekly cap.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Whether a compressed work week (longer days, fewer days) is lawful depends on the 8-hour daily limit in s.25(1)(a), the notified intervals and spread-over, and the overtime provisions. This is an interpretation question; State notifications were not reviewed.</li><li>An official FAQ (undated) describes &#39;flexibility to extend daily hours with the consent of workers on payment of overtime&#39;. That is guidance; the 8-hour limit is in the Code.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 64–66 — Central sphere: weekly hours; motor transport interruptions; working journalists and sales promotion employees.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No worker may be required or allowed to work more than eight hours in a day (s.25(1)(a)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Working journalists: at most 144 hours in four consecutive weeks, with 24 consecutive hours of rest in seven days (s.25(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: no worker may work more than forty-eight hours in a week (rule 64(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s26\">Section 26 — Weekly and compensatory holidays</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\">Section 27 — Extra wages for overtime</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s28\">Section 28 — Night shifts</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s13\">Section 13 — Fixing hours of work for normal working day</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s26","title":"Section 26 — Weekly and compensatory holidays","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 26 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">26. (1) No worker shall be allowed to work in an establishment for more than six days in any one week:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that in any motor transport undertaking, an employer may, in order to prevent any dislocation of a motor transport service, require a worker to work on any day of weekly holiday which is not a holiday so arranged that the worker does not work for more than ten days consecutively without a holiday for a whole day intervening.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The appropriate Government may, by notification, exempt such workers as it thinks fit from the provisions of sub-section (1), subject to such conditions as may be prescribed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Where, as a result of the passing of an order or the making of a rule under the provisions of this Code exempting an establishment or the workers therein from the provisions of sub-section (1), a worker is deprived of any of the weekly holidays, the worker shall be allowed, within the month in which the holidays were due or within the two months immediately following that month, compensatory holidays of equal number to the holidays, so deprived.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.26; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 28<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No worker may work more than <strong>six days</strong> a week. In motor transport, work on the weekly holiday is allowed so long as no more than ten consecutive days pass without a whole-day holiday. Where an exemption deprives a worker of weekly holidays, compensatory holidays of equal number must be given within that month or the next two.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 67–68):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>display the weekly holiday notice (including electronically), showing different days for different relays;</li><li>no more than two compensatory holidays in a week;</li><li>display a compensatory holiday notice by the end of the month in which holidays were lost;</li><li>give compensatory holidays before discharge; they do not count towards notice.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 67–68 — Central sphere: weekly and compensatory holiday notices.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No worker may work more than six days in a week (s.26(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Compensatory holidays within the month or the next two months where weekly holidays are lost under an exemption (s.26(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s28\">Section 28 — Night shifts</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s27","title":"Section 27 — Extra wages for overtime","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 27 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">27. There shall be paid wages at the rate of twice the rate of wages in respect of overtime work, where a worker works in an establishment or class of establishment for more</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">than such hours of work in any day or in any week as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government and the period of overtime work shall be calculated on a daily basis or weekly basis, whichever is more favourable to such worker:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that a worker shall be required to work overtime by the employer subject to the consent of such worker for such work:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that the appropriate Government may prescribe the total number of hours of overtime.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.27; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 29<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Overtime beyond the prescribed daily or weekly hours is paid at <strong>twice the rate of wages</strong>, calculated daily or weekly, whichever is more favourable to the worker. <strong>Overtime requires the worker&#39;s consent.</strong> The appropriate Government may cap total overtime hours.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 69):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>overtime arises after 8 hours a day (daily wagers) or 48 hours a week (others);</li><li>paid at the end of each wage period;</li><li>part-hours of 15–30 minutes count as 30 minutes; over 30 minutes count as an hour;</li><li>for monthly-paid workers, the daily rate is 1/26 of monthly wages;</li><li><strong>144 hours a quarter</strong> cap (proviso to rule 69(4)).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The 144-hour cap is a proviso to rule 69(4), which deals with dock, mine and building work circumstances; whether it applies to all Central-sphere establishments is an interpretation question.</li><li>The Code on Wages also provides for overtime at twice the ordinary rate (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s14\" data-ref=\"cow.s14\">Section 14</a>).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 69 — Central sphere: overtime computation and limits.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Overtime is paid at twice the rate of wages (s.27).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Overtime requires the worker&#39;s consent (first proviso to s.27).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: 15–30 minutes count as half an hour; more than 30 minutes as an hour; monthly-paid daily rate is 1/26 (rule 69(2)–(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: overtime not exceeding 144 hours in a quarter (proviso to rule 69(4)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s14\">Section 14 — Wages for overtime work</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s28","title":"Section 28 — Night shifts","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 28 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">28. Where a worker in an establishment works on a shift which extends beyond midnight,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) for the purposes of section 26, a weekly holiday for a whole day shall mean in his case a period of twenty-four consecutive hours beginning when his shift ends;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the following day for him shall be deemed to be the period of twenty-four hours beginning when such shift ends, and the hours he has worked after midnight shall be counted in the previous day.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.28; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 29<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>For a shift extending beyond midnight, the weekly holiday means 24 consecutive hours from the end of the shift, and hours worked after midnight count towards the previous day.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Configure time-and-attendance systems to attribute post-midnight hours to the previous day.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.28. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Hours worked after midnight count towards the previous day (s.28(b)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s26\">Section 26 — Weekly and compensatory holidays</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s29","title":"Section 29 — Prohibition of overlapping shifts","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 29 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">29. (1) The work shall not be carried on in any establishment by means of a system of shifts so arranged that more than one relay of workers is engaged in work of the same kind</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">at the same time.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The appropriate Government or subject to the approval of the appropriate Government, the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, may, by written order and for the reasons specified therein, exempt on such conditions as may be deemed expedient, any establishment or class of establishments or any department or section of an establishment or any category or description of workers therein from the provisions of sub-section (1):</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the provisions of this sub-section shall not apply to mines.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.29; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 29<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Shifts may not be arranged so that more than one relay of workers does the same kind of work at the same time, unless exempted by written order. The exemption power does not extend to mines.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Obtain an exemption before running overlapping relays.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.29. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Overlapping relays on the same kind of work are prohibited unless exempted (s.29).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s30","title":"Section 30 — Restriction on double employment in factory and mine","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 30 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">30. No worker shall be required or allowed to work in a mine or factory if he has already been working in any other such similar establishment within the preceding twelve hours, save in such circumstances as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.30; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 29<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No worker may work in a factory or mine if he has worked in another similar establishment within the preceding 12 hours, except in prescribed circumstances.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: exemptions for mines are specified by order (rule 70).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 70 — Central sphere: exemptions from the double-employment restriction in mines.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No work in a factory or mine within twelve hours of work in a similar establishment, save as prescribed (s.30).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s31","title":"Section 31 — Notice of periods of work","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 31 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">31. (1) There shall be displayed and correctly maintained in every establishment a notice of periods of work, showing clearly for every day the periods during which workers may be required to work in accordance with the provisions of this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The form of notice required by sub-section (1), the manner of display of such notice and the manner in which such notice shall be sent to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall be such as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Any proposed change in the system of work in any establishment which will necessitate a change in the notice referred to in sub-section (1) shall be intimated to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator before the change is made, and except with the previous sanction of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator, no such change shall be made until one week has elapsed since that last change.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.31; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 29<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>A notice of periods of work must be displayed and kept correct. Changes to the system of work must be intimated to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator in advance, and no change may be made within one week of the last change without sanction.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 71): notice in <strong>Form XII</strong>, on a notice board or electronic board, with a copy sent to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator electronically or by speed post.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 71 — Central sphere: Form XII.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A notice of periods of work must be displayed; changes intimated in advance, no more than one change a week without sanction (s.31).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: notice of periods of work in Form XII (rule 71).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s33\">Section 33 — Maintenance of registers, records and filing of returns</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s32","title":"Section 32 — Annual leave with wages, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.annual-leave"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 32 · Chapter VII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">32. (1) Every worker employed in an establishment shall be entitled for leave in a calendar year with wages subject to the following conditions, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) that he has worked one hundred and eighty days or more in such calendar year;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) that he shall be entitled for one-day leave for every twenty days of his work, in the case of adolescent worker for fifteen days of his work, and in case of worker employed below ground mine, at the rate of one day for every fifteen days of his work, in such calendar year;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) any period of layoff, maternity leave or annual leave availed by such worker in such calendar year shall be counted for calculating the period of one hundred and eighty days or more under clause (i), but he shall not earn leave for the period so counted;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iv) any holidays falling between the leave availed by such worker (in a calendar year or prefixed or suffixed holiday) shall be excluded from the period of leave so availed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) in case of such worker whose service commences otherwise than on the first day of January shall be entitled to leave with wages at the rate specified in clause (ii), if he has worked for one-fourth of the total number of days in the remainder of the calendar year;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vi) in case such worker is discharged or dismissed from service or quits employment or is superannuated or dies while in service, during the course of the calendar year, such worker or his heir or nominee, shall be entitled to wages in lieu of the quantum of leave to which such worker was entitled immediately before his discharge, dismissal, quitting of employment, superannuation or death, calculated as specified in preceding clauses, even if such worker has not worked for the required period under this sub-section making such worker eligible to avail such leave, and such payment shall be made—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) where such worker is discharged or dismissed or quits employment before the expiry of the second working day from the date of such discharge, dismissal or quitting; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) where such worker is superannuated or dies while in service, before the expiry of two months from the date of such superannuation or death;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vii) if such worker does not in any one calendar year take the whole of the leave allowed to him under this sub-section and the rules made thereunder, then, any leave not taken by him shall be added to the leave to be allowed to him in the succeeding calendar year so that—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the total number of days of leave that may be carried forward to a succeeding year shall not exceed thirty days; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) such worker, who has applied for leave with wages but has not been given such leave in accordance with this sub-section and the rules made thereunder shall be entitled to carry forward the leave refused without any limit;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(viii) without prejudice to clause (vi) such worker shall be entitled on his demand for encashment of leave at the end of calendar year;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ix) such worker shall be entitled, where his total number of leave exceeds thirty days under sub-clause (a) of clause (vii), to encash such exceeded leave.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The appropriate Government may, by notification, extend the provisions of sub-section (1) to any other establishment except railway establishment.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The provisions of sub-section (1) shall not operate to the prejudice of any right to which a person employed in a mine may be entitled under any other law or under the terms of any award, agreement or contract of service:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that if such award, agreement or contract of service, provides for longer annual leave with wages than that provided in sub-section (1), the quantum of leave, which the person employed shall be entitled to, shall be in accordance with such award, agreement or contract of service but leave shall be regulated in accordance with the provisions of sub-section (1) with respect of matters not provided for in such award, agreement or contract of service:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that where the Central Government is satisfied that the leave rules applicable to persons employed in any mine provide benefits which in its opinion are not less favourable than those provided for in sub-section (1) it may, by order in writing and subject to such conditions as may be specified therein exempt the mine from all or any of the provisions of sub-section (1).</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.32; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 30<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Entitlement.</strong> A worker who works <strong>180 days or more</strong> in a calendar year earns leave at:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>1 day per 20 days</strong> worked;</li><li>1 per 15 for adolescents and below-ground mine workers.</li></ul><p>Lay-off, maternity leave and annual leave count towards the 180 days but do not earn leave. A worker joining mid-year qualifies by working a quarter of the remaining days.</p><p><strong>On exit.</strong> Leave wages for accrued leave are payable on discharge, dismissal or quitting within <strong>two working days</strong>, and on superannuation or death within <strong>two months</strong>, even if the 180 days were not reached.</p><p><strong>Carry forward and encashment.</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Up to <strong>30 days</strong> carry forward; leave refused can be carried forward without limit.</li><li>Encashment on demand at year-end; leave above 30 days can be encashed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 76): leave register in <strong>Form XX</strong>, preserved five years; share each employee&#39;s leave record once a year on demand.</p><p>Payroll should support annual encashment requests and the two-working-day exit settlement.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The text of clause (viii) (&#39;encashment of leave at the end of calendar year&#39;) does not state a cap; interpret together with clause (vii).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 76 — Central sphere: register of leave with wages.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Annual leave: worked example\"><figcaption><strong>Annual leave: worked example</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Illustrative application of s.32(1). Figures are hypothetical.</span></figcaption><dl class=\"hr-formula\"><dt>Days worked in the calendar year</dt><dd>240 (plus 20 days of lay-off) <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(i), (iii)</span></dd><dt>Qualifies?</dt><dd>Yes: 240 + 20 = 260 ≥ 180 (lay-off counts towards 180) <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(iii)</span></dd><dt>Leave earned</dt><dd>240 ÷ 20 = 12 days (lay-off days do not earn leave) <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(ii)–(iii)</span></dd><dt>Below-ground mine worker</dt><dd>240 ÷ 15 = 16 days <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(ii)</span></dd><dt>Unused at year-end</dt><dd>Carry forward up to 30 days in total; encash on demand; days above 30 may be encashed <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(vii)–(ix)</span></dd><dt>Resigns mid-year</dt><dd>Leave wages for accrued leave within two working days <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(vi)(a)</span></dd></dl><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.32</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">One day&#39;s leave for every twenty days of work, for workers with 180 days or more in a calendar year (s.32(1)(i)–(ii)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Leave wages on discharge, dismissal or quitting are paid before the expiry of the second working day (s.32(1)(vi)(a)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Carry forward is capped at thirty days, except leave refused (s.32(1)(vii)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Workers may demand encashment at the end of the calendar year (s.32(1)(viii)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: leave register in Form XX, preserved five years (rule 76).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s33\">Section 33 — Maintenance of registers, records and filing of returns</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s17\">Section 17 — Time limit for payment of wages</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-viii","title":"Chapter VIII — Maintenance of Registers, Records and Returns (Sections 33–33)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s33","title":"Section 33 — Maintenance of registers, records and filing of returns","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 33 · Chapter VIII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">33. An employer of an establishment shall—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) maintain register in prescribed form, electronically or otherwise, containing such particulars of workers as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government including,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) work performed by them;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) number of hours of work constituting normal working hours in a day;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) day of rest allowed in every period of seven days;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) wage paid and receipts given therefor;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(v) leave, leave wages, overtime work, attendance and dangerous occurrences; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(vi) employment of adolescent;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) display notices at the work place of the workers in such manner and form as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) issue wage slips to the workers, in electronic forms or otherwise; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) file such return electronically or otherwise to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator in such manner and during such periods as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.33; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 31<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Employers must:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>(a) maintain registers (electronically or otherwise) of work performed, hours, rest days, wages and receipts, leave, overtime, attendance, dangerous occurrences and adolescent employment;</li><li>(b) display notices;</li><li>(c) issue wage slips;</li><li>(d) file returns with the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 72–75):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>registers: <strong>Form XIII</strong> (employees), <strong>Form XIV</strong> (attendance-cum-muster roll), <strong>Form XV</strong> (wages, overtime, deductions); registers kept under the Code on Wages rules count;</li><li>wage slips electronically in <strong>Form XVI</strong>;</li><li>preserve <strong>five years</strong>; keep within 3 km;</li><li>accident and dangerous occurrence register in <strong>Form XIX</strong>;</li><li>notice board: establishment details, hours, wage period and date, accidents in the last five years, the Inspector-cum-Facilitator&#39;s details;</li><li><strong>annual return in Forms XVII and XVIII by 28/29 February</strong>; further return on sale or discontinuance; EPF and ESI-covered establishments self-declare in Part IV of Form XVII.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rule 74 also refers to an annual return in Form XVII by the last day of February; rule 72(5) refers to Forms XVII and XVIII. Both are cited as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 72–75 — Central sphere: registers, wage slips, display, annual return, accident register.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employers must maintain registers, display notices, issue wage slips and file returns (s.33).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: registers in Forms XIII–XV; wage slips in Form XVI; five-year preservation (rule 72(1)–(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: annual return in Forms XVII and XVIII by 28/29 February (rule 72(5)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s96\">Section 96 — Penalty for non-maintenance of register, records and non-filing of returns, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s31\">Section 31 — Notice of periods of work</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s50\">Section 50 — Records, returns and notices</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s123\">Section 123 — Maintenance of records, registers, returns, etc.</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-ix","title":"Chapter IX — Inspector-cum-Facilitators and Other Authority (Sections 34–42)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s34","title":"Section 34 — Appointment of Inspector-cum-Facilitators","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 34 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">34. (1) The appropriate Government may, by notification, appoint Inspector-cum-Facilitators for the purposes of this Code who shall exercise the powers conferred on them under this Code throughout their respective jurisdiction specified in the notification.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The Inspector-cum-Facilitators appointed under sub-section (1) shall, apart from other duties to be discharged by them under this Code, conduct such inspections as specified in sub-section (3).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The appropriate Government may—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) for the purposes of inspection referred to in sub-section (2), by notification, lay down an inspection scheme which may provide for the generation of web-based inspection and calling of information under this Code, electronically and such scheme shall, inter alia, have provisions to cater to special circumstances for assigning inspection and calling for information from establishment or any other person besides web-based inspections; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) without prejudice to the provisions of sub-section (2), by notification, under the scheme, provide for the randomised selection of establishment and the Inspector-cum-Facilitator for inspection.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Without prejudice to the powers of the appropriate Government under this section, the inspection scheme referred to in sub-section (3) may be designed taking into account, inter alia, the following factors, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) assignment of unique number, to each establishment (which will be same as the registration number allotted to the establishment registered under section 3), unique number to each Inspector-cum-Facilitator and to each inspection in such manner as may be notified by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) timely uploading of inspection reports in such manner and subject to such conditions as may be notified in the scheme;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) provisions for special inspections based on such parameters as may be notified by the appropriate Government; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) the characteristics of employment, the nature of work, and characteristics of the workplaces based on such parameters as may be notified by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) The appropriate Government may, by notification, appoint any person or persons possessing the prescribed qualifications and experience to be Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator for the purposes of such establishments or class of establishments and for such local limits of jurisdiction as may be specified in the notification:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that a Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator may be appointed for the purposes of a State or more than one States or for the purposes of the whole of the Country.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) The appropriate Government may, by notification, appoint for the purposes of establishments as may be notified by that Government, as many Additional Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitators, Joint Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitators and Deputy Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitators or any other officer of any designation as it thinks appropriate, to exercise such powers of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator within his jurisdiction, as may be specified in the notification.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(7) Every Additional Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, Joint Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, Deputy Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator and every other officer appointed under sub-section (6) shall, in addition to the powers of a Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator specified in the notification by which the officer is appointed, exercise the powers of an Inspector-cum-Facilitator within such local limits as may be specified in the notification.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(8) No person shall be appointed under this section or having been so appointed, shall continue to hold office, who is, or who becomes, directly or indirectly interested in a workplace or work activity or in any process or business carried on in any workplace or in any plant or machinery connected therewith.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(9) The appropriate Government may also, by notification, appoint such public officers as it thinks fit to be Inspector-cum-Facilitators in addition to existing Inspector-cum-Facilitator for exercising the powers and discharging the duties of Inspector-cum-Facilitator for all or any of the purposes of this Code within such local limits as may be specified in such notification.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(10) Without prejudice to the other functions of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator under this Code, an Inspector-cum-Facilitator may in respect of any establishment or class of establishments in local area or areas of his jurisdiction where the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator with the approval of the appropriate Government and subject to such restrictions or conditions as he may think fit to impose, by order in writing authorise the Inspector-cum-Facilitator to exercise such of the powers of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator as may be specified in such order:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, with the approval of the appropriate Government, may by order in writing, prohibit the exercise, by any Inspector-cum- Facilitator or any class of Inspector-cum-Facilitators specified in such order, of any such power by such Inspector-cum-Facilitator or class of Inspector-cum-Facilitators.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(11) Every Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, Additional Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, Joint Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, Deputy Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, Inspector-cum-Facilitator and every other officer appointed under this section shall be deemed to be a public servant within the meaning of section 21 of the Indian Penal Code, and shall be officially subordinate to such authority as the appropriate Government may specify in this behalf.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.34; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 31<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government appoints Inspector-cum-Facilitators and Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitators (a Chief may cover one or more States or the whole country). Inspections follow a notified <strong>web-based scheme</strong> with randomised selection, unique numbers for establishments, inspectors and inspections, uploaded reports, and special inspections on notified parameters. Officers must have no interest in workplaces they regulate.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Expect web-scheduled inspections; your registration number is your establishment&#39;s unique number.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The IPC reference (s.21) is not updated for the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (interpretation).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.34. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Inspections follow a notified web-based scheme with randomised selection (s.34(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s35\">Section 35 — Powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s40\">Section 40 — Facilities to be afforded to Inspector-cum-Facilitator</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s35","title":"Section 35 — Powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitators","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 35 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">35. (1) Subject to any rules made in this behalf, an Inspector-cum-Facilitator may—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) enter, with such assistance of persons, being persons in the service of the Government, or any local or other public authority, or with an expert, as he thinks fit, any place which is used, or which he has reason to believe, is used as a work place;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) inspect and examine the establishment, any premises, plant, machinery, article, or any other relevant material;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) inquire into any accident or dangerous occurrence, whether resulting in bodily injury, disability or death or not and take on the spot or otherwise statement of any person which he may consider necessary for such inquiry;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iv) subject to any rules made by the State Government in this behalf, within his jurisdiction, examine the crops grown in any plantation or any worker employed therein or require the production of any register or other document maintained in pursuance of this Code, and take on the spot or otherwise statement of any person which he may consider necessary for carrying out the purposes of this Code relating to plantation;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) supply information and sensitise the employers and workers regarding the provisions of this Code and compliance thereof;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vi) require the production of any register or any other document relating to the workplace or work activity;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vii) search or seize, or take copies of, any register, record or other document or any portion thereof, as he may consider necessary in respect of any offence under this Code, which he has reason to believe, has been committed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(viii) direct the concerned occupier or employer that any premises or any part thereof, or anything lying therein, shall be left undisturbed (whether generally or in particular respects) for so long as is necessary for the purpose of any inspection or inquiry;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ix) take measurements, photographs and videographs and make such recordings as he considers necessary for the purpose of any examination or inquiry;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(x) take samples of any articles or substances found in any establishment or premises into which he has power to enter and of the air of the atmosphere in or in the vicinity of any such establishment or premises in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(xi) in case of any article or substance found in any establishment or premises, being an article or substance which appears to him as having caused or is likely to cause danger to the health and safety of the employees, direct it to be dismantled or subject it to any process or test (but not so as to damage or destroy it unless the same is, in the circumstances necessary, for carrying out the purposes of any provision of this Code) and take possession of any such article or substance or a part thereof, and detain it for so long as is necessary for such examination;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(xii) issue show cause notice relating to safety, health and welfare provisions arising under this Code, rules, regulations and bye-laws made thereunder;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(xiii) prosecute, conduct or defend before any court any complaint or other proceeding arising under this Code, the rules and regulations made thereunder; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(xiv) exercise such other powers and perform such other duties as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Any person required to produce any document or to give any information required by an Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-section (1) shall be deemed to be legally bound to do so within the meaning of section 175 and section 176 of the Indian Penal Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The provisions of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, shall, so far as may be, apply to such search or seizure under sub-section (1) as they apply to any search or seizure made under the authority of a warrant issued under section 94 of the said Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.35; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 33<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Inspector-cum-Facilitators may:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>enter and inspect workplaces;</li><li>inquire into accidents and dangerous occurrences;</li><li>require registers and documents;</li><li>search, seize or copy records where an offence is suspected;</li><li>direct that premises be left undisturbed;</li><li>take measurements, photographs, videos and samples;</li><li>order articles to be dismantled or tested;</li><li>issue show cause notices;</li><li>prosecute.</li></ul><p>They also have an advisory role: to inform and sensitise employers and workers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: sampling procedure in rule 77; inspector duties in rule 78. Preserve accident scenes when directed.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The IPC and CrPC references (ss.175–176; s.94) are not updated for the BNS and BNSS, 2023 (interpretation).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 77–78 — Central sphere: samples; powers and duties of Inspector-cum-Facilitators.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Inspector-cum-Facilitators may enter, inspect, inquire, seize records, take samples and issue show cause notices (s.35(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s34\">Section 34 — Appointment of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s95\">Section 95 — Punishment for causing obstruction to Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s36","title":"Section 36 — Powers and duties of District Magistrate","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 36 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">36. The District Magistrate shall, within the local limits of his jurisdiction, exercise such powers and duties of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator in respect of mines as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.36; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 34<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>District Magistrates exercise prescribed powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitators in respect of mines.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mines only.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 79 — Powers and duties of the District Magistrate.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">District Magistrates exercise prescribed inspector powers for mines (s.36).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s35\">Section 35 — Powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s37","title":"Section 37 — Third party audit and certification","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 37 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">37. (1) The appropriate Government may, by notification, formulate a scheme to empanel experts possessing such qualifications and experience as may be prescribed for the purpose of such start-up establishments or class of establishments, as may be specified in the notification.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The experts empanelled under sub-section (1), shall,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) be assigned the third party audit and certification in a randomised manner, by the appropriate Government through a web-based scheme;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) carry out the audit and certification in the manner and for the purpose specified in the scheme referred to in sub-section (1);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) perform such duties as may be specified in such scheme and submit his report to the concerned employer and to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.37; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 34<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government may empanel experts for <strong>third-party audit and certification</strong> of specified start-up establishments, assigned randomly through a web-based scheme. Experts report to the employer and the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Start-ups should check whether a third-party certification scheme has been notified for them.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Whether a scheme has been notified was not checked (rule 80 says it &#39;will be notified&#39;).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 80 — Experts: scheme to be notified.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A scheme may provide for third-party audit and certification of start-up establishments (s.37).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s34\">Section 34 — Appointment of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s38","title":"Section 38 — Special powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitator in respect of factory, mines, dock work and building or other construction work","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 38 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">38. (1) Without prejudice to the other powers of an Inspector-cum-Facilitator in this Code, an Inspector-cum-Facilitator,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(A) shall have the following special powers in respect of a factory, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) where it appears to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator that conditions in a factory or part thereof are such that they may cause serious hazard or imminent danger by way of injury or death to the persons employed therein or to the general public in the vicinity, he may, by order in writing to the occupier of the factory, state the particulars in respect of which he considers the factory or part thereof to be the cause of such serious hazard or imminent danger and prohibit such occupier from employing any person in the factory or any part thereof other than the minimum number of persons necessary to attend to the minimum tasks till the hazard or danger is removed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) any order issued by the Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-clause (a) shall have effect for a period of three days until extended by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator by a subsequent order;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) any person aggrieved by an order of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-clause (a), and the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-clause (b), shall have the right to appeal to the High Court;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) any person whose employment has been affected by an order issued under sub-clause (a), shall, without prejudice to the rights of the parties under the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947, be entitled to wages and other benefits and it shall be the duty of the occupier to provide alternative employment to him wherever possible in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(B) shall have the following special powers in respect of mines, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) if, in respect of any matter for which no express provision is made by or under this Code, it appears to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or an Inspector-cum-Facilitator that any mine or part thereof or any matter, thing or practice in or connected with the mine, or with the control, supervision, management or direction thereof, is dangerous to human life or safety or is defective so as to threaten or tend to cause, the bodily injury of any person, he may give notice in writing thereof to the employer of the mine stating therein the particulars in respect of which he considers the mine or part thereof or the matter, thing or practice to be dangerous or defective and require the same to be remedied within such time and in such manner as he may specify in the notice;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) where the employer of a mine fails to comply with the terms of a notice given under sub-clause (a) within the period specified therein, the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator may, by order in writing, prohibit the employment in or about the mine or any part thereof of any person whose employment is not in his opinion reasonably necessary for securing compliance with the terms of the notice;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) without prejudice to the provisions contained in sub-clause (a), the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator may, by order in writing addressed to the employer of a mine, prohibit the extraction or reduction of pillars or blocks of minerals in the mine or part thereof, if, in his opinion, such operation is likely to cause the crushing of pillars or blocks of minerals or the premature collapse of any part of the workings or otherwise endanger the mine or the life or safety of persons employed therein or if, in his opinion, adequate provision against the outbreak of fire or flooding has not been made by providing for the sealing off and isolation of the part of the mine in which such operation is contemplated and for restricting the area that might be affected by fire or flooding;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) if the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or an Inspector-cum-Facilitator authorised, by general or special order in writing by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, is of opinion that there is urgent and immediate danger to the life or safety of any person employed in any mine or part thereof, he may, by order in writing containing a statement of the grounds of his opinion, prohibit until he is satisfied that the danger is removed, the employment in or about the mine or any part thereof of any person whose employment is not in his opinion reasonably necessary for the purpose of removing the danger;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) every person whose employment is prohibited under sub-clause (b) or sub-clause (d) shall be entitled to payment of full wages for the period for which he would have been, but for the prohibition, in employment and the employer shall be liable for payment of such full wages of that person:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that the employer may instead of paying such full wages provide such person with an alternative employment at the same wages which such person was receiving in the employment which was prohibited;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) where a notice has been given under sub-clause (a) or an order is made under sub-clause (b) or sub-clause (c) or sub-clause (d) by an Inspector-cum-Facilitator, the employer of the mine may, within ten days after the receipt of the notice or order, as the case may be, appeal against the same to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator who may confirm, modify or cancel the notice or order;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator sending a notice under sub-clause (a) or making an order under sub-clause (b) or sub-clause (c) or sub-clause (d) and the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator making an order (other than an order of cancellation in appeal) under sub-clause (f) shall forthwith report the same to the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) if the employer of the mine objects to a notice sent under sub-clause (a) by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator or to an order made by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-clause (b) or sub-clause (c) or sub-clause (d) or sub-clause (f), as the case may be, he may, within twenty days after the receipt of the notice containing the requisition or of the order or after the date of the decision on appeal, as the case may be, send his objection in writing stating the grounds thereof to the Central Government which shall, ordinarily within a period of one month from the date of receipt of the objection, decide the matter;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) every notice under sub-clause (a), or order under sub-clause (b) or sub-clause (c) or sub-clause (d) or sub-clause (f), to which objection is made under sub-clause (h), shall be complied with, pending the objection with the concerned Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator of the mine, for the decision of the Central Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that the Central Government may, on the application of the employer, suspend the operation of a notice under sub-clause (a), pending its decision on the objection;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) nothing in this section shall affect the powers of a magistrate under section 144 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) where in respect of any matter relating to safety of mine for which express provision is made by or under this Code, the employer of a mine fails to comply with such provisions, the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator may give notice in writing requiring the same to be complied with within such time as he may specify in the notice or within such extended period of time as he may, from time to time, specify thereafter;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) where the employer fails to comply with the terms of a notice given under sub-clause (k) within the period specified in such notice or within the extended period of time specified under that sub-clause, the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator may, by order in writing, prohibit the employment, in or about the mine or any part thereof, of any person whose employment is not, in his opinion, reasonably necessary for securing compliance with the terms of the notice;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) every person whose employment is prohibited under sub-clause (l), shall be entitled to payment of full wages for the period for which he would have been, but for the prohibition, in employment, and the owner, agent or manager referred to in section 67 shall be liable for payment of such full wages of that person:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that the employer may, instead of paying such full wages, provide such person with an alternative employment at the same wages which such person was receiving in the employment which was prohibited under sub-clause (l);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(n) the provisions of sub-clauses (g), (h) and (i) shall apply in relation to a notice issued under sub-clause (k) or an order made under sub-clause (l) as they apply in relation to a notice or an order under sub-clause (b);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(o) the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator may, for reasons to be recorded in writing, reverse or modify any order passed by him under this Code or under any regulation, rule or bye-law made thereunder in relation to mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(p) no order prejudicial to the owner, agent or manager of a mine shall be made under this section unless such owner, agent or manager has been given a reasonable opportunity of making representation;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(q) the Central Government may reverse or modify any order passed by Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator under this Code or under any regulation, rule or bye-laws thereunder in relation to mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l3\">(C) shall have the following special powers in respect of dock work namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) if it appears to an Inspector-cum-Facilitator that any place where any dock work is being carried on is in such a condition that it is dangerous to life, safety or health, of workers employed in dock work, he may, in writing, serve on the employer, an order prohibiting any dock work, in such place, until measures have been taken to remove the cause of the danger to his satisfaction;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) an Inspector-cum-Facilitator after serving an order under clause (a) shall endorse a copy thereof to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator who may modify or cancel the order without waiting for an appeal;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) any person aggrieved by an order under clause (a) or clause (b) may, within fifteen days from the date on which the order is communicated to him, prefer an appeal to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or where such order is by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, to the Central Government and the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Central Government shall, after giving the appellant an opportunity of being heard, dispose of the appeal within sixty days:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Central Government may entertain the appeal after the expiry of the said period of fifteen days, if he or it is satisfied that the appellant was prevented by sufficient cause from filing the appeal in time:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided further that an order under clause (a) or an order modified under clause (b) shall be complied with, pending the decision of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Without prejudice to the other powers of an Inspector-cum-Facilitator elsewhere in this Code,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) if it appears to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator that any site or place at which any building or other construction work is being carried on, is in such condition that it is dangerous to life, safety or health of building workers or the general public, he may, in writing serve, on the employer of building workers working at such site or place or on the employer of the establishment in which such site or place is situated or on the person in charge of such site or place, an order prohibiting any building or other construction work at such site or place until measures have been taken to remove the cause of the danger to his satisfaction;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) an Inspector-cum-Facilitator serving an order under clause (a) shall endorse a copy of the order to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) such prohibition order made by the Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall be complied with by the employer forthwith.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Any person aggrieved by an order under clause (a) of sub-section (2), may, within fifteen days from the date on which the order is communicated to him, prefer an appeal to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or where such order is by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, to the appropriate Government and the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the appropriate Government, as the case may be, shall, after giving the appellant an opportunity of being heard, dispose of the appeal within sixty days:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the appropriate Government may, entertain the appeal after the expiry of the said period of fifteen days if it is satisfied that the appellant was prevented by sufficient cause from filing the appeal in time:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that the order under clause (a) of sub-section (2), shall be complied with, subject to the decision of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the appropriate Government as the case may be.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.38; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 34<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Prohibition orders.</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>Factories:</strong> where conditions may cause serious hazard or imminent danger, the inspector may prohibit employment (other than minimum staff) by written order. The order lasts three days unless extended by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator; appeal lies to the <strong>High Court</strong>. Affected workers are entitled to wages and benefits, and to alternative employment where possible.</li><li><strong>Mines:</strong> notices to remedy dangers, prohibition orders and pillar-extraction bans. Workers whose employment is prohibited get full wages or alternative employment. Appeals and objections lie to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator and the Central Government; notices must be complied with pending objection.</li><li><strong>Docks:</strong> prohibition of dock work at dangerous places; appeal within 15 days.</li><li><strong>Building work:</strong> prohibition orders at dangerous sites, complied with forthwith; appeal within 15 days, decided within 60 days.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Prohibition orders must be complied with at once, even while appealing. Factory occupiers must keep paying affected workers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The reference to the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947 in clause (A)(d) is not updated for the IR Code (interpretation).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.38. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A factory prohibition order lasts three days unless extended by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator; appeal to the High Court (s.38(1)(A)(b)–(c)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Workers affected by a factory prohibition order are entitled to wages and other benefits (s.38(1)(A)(d)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Building work prohibition orders are complied with forthwith; appeal within fifteen days (s.38(2)–(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s104\">Section 104 — Special provision for contravention of order under section 38</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s35\">Section 35 — Powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s39","title":"Section 39 — Secrecy of information by Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 39 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">39. (1) All copies of, and extracts from, registers or other records pertaining to any establishment and all other information relating to any manufacturing or commercial business or any working process acquired by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or an Inspector-cum-Facilitator or by any one assisting him, in the course of the inspection or survey of any establishment under this Code or acquired by any officer authorised under section 20 in the exercise of his duties thereunder, shall be regarded as confidential and shall not, while in service or after leaving the service, be disclosed to any person or authority unless the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator considers disclosure necessary to ensure the health, safety or welfare of any person employed in establishment.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Nothing in sub-section (1) shall apply to the disclosure of any such information to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) any court;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) any Committee or Board constituted under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) an official superior or the employer of the establishment concerned;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) a Commissioner for employees&#39; compensation appointed under the Employees&#39; Compensation Act, 1923;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) the Controller, Indian Bureau of Mines; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) any such officer, authority or authorised person as may be specified in this behalf by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Notwithstanding anything contained in the Right to Information Act, 2005, no Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall disclose the source of any complaint, made to him regarding the contravention of the provisions of this Code without the consent of the complainant and shall also not while making an inspection under this Code in pursuance of such complaint, disclose to the employer concerned or any of his representative that the inspection is being made in pursuance of such complaint.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.39; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 38<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Information obtained in inspections and surveys is confidential, except where disclosure is needed for workers&#39; health, safety or welfare, or is made to courts, Boards, superiors, the employer, compensation commissioners and similar bodies. Inspectors may not disclose the source of a complaint, or that an inspection follows a complaint, notwithstanding the RTI Act.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Complainant identity is protected by statute.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The reference to the Employees&#39; Compensation Act, 1923 (repealed by the Code on Social Security) is reproduced as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.39. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Inspection information is confidential, and the source of a complaint may not be disclosed (s.39(1), (3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s124\">Section 124 — General restriction on disclosure of information</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s100\">Section 100 — Punishment for disclosure of information</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s40","title":"Section 40 — Facilities to be afforded to Inspector-cum-Facilitator","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 40 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">40. Every employer of an establishment shall afford the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator and every Inspector-cum-Facilitator having jurisdiction or every person authorised by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator all reasonable facilities for making any entry, inspection, survey, measurement, examination or inquiry under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.40; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 38<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Employers must give Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitators, Inspector-cum-Facilitators and authorised persons all reasonable facilities for entry, inspection, survey, measurement, examination or inquiry.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Obstruction is an offence (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s95\" data-ref=\"osh.s95\">Section 95</a>).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.40. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employers must afford inspectors all reasonable facilities (s.40).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s95\">Section 95 — Punishment for causing obstruction to Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s41","title":"Section 41 — Powers of special officer to enter, measure, etc., in relation to mine","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 41 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">41. Any person in the service of the Government duly authorised in this behalf by a special order in writing of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or of an Inspector-cum-Facilitator may, for the purpose of surveying, leveling or measuring any mine or any output therefrom, after giving not less than three days&#39; notice to the manager of such mine, enter the mine and may survey, level or measure the mine or any part thereof or any output therefrom at any time by day or night:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that, where in the opinion of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or of an Inspector-cum-Facilitator an emergency exists, he may, by order in writing, authorise any such person to enter the mine for any of the aforesaid purposes without giving any such notice.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.41; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 38<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Authorised Government servants may enter a mine to survey, level or measure it on at least three days&#39; notice to the manager, or without notice in an emergency.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mines only.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.41. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Three days&#39; notice before entry for surveying a mine, except in an emergency (s.41).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s38\">Section 38 — Special powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitator in respect of factory, mines, dock work and building or other construction work</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s42","title":"Section 42 — Medical officer","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 42 · Chapter IX <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">42. (1) The appropriate Government may appoint medical practitioners having prescribed qualification to be medical officers for the purposes of this Code in relation to factory, mines, plantation, motor transport undertakings and in any other establishment as may be prescribed:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the medical officers so appointed shall before entering into their office shall disclose to the appropriate Government their interest in the concerned establishment.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The medical officer shall perform the following duties, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the examination and certification of workers in a mine or factory or in such other establishment engaged in such dangerous occupations or processes as may be prescribed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the exercise of such medical supervision for any factory, mines, plantation, motor transport undertaking and for such other establishment as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government where cases of illness have occurred which it is reasonable to believe are due to the nature of any process carried on or other conditions of work prevailing in such establishments;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the examination and certification of adolescent for the purpose of ascertaining his fitness for employment in factory, plantation, motor transport undertakings and in any other establishment as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government in any work which is likely to cause injury to their health.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.42; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 39<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government may appoint medical officers for factories, mines, plantations, motor transport and prescribed establishments. They examine and certify workers in dangerous occupations, supervise where illness is linked to work processes, and certify adolescents&#39; fitness.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: medical officer qualifications and duties are in rules 81–82.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 81–82 — Central sphere: medical officers.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Medical officers may be appointed to examine, certify and supervise workers (s.42).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s20\">Section 20 — Safety and occupational health surveys</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-x","title":"Chapter X — Special Provision relating to Employment of Women (Sections 43–44)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s43","title":"Section 43 — Employment of women","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.women-night-work"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 43 · Chapter X <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">43. Women shall be entitled to be employed in all establishments for all types of work under this Code and they may also be employed, with their consent before 6 a.m. and beyond 7 p.m. subject to such conditions relating to safety, holidays and working hours or any other condition to be observed by the employer as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.43; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 39<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Women may be employed in <strong>all establishments for all types of work</strong>, and with their consent <strong>before 6 a.m. and after 7 p.m.</strong>, subject to prescribed conditions on safety, holidays and working hours.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 83) for night work (before 6 a.m. or after 7 p.m.):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>written consent;</li><li>maternity provisions respected;</li><li>pick-up and drop transport to her residence;</li><li>well-lit workplace and passages, nearby toilets and water, CCTV on the way to facilities;</li><li>emergency telephone numbers displayed, including in vehicles;</li><li>at least three women together below ground in mines;</li><li>compliance with the POSH Act.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>State conditions for women&#39;s night work in State-sphere establishments (including all factories) were not reviewed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 83 — Central sphere: conditions for employing women at night.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Women&#39;s night work: checklist (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Women&#39;s night work: checklist (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rule 83, for work before 6 a.m. or beyond 7 p.m. State rules may differ for State-sphere establishments.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Consent</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Written consent of the woman employee</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Maternity</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">No employment contrary to the maternity benefit provisions of the SS Code</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Transport</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Pick-up and drop at her residence</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(c)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Facilities</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Well-lit workplace and passages; toilets, washrooms and water nearby; CCTV on the way to facilities</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(d)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Emergency</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Dedicated phone numbers displayed at the workplace and in vehicles</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(f)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Mines (below ground)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">At least three women on duty at any place</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(g)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Harassment</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Comply with the POSH Act, 2013</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(h)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.43; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 83</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Women are entitled to be employed in all establishments for all types of work (s.43).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Women may work before 6 a.m. and beyond 7 p.m. with their consent, subject to prescribed conditions (s.43).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: written consent, transport, lighting, CCTV, emergency numbers and POSH compliance for night work (rule 83).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s44\">Section 44 — Adequate safety of employment of women in dangerous operation</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s59\">Section 59 — Employment of, or work by, women prohibited during certain period</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s44","title":"Section 44 — Adequate safety of employment of women in dangerous operation","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 44 · Chapter X <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">44. Where the appropriate Government considers that the employment of women is dangerous for their health and safety, in an establishment or class of establishments or in any particular hazardous or dangerous processes in such establishment or class of establishments, due to the operation carried out therein, such Government may in the prescribed manner, require the employer to provide adequate safeguards prior to the employment of women for such operation.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.44; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 39<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Where the appropriate Government considers women&#39;s employment in an operation dangerous, it may require the employer to provide adequate safeguards before employing women in it.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: the Central Government may notify classes of establishments and safeguarding standards for women in First Schedule hazardous processes (rule 84).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Whether any such notification has issued was not checked.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 84 — Central sphere: notified standards for women in hazardous processes.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Safeguards may be required before employing women in dangerous operations (s.44).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\">Section 43 — Employment of women</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch1\">The First Schedule — List of industries involving hazardous processes</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-xi","title":"Chapter XI — Special Provisions for Contract Labour and Inter-State Migrant Worker, etc. (Sections 45–93)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s45","title":"Section 45 — Applicability of this Part","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 45 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">45. (1) This Part shall apply to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) every establishment in which fifty or more contract labour are employed or were employed on any day of the preceding twelve months through contract;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) every manpower supply contractor who has employed, on any day of the preceding twelve months, fifty or more contract labour.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) This Part shall not apply to the establishment in which work only of an intermittent or casual nature is performed:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that if a question arises as to whether work performed in an establishment is of an intermittent or casual nature, the appropriate Government shall decide that question after consultation with the National Board or a State Advisory Board and its decision thereon shall be final.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purpose of this sub-section, work performed in an establishment shall not be deemed to be of an intermittent nature—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) if it was performed for more than one hundred and twenty days in the preceding twelve months; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) if it is of seasonal character and is performed for more than sixty days in a year.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.45; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 39<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Part I (contract labour) applies to:</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>every establishment employing <strong>50 or more contract labour</strong> on any day in the preceding 12 months;</li><li>every <strong>manpower supply contractor</strong> who employed 50 or more contract labour on any day in that period.</li></ul><p><strong>Exception.</strong> It does not apply where only intermittent or casual work is performed. Work is not intermittent if done for more than <strong>120 days</strong> in 12 months, or more than <strong>60 days</strong> a year if seasonal. Disputes are decided by the appropriate Government after consulting the National or State Board.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Count contract labour per establishment, across all contractors, on any day in the last 12 months. Below 50, the licensing Part does not apply, but s.53 welfare duties and other Chapters may.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The threshold was 20 under the Contract Labour (R&amp;A) Act, 1970 (predecessor not re-read here); see the comparison.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.45. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Part I applies to establishments with fifty or more contract labour, and to manpower supply contractors with fifty or more, on any day of the preceding twelve months (s.45(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Work is not intermittent if performed for more than 120 days in twelve months, or more than 60 days a year if seasonal (Explanation to s.45(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\">Section 53 — Liability of principal employer for welfare facilities</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\">Section 57 — Prohibition of employment of contract labour</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s46","title":"Section 46 — Appointment of designated authority","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 46 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">46. The appropriate Government may, by an order, appoint such persons, being Gazetted officers of the Government, as it thinks fit to be designated as authority under sub-section (1) of section 119 and specify the limits of their jurisdiction and vest with such powers and duties including dealing with issuance and revocation of licences electronically as may be specified therein.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.46; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 40<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government designates Gazetted officers as the licensing authority under s.119(1), with jurisdiction and powers including electronic issue and revocation of licences.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Identify the designated authority for your jurisdiction.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.46. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Gazetted officers are designated as authorities for licensing under s.119(1) (s.46).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s47","title":"Section 47 — Licensing of contractors","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 47 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">47. (1) No contractor to whom this Part applies shall—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) supply or engage contract labour in any establishment; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) undertake or execute the work through contract labour,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">except under and in accordance with a licence issued to him by the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 in accordance with the provisions of that section after satisfying that the contractor fulfills such requisite qualifications or criteria as may be prescribed by the Central Government and such licence shall, in addition to the requisite particulars and conditions specified in sub-section (3), specify the number of such contract labour who can be supplied or engaged and the amount of security to be deposited by the contractor.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where the contractor does not fulfil the requisite qualifications or criteria referred to in sub-section (1), the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 may issue him a &quot;work specific licence&quot; electronically renewable within such period as may be prescribed by the Central Government to supply or engage the contract labour, or execute the work through contract labour, only for the concerned work order as may be specified in such licence and subject to such conditions as may be specified in such licence.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Subject to the provisions of this Part,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) a licence under sub-section (1) may contain such conditions including, in particular, conditions as to hours of work, fixation of wages and other essential amenities in respect of contract labour as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the licence referred to in sub-section (1) or sub-section (2), shall be obtained from, if for such establishment the appropriate Government is—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) the Central Government, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 designated by that Government; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) the State Government, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 designated by that Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that where the contractor is desirous of obtaining licence for supplying or engaging contract labour or undertaking or executing the contract works under sub-section (1) or sub- section (2) in more than one States or for the whole of India, then, he may obtain the licence from the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 designated by the Central Government for such purpose and the provisions of that section shall apply:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that before issuing such licence the authority referred to in the first proviso shall consult the concerned State or States authorities designated under sub-section (1) of section 119, electronically before issuing licence for the establishments for which the appropriate Government is the State Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.47; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 40<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Licence required.</strong> A contractor to whom Part I applies may not supply or engage contract labour, or execute work through contract labour, without a licence. The licence states the number of contract labour and the security deposit.</p><p><strong>Work-specific licence.</strong> A contractor who does not meet the prescribed criteria may get a licence limited to a particular work order.</p><p><strong>Pan-India licence.</strong> A contractor working in more than one State may obtain a single licence from the Central authority, after electronic consultation with the States.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 85–90):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>eligibility:</strong> not an undischarged insolvent; no conviction in the last two years for an offence punishable with more than three months&#39; imprisonment;</li><li><strong>application:</strong> Form XXI on the Shram Suvidha Portal; licence in Form XXII;</li><li><strong>single (multi-State) licence:</strong> decided within 45 days, otherwise deemed and auto-generated; States deemed consulted after 30 days;</li><li><strong>security:</strong> bank guarantee of <strong>₹1,000 per contract labour</strong>, with fixed amounts of ₹10–20 crore at 1 lakh or more;</li><li><strong>fees:</strong> from ₹1,000 (50–100 workers) to ₹40,000 (above 20,000);</li><li><strong>conditions:</strong> wages not below Code on Wages rates; the headcount cap is not to be exceeded.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 85–90 — Central sphere: contractor eligibility, application, single licence, conditions, security and fees.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A contractor covered by Part I may not supply or engage contract labour except under a licence (s.47(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A single licence for more than one State or all of India may be obtained from the Central authority (first proviso to s.47(3)(b)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: bank guarantee of ₹1,000 per contract labour as security (rule 90(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: single licence decided within 45 days or deemed issued (rule 88(4)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s48\">Section 48 — Procedure for issue or renewal of licence</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s54\">Section 54 — Effect of employing contract labour from a non-licenced contractor</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s48","title":"Section 48 — Procedure for issue or renewal of licence","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 48 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">48. (1) Subject to the provisions of section 119, every application for issuing a licence under section 119 for the purposes of sub-section (1) or sub-section (2) of section 47 shall be made electronically in such form and manner and shall contain such particulars regarding the number of contract labour, nature of work for which contract labour is to be employed and such other particulars including the information relating to the employment of inter-State migrant workers as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Subject to the provisions of section 119, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) thereof shall follow such procedure as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Subject to the provisions of section 119, the licence issued for the purposes of sub-section (1) of section 47 shall be valid for a period of five years in respect of the number of contract labour specified therein and in case the contractor wants to increase the number of the contract labour, he shall apply in the prescribed manner for the amendment to the licence for such purpose to the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section119 and if the licence is so amended, the number of contract labour shall be increased to such extent by depositing such security deposit as specified in the amended licence for the balance period.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Subject to the provisions of section 119, the licence issued for the purposes of sub-section (1) of section 47 shall contain responsibility of the contractor as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.48; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 41<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Licence applications are made electronically with particulars including the number of contract labour, the nature of work and inter-State migrant workers. A licence is valid for <strong>five years</strong> for the specified number of workers. Increasing the number needs an amendment and additional security.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 91, 96): apply for renewal <strong>30 to 90 days before expiry</strong> (25% extra fee if late); renewal within 7 days or deemed; amendments in Form XXI, issued within 7 days or deemed, with proportionate security adjustments.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 91–92, 96 — Central sphere: renewal, refund of security, amendment.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A licence is valid for five years for the number of contract labour specified (s.48(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: renewal applied for at least 30 but not more than 90 days before expiry (rule 91(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s49","title":"Section 49 — No fees or commission or any cost to workers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 49 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">49. The contractor shall not charge directly or indirectly, in whole or in part, any fee or commission from the contract labour.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.49; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 41<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>A contractor may not charge contract labour any fee or commission, directly or indirectly.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Include a no-fee clause in contractor agreements and audit worker onboarding.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.49. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Contractors may not charge contract labour any fee or commission (s.49).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s50","title":"Section 50 — Information regarding work order to be given to the appropriate Government","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 50 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">50. (1) When a contractor receives work order from an establishment either to supply contract labour in the establishment or to execute the contract through contract labour in the establishment he shall, within such time and in such manner as may be prescribed, intimate to the authority referred to in section 119.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where the contractor fails to give intimation under sub-section (1), the designated authority may, after giving the holder of the licence an opportunity of showing cause, suspend or cancel the licence in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.50; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 41<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>A contractor receiving a work order must intimate the licensing authority as prescribed. Failure may lead to suspension or cancellation of the licence after a show cause notice.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 94): intimate within <strong>15 days</strong> of the work order on the Shram Suvidha Portal, giving the principal employer, site, start date, number of contract labour and duration.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 94 — Central sphere: intimation of work orders.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Contractors must intimate work orders to the licensing authority (s.50(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: work orders intimated within fifteen days on the Shram Suvidha Portal (rule 94).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s51\">Section 51 — Revocation, suspension and amendment of licence</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s51","title":"Section 51 — Revocation, suspension and amendment of licence","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 51 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">51. (1) If the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 is satisfied, either on a reference made to him in this behalf or otherwise, that—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) a licence granted for the purposes of this Part has been obtained by misrepresentation or suppression of any material fact, or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the holder of a licence has, failed to comply with the conditions subject to which the licence has been granted or has contravened any of the provisions of this Part or the rules made thereunder, then,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">without prejudice to any other penalty to which the contractor may be liable under this Code, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 may, after giving the contractor an opportunity of showing cause, revoke or suspend the licence in accordance with the procedure as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Subject to any rules that may be made in this behalf, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 may amend a licence granted for the purposes of this Part.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.51; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 41<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>A licence obtained by misrepresentation, or whose conditions or Part I have been breached, may be revoked or suspended after a show cause notice. Licences may also be amended.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 95): 15-day show cause notice electronically; speaking order; copies to the CLC(C) and the State.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 95 — Central sphere: revocation and suspension procedure.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Licences may be revoked or suspended for misrepresentation or breach, after a show cause notice (s.51(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s52\">Section 52 — Appeal</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s52","title":"Section 52 — Appeal","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 52 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">52. (1) Any person aggrieved by an order made under section 47, section 48 or section 51 may, within thirty days from the date on which the order is communicated to him, prefer an appeal to an appellate authority prescribed by the appropriate Government under sub-section (6) of section 119:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the appellate authority may entertain the appeal after the expiry of the said period of thirty days, if he is satisfied that the appellant was prevented by sufficient cause from filing the appeal in time.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) On receipt of an appeal under sub-section (1), the appellate authority shall, after giving the appellant an opportunity of being heard, dispose of the appeal within thirty days from the date on which the appeal is preferred.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.52; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 42<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Appeals against licensing orders under ss.47, 48 and 51 lie to the prescribed appellate authority within <strong>30 days</strong> (later for sufficient cause), and are decided within 30 days.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: the appellate authority, form and fee are specified by Central Government order (rule 97).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 97 — Central sphere: appellate authority.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Appeal within thirty days; decided within thirty days (s.52).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s51\">Section 51 — Revocation, suspension and amendment of licence</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s53","title":"Section 53 — Liability of principal employer for welfare facilities","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 53 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">53. Welfare facilities specified under section 23 and section 24 shall be provided by the principal employer of the establishment to the contract labour who are employed in such establishment.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.53; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 42<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The <strong>principal employer</strong> must provide the welfare facilities under ss.23 and 24 to contract labour employed in its establishment.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 86(1)(iii)): where contract labour work at the principal employer&#39;s premises, the principal employer provides toilets, washrooms, drinking water, bathing facilities if required, changing rooms, first aid, canteen and crèche.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Section 53 is in Part I, which applies only at 50 or more contract labour (s.45).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 86 — Central sphere: conditions of licence.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The principal employer provides ss.23–24 welfare facilities to contract labour (s.53).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: the principal employer provides amenities to contract labour working at its premises (rule 86(1)(iii)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s54","title":"Section 54 — Effect of employing contract labour from a non-licenced contractor","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 54 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">54. Where any principal employer of an establishment is employing contract labour through a contractor who is required to obtain a licence under this Part, but he has not obtained such licence, then, such employment shall be deemed to be in contravention of the provision of this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.54; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 42<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Engaging contract labour through a contractor who needs a licence but does not have one is a contravention of the Code by the principal employer.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Verify every contractor&#39;s licence (and its headcount cap) before engagement and at renewal.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.54. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employing contract labour through an unlicensed contractor who requires a licence is a contravention (s.54).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\">Section 94 — General penalty for offences</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s55","title":"Section 55 — Responsibility for payment of wages","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.contract-wage-default"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 55 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">55. (1) A contractor shall be responsible for payment of wages to each contract labour employed by him and such wages shall be paid before the expiry of such period as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Every contractor shall, make the disbursement of wages referred to in sub-section (1) through bank transfer or electronic mode and inform the principal employer electronically the amount so paid by such mode:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that where it is not practicable to disburse payment in the mode specified in this section, then, the payment shall be made in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) In case the contractor fails to make payment of wages referred to in sub-section (1) within the prescribed period or makes short payment, then, the principal employer shall be liable to make payment of the wages in full or the unpaid balance due, as the case may be, to the concerned contract labour employed by the contractor and recover the amount so paid from the contractor either by deduction from any amount payable to the contractor under any contract or as a debt payable by the contractor.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) The appropriate Government, in the event the contractor does not pay the wages to the contract labour employed by him, shall pass the orders of making payment of such wages from the amount deposited by such contractor as security deposit under the licence issued by the licensing officer to the contractor, in such manner as may be prescribed by such Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.55; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 42<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Payment.</strong> The contractor pays wages within the prescribed period, by <strong>bank transfer or electronically</strong>, and informs the principal employer electronically.</p><p><strong>Principal employer&#39;s liability.</strong> If the contractor fails to pay, or short-pays, the <strong>principal employer must pay</strong> in full or the balance, and may recover from the contractor.</p><p><strong>Security deposit.</strong> The appropriate Government may order unpaid wages to be paid from the contractor&#39;s security deposit.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 98–99):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>wage periods not longer than a month; wages paid <strong>by the 7th day</strong> after the period, without unauthorised deductions;</li><li>display a notice of the wage period and payment date;</li><li>half-yearly contractor return in Form XVIII within 30 days of each half-year;</li><li>if the contractor has not paid within 7 days, <strong>the principal employer pays within 15 days</strong> and recovers;</li><li>principal employer&#39;s annual return in <strong>Form XVII (Part III)</strong> by the end of February.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 98–99 — Central sphere: wage payment, returns, payment from security.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"When a contractor does not pay wages (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>When a contractor does not pay wages (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">s.55 and rule 98. Wage period of one month assumed.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-timeline\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">End of wage period (day 0)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Wage period closes (no wage period may exceed one month)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 98(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">By day 7</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Contractor pays by bank transfer or electronically, and informs the principal employer</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.55(1)–(2); rule 98(2)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Day 7 passes unpaid</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Principal employer must act</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 98(8)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Within 15 days</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Principal employer pays in full or the balance; recovers from the contractor (bills, debt or security)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.55(3); rule 98(8)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">If still unpaid</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">CLC(C) inquiry; payment ordered from the security deposit; contractor re-furnishes the security within 15 days</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.55(4); rule 99</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.55; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 98–99</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">If the contractor fails to pay or short-pays wages, the principal employer must pay and may recover from the contractor (s.55(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Contractors pay wages through bank transfer or electronic mode (s.55(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: contract labour wages paid before the seventh day after the wage period (rule 98(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: the principal employer pays unpaid contract wages within fifteen days (rule 98(8)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\">Section 53 — Liability of principal employer for welfare facilities</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s17\">Section 17 — Time limit for payment of wages</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s18\">Section 18 — Deductions which may be made from wages</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s56","title":"Section 56 — Experience certificate","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 56 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">56. Every concerned contractor shall issue, on demand, experience certificate, in such form as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government, to the contract labour giving details of the work performed by such contract labour.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.56; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 42<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>On demand, a contractor must issue contract labour an experience certificate describing the work performed.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 100): <strong>Form XXIII</strong>, covering the period, the work and the experience gained.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 100 — Central sphere: experience certificate.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Contractors issue experience certificates on demand (s.56).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: experience certificate in Form XXIII (rule 100).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s57","title":"Section 57 — Prohibition of employment of contract labour","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.core-activity"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 57 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">57. (1) Notwithstanding anything contained in this Part, employment of contract labour in core activities of any establishment is prohibited:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the principal employer may engage contract labour through a contractor to any core activity, if—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the normal functioning of the establishment is such that the activity is ordinarily done through contractor; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the activities are such that they do not require full time workers for the major portion of the working hours in a day or for longer periods, as the case may be;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) any sudden increase of volume of work in the core activity which needs to be accomplished in a specified time.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) (a) The appropriate Government may, by notification, appoint a designated authority to advise that Government on the question whether any activity of an establishment is a core activity or otherwise;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) if a question arises as to whether any activity of an establishment is a core activity or otherwise, the aggrieved party may make an application in such form and manner as may be prescribed, to the appropriate Government for decision;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the appropriate Government may refer any such question suo motu or refer the application to the designated authority, which on the basis of relevant material in its possession, or after making such an enquiry as it deems fit, shall report to the appropriate Government, within such period and thereafter the appropriate Government shall decide the question within such period as may be prescribed.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.57; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 42<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Core activities.</strong> Contract labour in the <strong>core activities</strong> of any establishment is prohibited, except where:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>the activity is ordinarily done through contractors in the normal functioning of the establishment;</li><li>the activity does not need full-time workers for most of the day or for longer periods; or</li><li>there is a sudden increase in the volume of core work that must be done in a specified time.</li></ul><p><strong>Disputes.</strong> Whether an activity is core is decided by the appropriate Government, which may refer the question to a designated authority.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>Map every contracted activity</strong> against the &#39;core activity&#39; definition and its listed exclusions (support services such as sanitation, security, canteen, loading and unloading, housekeeping and transport are not core unless the establishment exists for them) (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(p)</a>).</li><li><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 101): applications on whether an activity is core go to the Central Government; no adverse action while the matter is pending.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The previous regime prohibited contract labour by notification under s.10 of the CLRA Act (not re-read here). The comparison row explains the change.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 101 — Central sphere: core-activity questions.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Can this activity be given to contract labour?\"><figcaption><strong>Can this activity be given to contract labour?</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Section 57 read with s.2(1)(p). A decision aid, not a substitute for the statutory test.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">1. Is it a core activity?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">The activity for which the establishment is set up, or one essential or necessary to it (s.2(1)(p))</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.2(1)(p)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">2. Listed support service?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Sanitation, security, canteen, loading and unloading, hospitals or schools, courier, civil works, gardening, housekeeping, transport, intermittent activity: not core unless the establishment is set up for it</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">proviso to s.2(1)(p)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">3. If core: does an exception apply?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">(a) ordinarily done through contractors; (b) not needing full-time workers; (c) a sudden increase in volume to finish in a specified time</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.57(1) proviso</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">4. No exception</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Contract labour prohibited</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.57(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">5. Unsure</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Apply to the appropriate Government (Central sphere: rule 101; no adverse action while pending)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.57(2); rule 101</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(p), 57; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 101</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employment of contract labour in core activities is prohibited, subject to three exceptions (s.57(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Whether an activity is core is decided by the appropriate Government, with a designated authority advising (s.57(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: no adverse action while a core-activity question is pending (proviso to rule 101).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s54\">Section 54 — Effect of employing contract labour from a non-licenced contractor</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s58","title":"Section 58 — Power to exempt in special cases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 58 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">58. The appropriate Government may, in the case of an emergency, direct, by notification, that subject to such conditions and restrictions, if any, and for such period, as may be specified in the notification, all or any of the provisions of this Code or the rules made thereunder shall not apply to any establishment or class of establishments or any class of contractors.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.58; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 43<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>In an emergency, the appropriate Government may by notification exempt establishments or contractors from the Code or rules, on conditions and for a period.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check for emergency exemption notifications.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.58. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Emergency exemptions may be granted by notification (s.58).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s127\">Section 127 — Power to exempt in special cases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s128\">Section 128 — Power to exempt during public emergency</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s59","title":"Section 59 — Applicability of Part II","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 59 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">59. This Part shall apply to every establishment in which ten or more inter-State migrant workers are employed or were employed on any day of the preceding twelve months.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.59; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 43<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Part II (inter-State migrant workers) applies to every establishment employing <strong>10 or more inter-State migrant workers</strong> on any day in the preceding 12 months.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Track migrant-worker headcount (wages up to ₹18,000 a month, s.2(1)(zf)) by establishment.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.59. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Part II applies to establishments with ten or more inter-State migrant workers on any day of the preceding twelve months (s.59).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s60\">Section 60 — Facilities to inter-State migrant workers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\">Section 61 — Journey allowance</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s60","title":"Section 60 — Facilities to inter-State migrant workers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 60 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">60. It shall be the duty of every contractor or the employer, of an establishment employing inter-State migrant workers in connection with the work of that establishment—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) to ensure suitable conditions of work to such worker having regard to the fact that he is required to work in a State different from his own State;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) in case of fatal accident or serious bodily injury to any such worker, to report to the specified authorities of both the States and also the next of kin of the worker;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) to extend all benefits to such worker which are available to a worker of that establishment including benefits under the Employees&#39; State Insurance Act, 1948 or the Employees&#39; Provident Funds and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1952 or any other law for the time being in force and the facility of medical check-up as available to a worker under clause (c) of sub-section (1) of section 6.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.60; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 43<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The contractor or employer must:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>ensure suitable working conditions, given that the worker is away from his own State;</li><li>report fatal accidents and serious injuries to the authorities of <strong>both States</strong> and to the next of kin;</li><li>extend all benefits available to other workers, including ESI, EPF and the medical check-up under s.6(1)(c).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Add both-State reporting and next-of-kin intimation to the accident protocol for migrant workers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The references to the ESI Act, 1948 and the EPF Act, 1952 are reproduced as printed; the ESI Act is repealed by the Code on Social Security (the EPF Act&#39;s status is unresolved; see <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s164\" data-ref=\"ssc.s164\">Section 164</a>).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.60. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Fatal accidents and serious injuries to inter-State migrant workers are reported to authorities of both States and the next of kin (s.60(ii)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Migrant workers receive all benefits available to other workers of the establishment (s.60(iii)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s61","title":"Section 61 — Journey allowance","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 61 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">61. The employer shall pay, to every inter-State migrant worker employed in his establishment, in a year a lump sum amount of fare for to and fro journey to his native place from the place of his employment, in the manner taking into account the minimum service for entitlement, periodicity and class of travel and such other matters as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.61; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 43<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The employer pays every inter-State migrant worker, once a year, a lump sum for the <strong>to-and-fro fare</strong> to his native place, as prescribed.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 102): payable if the worker has worked <strong>180 days</strong> in the preceding 12 months (counting service with a previous employer, on the worker&#39;s certificate); fare by train (at least second-class sleeper), bus or other passenger transport; once in 12 months.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 102 — Central sphere: journey allowance.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employers pay migrant workers a yearly lump sum for the journey to and from their native place (s.61).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: journey allowance after 180 days in the preceding twelve months, once a year (rule 102).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s62","title":"Section 62 — Benefits of public distribution system, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 62 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">62. The appropriate Government shall make schemes to provide—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) option to an inter-State migrant worker for availing benefits of public distribution system either in his native State or the destination State where he is employed; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) for portability of the benefits of the inter-State migrant worker working for building or other construction work out of the building and other construction cess fund in the destination State where such inter-State migrant worker is employed.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.62; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 43<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Governments frame schemes for migrant workers to draw <strong>public distribution system</strong> benefits in either State, and for portability of building workers&#39; cess-fund benefits to the destination State.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>No direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.62. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Schemes provide PDS portability and BOCW benefit portability (s.62).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s108\">Section 108 — Building and Other Construction Workers’ Welfare Fund and its application</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s63","title":"Section 63 — Toll free helpline","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 63 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">63. The appropriate Government may provide facility of toll free helpline to the inter-State migrant workers in such manner as may be prescribed by that Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.63; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 44<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government may provide a toll-free helpline for migrant workers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: helpline number by order of the DG Labour Welfare (rule 103).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 103 — Helpline.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A toll-free helpline may be provided (s.63).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s64","title":"Section 64 — Study of inter-State migrant workers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 64 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">64. The appropriate Government may provide for study of inter-State migrant workers in such manner as may be prescribed by that Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.64; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 44<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government may study inter-State migrant workers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>No direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 104 — Studies.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Studies of migrant workers may be undertaken (s.64).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s65","title":"Section 65 — Past liabilities","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 65 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">65. No suit or other proceeding shall lie in any court or before any authority for the recovery of debt or any part thereof relating to an inter-State migrant worker after the completion of his employment where it remains unsettled obligation to the contractor or the principal employer and such debt or part thereof shall, on the completion of the period of employment of such worker, be deemed to have been extinguished.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.65; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 44<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Debts of a migrant worker to the contractor or principal employer that remain unsettled at the end of his employment are <strong>extinguished</strong>; no suit or proceeding lies to recover them.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Do not rely on recovering advances to migrant workers after their employment ends.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.65. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Unsettled debts of a migrant worker to the contractor or principal employer are extinguished on completion of employment (s.65).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s66","title":"Section 66 — Prohibition of employment of audio-visual worker without agreement","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 66 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">66. (1) No person shall be employed as an audio-visual worker in or in connection with production of any audio-visual programme unless,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) an agreement in writing is entered into—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) with such person by the producer of such audio-visual programme; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) with such person by the producer of such audio-visual programme with the contractor, where such person is employed through such contractor; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) with such person by the contractor or other person through whom such person is employed; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) such agreement is registered with the competent authority, to be notified by the appropriate Government, by the producer of such audio-visual programme.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Every agreement, referred to in sub-section (1) shall,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) be in the prescribed form;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) specify the name and such other particulars as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government with respect to, such person to be employed under the agreement as audio-visual worker;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) include, where such audio-visual worker is employed through a contractor, a specific condition to the effect that in the event of the contractor failing to discharge his obligations under the agreement to the audio-visual worker with respect to payment of wages or any other matter, the producer of the audio-visual programme shall also be liable to discharge such obligations and shall be entitled to be reimbursed with respect thereto by the contractor.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) A copy of the agreement referred to in sub-section (1) with respect to the employment of the audio-visual worker shall, if such audio-visual worker is covered under the provision of an enactment for the time being in force for providing the benefit of provident fund to him, also be forwarded by the producer of the audio-visual programme to such authority as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Notwithstanding anything contained in Chapters V, VI and VII, the agreement referred to in sub-section (1) shall include,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) nature of assignment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) wages and other benefits (including provident fund, if covered under the Employees’ Provident Fund and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1952);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) health and working conditions;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iv) safety;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) hours of work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vi) welfare facilities; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(vii) dispute resolution process or mechanism, the constitution and other details of which shall be prescribed by the appropriate Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that in case of failure of the resolution of the dispute in such dispute resolution process or mechanism, either party in the dispute may invoke the jurisdiction of the Industrial Tribunal established by the appropriate Government under section 7A of the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947 and for such purpose such dispute shall be deemed to be industrial dispute within the meaning of that Act and it shall be the responsibility of the producer of the audio-visual programme to provide the facilities specified in the agreement to the audio-visual worker and the payment of wages shall be through electronic mode.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.66; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 44<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No person may be employed as an <strong>audio-visual worker</strong> unless:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>there is a <strong>written agreement</strong> (with the producer, or through the contractor); and</li><li>the producer registers it with the competent authority.</li></ul><p><strong>Content of the agreement:</strong> the nature of the assignment, wages and benefits (including PF if covered), health and working conditions, safety, hours, welfare, and a dispute resolution mechanism. Where a contractor is involved, the producer is also liable if the contractor defaults.</p><p><strong>Disputes</strong> that the mechanism cannot resolve may go to the Industrial Tribunal. Wages are paid electronically.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 105–106): register the agreement in <strong>Form XXIV</strong>; disputes follow the IR Code procedure.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The references to the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947 (s.7A) and the EPF Act, 1952 are reproduced as printed; the ID Act is repealed by the IR Code (interpretation).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 105–106 — Central sphere: audio-visual worker agreements; disputes.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Audio-visual workers may be employed only under a written agreement registered by the producer (s.66(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Where a contractor is involved, the producer is also liable for the contractor&#39;s obligations (s.66(2)(c)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: agreements registered in Form XXIV (rule 105).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/irc.s44\">Section 44 — Industrial Tribunal</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s67","title":"Section 67 — Managers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 67 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">67. (1) Save as may be otherwise prescribed, every mine shall be under a sole manager who shall have such qualifications as may be prescribed by the Central Government and the owner or agent of every mine shall appoint a person having such qualifications to be the manager:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the owner or agent may appoint himself as manager if he possesses the prescribed qualifications.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Subject to any instructions given to him by or on behalf of the owner or agent of the mine, the manager shall be responsible for the overall management, control, supervision and direction of the mine and all such instructions when given by the owner or agent shall be confirmed in writing forthwith.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Except in case of an emergency, the owner or agent of a mine or anyone on his behalf shall not give, otherwise than through the manager, instructions affecting the fulfilment of his statutory duties, to a person, employed in a mine, who is responsible to the manager.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.67; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 45<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Every mine must have a <strong>sole manager</strong> with qualifications prescribed by the Central Government, appointed by the owner or agent (who may appoint himself if qualified). The manager is responsible for overall management, control, supervision and direction. Owner or agent instructions must be confirmed in writing, and, except in an emergency, go through the manager.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 107): manager qualifications and appointment, for example certificates of competency granted by the DGMS according to the type and size of mine.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rule 107 is detailed and technical; it is summarised only at headline level here.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 107 — Central sphere: qualification and appointment of mine managers.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Every mine must be under a sole qualified manager (s.67(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Except in an emergency, instructions affecting statutory duties go through the manager (s.67(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s7\">Section 7 — Duties and responsibilities of owner, agent and manager in relation to mine</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s105\">Section 105 — Failure to appoint manager in mine</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s68","title":"Section 68 — Code not to apply in certain cases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 68 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">68. (1) The provisions of this Code, except those contained in sections 35, 38, 40, 41 and 44, shall not apply to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) any mine or part thereof in which excavation is being made for prospecting purposes only and not for the purpose of obtaining minerals for use or sale subject to such conditions relating to number of employees, depth of excavation and other matters as may be prescribed by the Central Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) any mine engaged in the extraction of kankar, murrum, laterite, boulder, gravel, shingle, ordinary sand (excluding mouldings and glass sand and other mineral sands), ordinary clay (excluding kaolin, china clay, white clay or fire clay), building stone, slate, road metal, earth, fullers earth (marl, chalk) and lime stone subject to such conditions relating to workings, open cast workings and explosives as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1), the Central Government may declare that the provisions of this Code shall apply to such mine or part thereof as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Without prejudice to the provisions contained in sub-section (2), if at any time any of the conditions specified in clause (a) or clause (b) of sub-section (1) is not fulfilled in relation to any mine referred to in that sub-section, the provisions of this Code not set out in sub-section (1), shall become immediately applicable, and it shall be the duty of the employer of the mine to inform about such non-fulfilment to such authority in such manner and within such time as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.68; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 45<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Code (apart from ss.35, 38, 40, 41 and 44) does not apply to prospecting-only excavations or to mines of minor minerals listed in s.68(1)(b) (kankar, murrum, laterite, gravel, ordinary sand and clay, building stone and others), subject to prescribed conditions. If a condition stops being met, the full Code applies at once, and the employer must inform the authority.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: conditions in rule 108. Monitor depth, headcount and explosives use against those conditions.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 108 — Central sphere: conditions for exclusion of certain mines.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Prospecting excavations and listed minor-mineral mines are largely excluded, subject to prescribed conditions (s.68(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">If a condition is not fulfilled, the Code applies immediately and the employer must inform the authority (s.68(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s73\">Section 73 — Decision of question whether a mine is covered under this Code</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s69","title":"Section 69 — Exemption from provision regarding employment","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 69 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">69. (1) In case of an emergency involving serious risk to the safety of the mine or of persons employed therein, or in case of an accident, whether actual or apprehended, or in case of any act of God or in case of any urgent work to be done to machinery, plant or equipment of the mine as a result of breakdown of such machinery plant or equipment, the manager may, subject to the provision of clause (B) of sub-section (1) of section 38 and in accordance with the provisions of section 25 relating to exemption from hours of work above ground, hours of work below ground and notification regarding hours of work and weekly day of rest relating to mines under section 26, permit persons to be employed in contravention of sections 25 and 30 and sub-section (1) of section 31 on such work as may be necessary to protect the safety of the mine or of the persons employed therein:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that in case of any urgent work to be done to machinery, plant or equipment under this section, the manager may take the action permitted by this section, although the production of mineral would thereby be incidentally affected, but any action so taken shall not exceed the limits necessary for the purpose of avoiding serious interference with the ordinary working of the mine.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Every case in which action has been taken by the manager under sub-section (1), shall be recorded together with the circumstances relating thereto and a report thereof shall also be made to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.69; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 46<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>In emergencies, accidents, acts of God or urgent machinery breakdowns, the mine manager may permit work beyond the limits in ss.25, 30 and 31(1) as necessary for safety. Each case must be recorded and reported to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Keep an emergency-deviation log with reports.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.69. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Mine managers may permit emergency work beyond normal limits; each case recorded and reported (s.69).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s30\">Section 30 — Restriction on double employment in factory and mine</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s70","title":"Section 70 — Employment of persons below eighteen years of age","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 70 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">70. (1) No person below eighteen years of age shall be allowed to work in any mine or part thereof.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1), apprentices and other</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">trainees, not below sixteen years of age, may be allowed to work, under proper supervision, in a mine or part thereof by the manager as referred to in section 67:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that in the case of trainees, other than apprentices, prior approval of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or an Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall be obtained before they are allowed to work.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The Central Government may prescribe the provisions for medical examination of apprentice, other trainee and employee in the mine to ensure their fitness to work and to prevent the persons below sixteen years of age to work as apprentice or trainee and those who are not adults to work as such employee.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—In this section, &quot;apprentice&quot; means an apprentice as defined in clause (a) of section 2 of the Apprentices Act, 1961.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.70; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 46<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No person <strong>under 18</strong> may work in a mine. Apprentices and trainees aged <strong>16 or over</strong> may work under supervision (non-apprentice trainees need prior approval). Medical examination rules ensure fitness and exclude under-age persons.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 109–121): initial and periodic medical examinations, examining authorities, appeals to an appellate medical board, and costs borne by the employer.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 109–121 — Central sphere: medical examinations in mines.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No person below eighteen may work in a mine (s.70(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Apprentices and trainees aged sixteen or over may work under supervision (s.70(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s71","title":"Section 71 — Exemption to certain persons","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 71 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">71. The Central Government may make rules to provide for exemption to certain persons or category of persons employed in mines from the provisions of sub-section (1) of section 25, sub-section (1) of section 26, section 30 and sub-section (1) of section 31.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.71; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 46<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may make rules exempting persons or categories employed in mines from ss.25(1), 26(1), 30 and 31(1).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: exemptions from hours limits in rule 122.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 122 — Central sphere: exemptions from hours and limitation of employment.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Rules may exempt categories of mine employees from hours, weekly holiday and related provisions (s.71).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s72","title":"Section 72 — Establishment, maintenance of rescue services and vocational training","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 72 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">72. The Central Government may prescribe vocational training and rescue and recovery services for persons employed in a mine.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.72; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 46<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may prescribe vocational training and rescue and recovery services for mine workers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere: rescue stations, rescue rooms, staffing and equipment in rules 123 onwards.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 123 onwards — Central sphere: rescue stations and rooms.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Vocational training and rescue services may be prescribed for mines (s.72).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s67\">Section 67 — Managers</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s73","title":"Section 73 — Decision of question whether a mine is covered under this Code","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 73 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">73. If any question arises as to whether any excavation or working or premises in or adjacent to and belonging to a mine, on which any process ancillary to the getting, dressing or preparation for sale of minerals or of coke is being carried on in a mine within the meaning of this Code, the Central Government may decide the question, and a certificate signed by a Secretary to the Government of India in the Ministry of Labour and Employment shall be conclusive proof thereof.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.73; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 47<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government decides whether a working or premises ancillary to mining is part of a mine. A certificate signed by a Secretary in the Ministry of Labour and Employment is conclusive.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Seek a decision where coverage of ancillary premises is unclear.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.73. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Central Government decides whether premises are part of a mine; its certificate is conclusive (s.73).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s68\">Section 68 — Code not to apply in certain cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s74","title":"Section 74 — Licence to industrial premises and person","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 74 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">74. (1) Save as otherwise provided in this Part, no employer shall use or allow to use any place or premises as an industrial premises unless he holds a valid licence issued under section 119 for the purposes of this Part and no such premises shall be used except in accordance with the terms and conditions of such licence.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Subject to the provisions of section 119, any person who intends to use or allows to use any place or premises specified in sub-section (1) shall make an application to the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119, in such form and on payment of such fees as may be prescribed by the State Government, for a licence to use, or allow to use, such premises as an industrial premises.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Subject to the provisions of section 119, the application shall specify the maximum number of employees proposed to be employed at any time of the day in the place or premises and shall be accompanied by a plan of the place or premises prepared in such manner as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Subject to the provisions of section 119, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) thereof shall, in deciding whether to grant or refuse to grant a licence, have regard to the following matters, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the suitability of the place or premises which is proposed to be used for the manufacture of beedi or cigar or both;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) previous experience of the applicant or he has employed experienced person or has entered into agreement with the experienced person for employment for the period of licence;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the financial resources of the applicant including his financial capacity to meet the demands arising out of the provisions of the laws for the time being in force relating to welfare of labour;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) whether the application is made bona fide on behalf of the applicant himself or in benami of any other person;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) welfare of the labour in the locality, the interest of the public generally and such other matters as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Subject to the provisions of section 119, a licence granted under the said section for the purposes of this section shall be valid for five years and may be renewed thereafter.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) Subject to the provisions of section 119, an application for the renewal of a licence for the purposes of this Part shall be made at least thirty days before the expiry of the period thereof, on payment of such fees as may be prescribed by the State Government, and where such an application has been made, the licence shall be deemed to continue, notwithstanding the expiry of the period thereof, until the renewal of the licence, or, as the case may be, the rejection of the application for the renewal thereof:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 shall not grant or renew a licence unless it is satisfied that the provisions of this Part and the rules made thereunder have been complied with:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 shall renew or refuse to renew the licence within such period as may be prescribed by the State Government and in deciding whether to renew a licence or to refuse a renewal thereof shall have regard to the matters specified in sub-section (4).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(7) Subject to the provisions of section 119, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) thereof may, after giving the holder of a licence an opportunity of being heard, cancel or suspend any licence granted or renewed under section 119 for the purposes of this Part, if it appears to it that such licence has been obtained by misrepresentation or fraud or that the licence has contravened or failed to comply with any of the provisions of this Part or the rules made thereunder or any of the terms or conditions of the licence.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(8) The State Government may issue in writing to an authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 such directions of a general character as that Government may consider necessary in respect of any matter relating to the grant or renewal of licence under section 119 relating to this section.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(9) Subject to section 119 and the foregoing provisions of this section, the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 may grant or renew licence relating to this Part on such terms and conditions as it may determine and where such authority refuses to grant or renew any licence, it shall do so by an order communicated to the applicant, giving the reasons in writing for such refusal.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.74; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 47<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No premises may be used as <strong>industrial premises</strong> for beedi or cigar manufacture without a licence under s.119. The application states the maximum number of employees and includes a plan. In deciding, the authority considers suitability, experience, financial capacity, bona fides and local labour welfare. Licences last five years; renewal applications are due 30 days before expiry, and the licence continues until decided. Licences can be cancelled or suspended for misrepresentation or breach.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Beedi and cigar employers should diarise renewal at least 30 days before expiry.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rules under this provision are made by the State Government (or the appropriate Government, which for factories and plantations is the State). State rules are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter: the State Government prescribes the form, fees and plan. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Industrial premises for beedi or cigar manufacture require a licence (s.74(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Renewal applications are due at least thirty days before expiry; the licence continues until decided (s.74(6)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s75\">Section 75 — Appeals</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s75","title":"Section 75 — Appeals","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 75 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">75. Any person aggrieved by the decision of the authority referred to in sub-section (1) of section 119 refusing to grant or renew a licence, or cancelling or suspending a licence, relating to this Part may, within such time and on payment of such fees as may be prescribed, appeal to the appellate authority referred to in sub-section (6) of section 119, and such authority may by order confirm, modify or reverse any order refusing to grant or renew a licence, or cancelling or suspending a licence, relating to this Part.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.75; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 48<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Appeals against refusal, cancellation or suspension of a beedi or cigar licence lie to the appellate authority under s.119(6), within the prescribed time.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check the State&#39;s appeal time limit.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rules under this provision are made by the State Government (or the appropriate Government, which for factories and plantations is the State). State rules are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Licensing decisions are appealable (s.75).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s74\">Section 74 — Licence to industrial premises and person</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s76","title":"Section 76 — Permission to work by employees outside industrial premises","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 76 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">76. (1) The State Government may permit the wetting or cutting of beedi or tobacco leaves by employees outside the industrial premises on an application made to it by the employer on behalf of such employees, subject to such conditions as may be prescribed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The employer shall maintain the record of the work permitted under sub-section (1), to be carried on outside the industrial premises, in such form as may be prescribed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Save as otherwise provided in this section, no employer shall require or allow any manufacturing process connected with the making of beedi or cigar or both to be carried on outside the industrial premises:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that nothing in this sub-section shall apply to any worker who is given raw material by an employer or a contractor to make beedi or cigar or both at home.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.76; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 48<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The State Government may permit wetting or cutting of beedi or tobacco leaves outside industrial premises. Otherwise no beedi or cigar manufacturing process may be carried on outside the premises, except by home workers given raw material.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Keep records of work permitted outside the premises.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rules under this provision are made by the State Government (or the appropriate Government, which for factories and plantations is the State). State rules are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Beedi or cigar manufacturing processes may not be carried on outside licensed premises, except by home workers or with permission (s.76).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s74\">Section 74 — Licence to industrial premises and person</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s77","title":"Section 77 — Part not to apply to self-employed persons in private dwelling houses","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 77 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">77. Nothing contained in this Part shall apply to the owner or occupier of a private dwelling house, not being an employee of an employer to whom this Part applies, who carries on any manufacturing process in such private dwelling house with the assistance of the members of his family living with him in such dwelling house and dependent on him.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this section,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) &quot;family&quot; does not include child, as defined in the Child and Adolescent (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986, for this section;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) &quot;private dwelling house&quot; means a house in which persons engaged in the manufacture of beedi or cigar or both reside.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.77; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 48<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Part V does not apply to a self-employed owner or occupier of a private dwelling house manufacturing beedi or cigar with dependent family members (not children).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Applies to household production only.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.77. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Household beedi or cigar production by a self-employed person with dependent family is excluded (s.77).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s74\">Section 74 — Licence to industrial premises and person</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s78","title":"Section 78 — Prohibition of employment of certain persons in certain building or construction","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 78 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">78. No person, about whom the employer knows or has reasons to believe that he is a deaf or he has a defective vision or he has a tendency to giddiness, shall be required or allowed to work in any such operation of building or other construction work which is likely to involve a risk of any accident either to the building worker himself or to any other person.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.78; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 49<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No person whom the employer knows, or has reason to believe, to be deaf, to have defective vision or to have a tendency to giddiness may be required or allowed to work in a building or construction operation that risks an accident to him or others.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Include fitness screening for work at height and similar risk operations at construction sites.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.78. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Persons known to be deaf, with defective vision or prone to giddiness may not be employed in risky construction operations (s.78).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s9\">Section 9 — Duties of architect, project engineer and designer</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s79","title":"Section 79 — Approval and licensing of factories","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 79 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">79. (1) The appropriate Government may make rules in respect of factory or class or description of factories for—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the submission of plans including specifications, nature and certification thereof;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the previous permission for the site on which the factory is to be situated and for the construction or extension thereof; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) subject to the provision of sub-section 119, licensing and renewal thereof including fees to be payable for such, licensing and renewal, if required, as the case may be.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) If on an application for permission referred to in clause (b) of sub-section (1) accompanied by the plans and specifications required by the rules made under clause (a) of that sub-section, sent to the State Government or Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator in the electronic mode, no order is communicated to the applicant within such period not exceeding thirty days, the permission applied for in the said application shall be deemed to have been granted.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Where a State Government or a Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator refuses to grant permission to the site, construction or extension of a factory and licensing of a factory, the applicant may within thirty days of the date of such refusal appeal to the Central Government if the decision appealed from was of the State Government and to the State Government in any other case.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—A factory shall not be deemed to be extended within the meaning of this section by reason only of the replacement of any plant or machinery or within such limits as may be prescribed, of the addition of any plant or machinery if such replacement or addition does not reduce the minimum clear space required for safe working around the plant or machinery or adversely affect the environmental conditions from the evolution or emission of steam, heat or dust or fumes injurious to health.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.79; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 49<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government makes rules for factory <strong>plans</strong>, <strong>site and construction permission</strong> and <strong>licensing</strong> (subject to s.119). Permission applied for electronically is <strong>deemed granted</strong> if no order is communicated within the prescribed period, which may not exceed <strong>30 days</strong>. Refusals may be appealed within 30 days.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Use the 30-day deemed-permission provision; keep proof of the electronic submission date.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Site and construction permission is deemed granted if no order is communicated within the prescribed period, not exceeding thirty days (s.79(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\">Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s80","title":"Section 80 — Liability of owner of premises in certain circumstances","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 80 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">80. Where any premises or separate buildings are leased to different occupiers for use as separate factories, the owner of the premises and occupiers of the factories utilising such common facilities which include safety and fire prevention and protection, access, hygiene, occupational health, ventilation, temperature, emergency preparedness and response, canteens, shelter, rest rooms and crèches shall jointly and severally be responsible for provision and maintenance of such common facilities and services as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.80; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 49<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Where premises are leased to different occupiers as separate factories, the owner and occupiers are <strong>jointly and severally responsible</strong> for common facilities (safety and fire, access, hygiene, health, ventilation, emergency response, canteens, rest rooms, crèches).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Industrial park owners and tenants should allocate common-facility responsibilities by contract.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Owner and occupiers are jointly and severally responsible for common facilities in multi-tenant factory premises (s.80).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s81","title":"Section 81 — Power to apply Code to certain premises","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 81 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">81. (1) The appropriate Government may, by notification, declare that all or any of the provisions of this Part shall apply to any place wherein a manufacturing process is carried on with or without the aid of power or is ordinarily carried on irrespective of the number of workers working in the factory.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) After a place is so declared, it shall be deemed to be a factory for the purposes of this Code, and the owner shall be deemed to be the occupier, and any person working therein, a worker.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this section, &quot;owner&quot; shall include a lessee or mortgagee with possession of the premises.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.81; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 49<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government may declare that Part VII applies to any place with a manufacturing process, regardless of the number of workers. The place is then a factory, its owner the occupier, and those working there workers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Small units may be brought within factory regulation by notification.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Any manufacturing place may be declared a factory regardless of headcount (s.81).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s82","title":"Section 82 — Dangerous operations","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 82 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">82. The appropriate Government may by rules make the provisions relating to any factory or class or description of factories in which manufacturing process or operation is carried on which exposes any of the persons employed in it to a serious risk of bodily injury, poisoning or disease, for—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) specifying the manufacturing process or operation and declaring it to be dangerous;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) prohibiting or restricting the employment of pregnant women in the manufacturing process or operation;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the periodical medical examination before, or at any time during the employment to ascertain the fitness of a worker or employee for such employment on the cost of the occupier; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) welfare amenities, sanitary facilities, protective equipment and clothing, and any other requirement necessary for dangerous operations.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.82; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 50<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Rules may declare manufacturing processes <strong>dangerous</strong>, and may prohibit or restrict employment of pregnant women in them, require medical examinations at the occupier&#39;s cost, and require welfare, sanitary and protective measures.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check State dangerous-operations rules for your processes.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Rules may regulate dangerous operations, including restricting pregnant women&#39;s employment (s.82).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s44\">Section 44 — Adequate safety of employment of women in dangerous operation</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch1\">The First Schedule — List of industries involving hazardous processes</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s83","title":"Section 83 — Constitution of site appraisal committee","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 83 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">83. (1) The appropriate Government may, constitute one or more site appraisal committees consisting of a chairman and other members, for such purpose as may be prescribed including to consider and to give recommendations on an application for grant of permission for the initial location of a factory involving a hazardous process or for the expansion of such factory.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The site appraisal committee referred to in sub-section (1) shall make its recommendation within a period of thirty days of the receipt of the application for any of the purpose referred to in the said sub-section in such form, as may be prescribed.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.83; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 50<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Site appraisal committees may be constituted to recommend on the location or expansion of factories involving <strong>hazardous processes</strong>, within 30 days of an application.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Hazardous-process projects should plan for site appraisal.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Site appraisal committees recommend within thirty days on hazardous-process factory locations (s.83).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\">Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s17\">Section 17 — State Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s84","title":"Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 84 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">84. (1) The occupier of every factory involving a hazardous process shall disclose in the manner prescribed by the State Government all information regarding dangers, including health hazards and the measures to overcome such hazards arising from the exposure to or handling of the materials or substances in the manufacture, transportation, storage and other processes, to the workers employed in the factory, the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, the local authority within whose jurisdiction the factory is situate and the general public in the vicinity.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The occupier shall, at the time of registering the factory involving a hazardous process, lay down a detailed policy with respect to the health and safety of the workers employed therein and intimate such policy to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator and the local authority and, thereafter, at such intervals as may be prescribed by the State Government, inform the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator and the local authority of any change made in the said policy.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The information furnished under sub-section (1) shall include accurate information as to the quantity, specifications and other characteristics of wastes and the manner of their disposal.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Every occupier shall, with the approval of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, draw up an on-site emergency plan and detailed disaster control measures for his factory and make known to the workers employed therein and to the general public living in the vicinity of the factory the safety measures required to be taken in the event of an accident taking place.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Every occupier of a factory shall, if such factory proposes to engage in a hazardous process at any time after the commencement of this Code, within a period of thirty days before the commencement of such process, inform the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator about the nature and details of the process in such form and in such manner as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) Where any occupier of a factory contravenes the provisions of sub-section (5), the licence issued under section 79 to such factory shall, notwithstanding any penalty to which the occupier of factory shall be subjected to under the provisions of this Code, be liable for cancellation.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(7) The occupier of a factory involving a hazardous process shall, with the previous approval of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, lay down measures for the handling, usage, transportation and storage of hazardous substances inside the factory premises and the disposal of such substances outside the factory premises and publicise them in the manner prescribed by the State Government among the workers and the general public living in the vicinity.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.84; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 50<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The occupier of a factory involving a <strong>hazardous process</strong> must:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>disclose information on dangers, health hazards, control measures and wastes to workers, the inspectorate, the local authority and the public nearby;</li><li>lay down a <strong>health and safety policy</strong> at registration and report changes;</li><li>draw up an approved <strong>on-site emergency plan</strong> and disaster control measures, and publicise them;</li><li>inform the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator <strong>30 days before</strong> starting a new hazardous process (breach may lead to cancellation of the factory licence);</li><li>set approved measures for handling, storing, transporting and disposing of hazardous substances.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Hazardous-process factories: maintain the policy, emergency plan and disclosure pack; diarise the 30-day prior notice for new processes.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Occupiers of hazardous-process factories draw up an approved on-site emergency plan (s.84(4)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Notice to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator thirty days before starting a hazardous process (s.84(5)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s85\">Section 85 — Specific responsibility of the occupier in relation to hazardous processes</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s102\">Section 102 — Punishment for contravention of provisions of duties relating to hazardous processes</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch1\">The First Schedule — List of industries involving hazardous processes</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s85","title":"Section 85 — Specific responsibility of the occupier in relation to hazardous processes","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 85 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">85. Every occupier of a factory involving any hazardous process shall—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) maintain accurate and up-to-date health records or, as the case may be, medical records, of the workers in the factory who are exposed to any chemical, toxic or any other harmful substances which are manufactured, stored, handled or transported and such records shall be accessible to the workers subject to such conditions as may be prescribed by the State Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) appoint persons who possess prescribed qualifications and experience in handling hazardous substances and are competent to supervise such handling within the factory and to provide at the working place all the necessary facilities for protecting the workers in the manner prescribed by the State Government:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that where any question arises as to the qualifications and experience of a person so appointed, the decision of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall be final;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) provide for medical examination of every worker—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) before such worker is assigned to a job involving the handling of, or working with, a hazardous substance; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) while continuing in such job, and after he has ceased to work in such job, at intervals not exceeding twelve months, in such manner as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.85; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 51<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Occupiers of hazardous-process factories must:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>keep accurate health and medical records of exposed workers, accessible to them;</li><li>appoint qualified persons to supervise the handling of hazardous substances;</li><li>provide medical examinations before assignment, and at intervals of no more than <strong>12 months</strong> during the job and after leaving it.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Keep exposure-linked medical records and examination schedules.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Hazardous-process occupiers keep health records accessible to workers (s.85(a)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Medical examinations before assignment and at intervals not exceeding twelve months, including after the job ends (s.85(c)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\">Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s102\">Section 102 — Punishment for contravention of provisions of duties relating to hazardous processes</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s86","title":"Section 86 — National Board to inquire into certain situations","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 86 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">86. (1) The Central Government may, in the event of the occurrence of an extraordinary situation involving a factory engaged in a hazardous process, direct the National Board to inquire into the standards of health and safety observed in the factory with a view to finding out the causes of any failure or neglect in the adoption of any measures or standards prescribed by the State Government for the health and safety of the workers employed in the factory or the general public affected, or likely to be affected due to such failure or neglect and for the prevention of recurrence of such extraordinary situations in future in such factory or elsewhere.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The recommendations of the National Board shall be advisory in the nature.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.86; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 51<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>In an extraordinary situation at a hazardous-process factory, the Central Government may direct the National Board to inquire into health and safety standards. The recommendations are advisory.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>No direct duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.86. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The National Board may inquire into extraordinary situations; recommendations are advisory (s.86).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s16\">Section 16 — National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s87","title":"Section 87 — Emergency standards","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 87 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">87. (1) Where the Central Government is satisfied that no standards of safety have been prescribed in respect of a hazardous process or class of hazardous processes, or where the standards so prescribed are inadequate, it may direct the Directorate General Occupational Safety and Health formerly known as the Directorate General of Factory Advice Service and Labour Institutes or any Institution authorised in matters relating to standards of safety in hazardous processes, to lay down emergency standards for enforcement of suitable standards in respect of such hazardous processes.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The emergency standards laid down under sub-section (1) shall, until they are incorporated in the rules made under this Code, be enforceable and have the same effect as if they had been incorporated in the rules made under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.87; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 51<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Where safety standards for a hazardous process are missing or inadequate, the Central Government may direct the DGOSH (formerly DGFASLI) or an authorised institution to lay down <strong>emergency standards</strong>, enforceable as if in the rules.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Watch for emergency standards for your processes.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.87. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Emergency standards for hazardous processes are enforceable as rules (s.87(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\">Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s88","title":"Section 88 — Permissible limits of exposure of chemicals and toxic substances","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 88 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">88. The maximum permissible limits of exposure of chemical and toxic substances in manufacturing process in any factory shall be of the value as may be prescribed by the</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">State Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.88; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 52<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Maximum permissible exposure limits for chemicals and toxic substances in factories are prescribed by the State Government.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Apply the State&#39;s exposure limits.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Permissible exposure limits are prescribed by the State Government (s.88).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\">Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s89","title":"Section 89 — Right of workers to warn about imminent danger","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 89 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">89. (1) Where the workers employed in any factory engaged in a hazardous process have reasonable apprehension that there is a likelihood of imminent danger to their lives or health due to any accident, they may, bring the same to the notice of the occupier, agent, manager or any other person who is in-charge of the factory or the process concerned directly or through their representatives in the Safety Committee and simultaneously bring the same to the notice of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) It shall be the duty of such occupier, agent, manager or the person in-charge of the factory or process to take immediate remedial action if he is satisfied about the existence of such imminent danger and send a report forthwith of the action taken to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) If the occupier, agent, manager or the person in-charge referred to in sub-section (2) is not satisfied about the existence of any imminent danger as apprehended by the workers, he shall, nevertheless, refer the matter forthwith to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator whose decision on the question of the existence of such imminent danger shall be final.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.89; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 52<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Workers in a hazardous-process factory who reasonably apprehend imminent danger may warn the occupier or person in charge (directly or through the Safety Committee) and simultaneously the Inspector-cum-Facilitator. The occupier must act and report, or refer the question to the Inspector, whose decision is final.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mirror s.14 procedures in hazardous-process plants.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.89. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Workers may warn about imminent danger in hazardous-process factories; the occupier must act or refer (s.89).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s14\">Section 14 — Rights of employee</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s90","title":"Section 90 — Appeal against the order of Inspector-cum-Facilitator in case of factory","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 90 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">90. The appropriate Government may prescribe provisions providing the manner in which and the appropriate authority to whom the manager or occupier of the factory may</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">make appeal against the order of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator and the procedure for disposing of such appeals.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.90; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 52<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government may prescribe the procedure and authority for factory managers or occupiers to appeal against Inspector-cum-Facilitator orders.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check State rules for the appeal route.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Appeals against inspector orders in factories follow prescribed procedure (s.90).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s38\">Section 38 — Special powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitator in respect of factory, mines, dock work and building or other construction work</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s91","title":"Section 91 — Power to make rules to exempt","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 91 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">91. (1) The appropriate Government may make rules,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) specifying the persons who hold positions of supervision or management or are employed in a confidential position in a factory or empowering the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator to declare any person, other than a person so specified, as a person holding position of supervision or management or employed in a confidential position in a factory if, in the opinion of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, such person holds such position or is so employed, and the provisions of this Code, shall not apply to any person so defined or declared;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) in respect of any worker or class of workers in any establishment or class of establishment, for providing the exemption, extent of exemption and conditions subject to which such exemption may be given.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The appropriate Government or the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator may, by order in writing, exempt subject to such conditions as it may deem expedient, any or all of the adult workers in any establishment or class of establishments.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.91; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 52<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Rules may exempt persons in supervisory, managerial or confidential positions in a factory from the Code, and may exempt classes of workers. The Government or Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator may exempt adult workers by written order.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Exemptions depend on State rules or orders.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories the appropriate Government is always the State Government (Explanation to s.2(1)(d)), so the factory rules referred to here are State rules, which are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter for factories. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Rules may exempt persons in supervisory, managerial or confidential positions in factories (s.91(1)(a)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s92","title":"Section 92 — Facilities for workers in plantation","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 92 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">92. (1) Without prejudice to the generality of sections 23 and 24, the State Government may prescribe requiring every employer to make provisions in his plantation for—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) necessary housing accommodation including drinking water, kitchen and toilet to every worker employed in the plantation (including his family);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) crèches facilities where in the plantation fifty or more workers (including workers employed by any contractor) are employed or were employed on any day of the preceding twelve months:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">Provided that,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) an establishment may avail common crèche facility of the Central Government, State Government, municipality or private entity or provided by non-Governmental organisation or by any other organisation; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) a group of establishments may agree to pool their resources for setting up of common crèche;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) educational facilities for the children of the workers employed in the plantation where the children between the ages of six to twelve of the workers exceed twenty-five in number;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) health facilities to every worker employed in the plantation (including his family) or provide coverage under the Employees State Insurance Act, 1948; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) recreational facilities for the workers employed in the plantation.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) An employer of a plantation shall be responsible to provide and maintain welfare facilities for which the workers in the plantation are entitled under this Code either from his own resources or through the schemes of the Central Government or State Government, Municipality or Panchayat for the locality in which the plantation is situated.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this sub-section—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) the expression &quot;Municipality&quot; has the same meaning as assigned to it in clause (e) of article 243 of the Constitution; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) the expression &quot;Panchayat&quot; has the same meaning as assigned to it in clause (d) of article 243 of the Constitution.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.92; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 52<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The State Government may require plantation employers to provide:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>housing with drinking water, kitchen and toilet;</li><li>a <strong>crèche</strong> where <strong>50 or more</strong> workers (including contractors&#39; workers) are or were employed on any day in the preceding 12 months (common crèches allowed);</li><li>educational facilities where more than 25 workers&#39; children are aged 6–12;</li><li>health facilities (or ESI coverage);</li><li>recreational facilities.</li></ul><p>The employer provides these facilities from his own resources or through Government or local body schemes.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check the State&#39;s plantation rules for the detail.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rules under this provision are made by the State Government (or the appropriate Government, which for factories and plantations is the State). State rules are outside scope.</li><li>The reference to the ESI Act, 1948 is reproduced as printed; it is repealed by the Code on Social Security.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Plantations: crèche where fifty or more workers including contractors&#39; workers are employed (s.92(1)(b)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Educational facilities where children aged six to twelve exceed twenty-five (s.92(1)(c)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s67\">Section 67 — Creche facility</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s93","title":"Section 93 — Safety","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 93 · Chapter XI <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">93. (1) In every plantation, arrangement shall be made by the employer to provide for the safety of a worker in connection with the use, handling, storage and transport of insecticides, pesticides and chemicals and toxic substances.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The State Government may prescribe for special safeguards for employment of women or adolescents in using or handling hazardous chemicals.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The employer of a plantation shall appoint persons possessing the prescribed qualifications to supervise the use, handling, storage and transportation of insecticides, chemicals and toxic substances in his plantation.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Every employer of a plantation shall ensure that every worker in plantation employed for handling, mixing, blending and applying insecticides, chemicals and toxic substances, is trained about the hazards involved in different operations in which he is engaged, the various safety measures and safe work practices to be adopted in emergencies arising from spillage of such insecticides, chemicals and toxic substances and such other matters as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Every worker in a plantation who is exposed to insecticides, pesticides, chemicals and toxic substances shall be medically examined periodically, in such manner as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) Every employer of a plantation shall maintain health record of every worker in plantation who is exposed to insecticides, pesticides, chemicals and toxic substances which are used, handled, stored or transported in a plantation, and every such worker shall have access to such record.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(7) Every employer of a plantation shall provide—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) washing, bathing and clock room facilities; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) protective clothing and equipment,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">to every worker engaged in the handling insecticides, pesticides, chemicals and toxic substances in such manner as may be prescribed by the State Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(8) Every employer of a plantation shall display in the plantation, a list of permissible concentrations of insecticides, pesticides, chemicals and toxic substances in the breathing zone of the workers engaged in the handling and application of insecticides, pesticides, chemicals and toxic substances in the plantation.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(9) Every employer of a plantation shall exhibit such precautionary notices in the plantation as may be prescribed by the State Government indicating the hazards of insecticides, pesticides, chemicals and toxic substances.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.93; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 53<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Plantation employers must:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>arrange for safety in the use, handling, storage and transport of pesticides and chemicals;</li><li>appoint qualified supervisors;</li><li>train exposed workers;</li><li>arrange periodic medical examinations and keep health records accessible to workers;</li><li>provide washing and protective equipment;</li><li>display permissible concentrations and precautionary notices.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Build a pesticide safety programme with training and health records.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Rules under this provision are made by the State Government (or the appropriate Government, which for factories and plantations is the State). State rules are outside scope.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>State matter. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Plantation employers must ensure safety, training, medical examination and records for workers handling pesticides and chemicals (s.93).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s92\">Section 92 — Facilities for workers in plantation</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-xii","title":"Chapter XII — Offences and Penalties (Sections 94–114)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s94","title":"Section 94 — General penalty for offences","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 94 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">94. Save as otherwise expressly provided in this Code, if in, or in respect of, any establishment, there is any contravention of the provisions of this Code or regulations or rules, or bye-laws or any of standards, made thereunder or of any order in writing given under this Code or such regulations or rules or bye-laws or standards, the employer or the principal employer of the establishment, as the case may be, shall be liable to penalty which shall not be less than two lakhs rupees but which may extend up to three lakh rupees, and if the contravention is continued after the conviction, then, with further penalty which may extend to two thousand rupees for each day till such contravention continues.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.94; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 54<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Unless the Code provides otherwise, any contravention of the Code, rules, regulations, bye-laws, standards or written orders in an establishment makes the <strong>employer or principal employer</strong> liable to a penalty of <strong>₹2 lakh to ₹3 lakh</strong>, plus up to <strong>₹2,000 a day</strong> if the contravention continues after conviction.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>This is the default penalty for any breach with no specific penalty, imposed after an enquiry under s.111. It is compoundable at 50% of the maximum (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s114\" data-ref=\"osh.s114\">Section 114</a>).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The section uses &#39;penalty&#39; but also refers to &#39;conviction&#39; for continuing contraventions; reproduced as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 177 — Central sphere: enquiry by the officer under s.111; appeal within sixty days.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">General penalty of ₹2 lakh to ₹3 lakh on the employer or principal employer, plus up to ₹2,000 a day for continuing contraventions (s.94).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\">Section 111 — Power of officers of appropriate Government to impose penalty in certain cases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s114\">Section 114 — Composition of certain offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\">Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s95","title":"Section 95 — Punishment for causing obstruction to Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 95 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">95. (1)Whoever wilfully—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) prevents or causes obstruction to a Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator or an officer of the appropriate Government or a person authorised to discharge any duty or to exercise any powers under this Code or the rules or the regulations or the bye-laws made thereunder, from discharging such duty or exercising such power; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) refuses entry to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator or person or public authority referred to in clause (i) of sub-section (1) of section 35 or expert referred to in section 37, to any place where such Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator or such person or authority or expert is entitled to enter; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iii) fails or refuses to produce any document which he is required to produce; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(iv) fails to comply with any requisition or order issued to him,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">under this Code or the rules, regulations or bye-laws made thereunder he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to three months, or with fine which may extend to one lakh rupees, or with both.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where any person convicted of an offence punishable under sub-section (1) is again convicted of an offence under the same provision, then, he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to six months, or with fine which shall not be less than one lakh rupees but which may extend to two lakh rupees, or with both.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.95; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 54<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Wilfully obstructing inspectors or authorised persons, refusing entry, failing to produce documents, or failing to comply with a requisition or order: imprisonment up to <strong>three months</strong>, or a fine up to <strong>₹1 lakh</strong>, or both. On a repeat conviction: up to six months, or a fine of ₹1–2 lakh, or both.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Brief reception and security staff on inspectors&#39; right of entry.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.95. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Obstruction: up to three months or fine up to ₹1 lakh, or both (s.95(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s35\">Section 35 — Powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s40\">Section 40 — Facilities to be afforded to Inspector-cum-Facilitator</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s96","title":"Section 96 — Penalty for non-maintenance of register, records and non-filing of returns, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 96 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">96. (1) Any person, who is required under this Code or the rules or regulations or bye-laws or order made thereunder, to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) maintain any register or other document or to file returns, omits or fails to maintain such register or document or to file such returns; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) produce any register or plan or record or report or any other document, omits or fails to produce such register or plan or record or report or such other document,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">he shall be liable to penalty which shall not be less than fifty thousand rupees but which may extend to one lakh rupees.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where any person convicted of an offence punishable under sub-section (1) is again convicted of an offence under the same provision, then, he shall be liable to penalty which shall not be less than fifty thousand rupees but which may extend to two lakh rupees.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.96; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 54<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Failing to maintain registers or documents, to file returns, or to produce registers, plans, records or reports: penalty of <strong>₹50,000 to ₹1 lakh</strong>. Repeat: ₹50,000 to ₹2 lakh.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Record-keeping failures carry a minimum ₹50,000 penalty; keep the rule 72 registers current.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 177 — Central sphere: enquiry by the officer under s.111; appeal within sixty days.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Penalty of ₹50,000 to ₹1 lakh for failure to maintain registers, file returns or produce records (s.96(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s33\">Section 33 — Maintenance of registers, records and filing of returns</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\">Section 111 — Power of officers of appropriate Government to impose penalty in certain cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s97","title":"Section 97 — Punishment for contravention of certain provisions","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 97 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">97. (1) Any person, who, save as permitted by or under this Code, contravenes, any—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) provision of this Code or of any rule, regulation or bye-laws; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) order made under this Code prohibiting, restricting or regulating the employment of workers including women, audio-visual worker and contract labour and employee below eighteen years of age in case of mines,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">he shall be liable to penalty which shall not be less than fifty thousand rupees but which may extend to one lakh rupees.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where any person convicted of an offence punishable under sub-section (1) is again convicted of an offence under the same provision, then, he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to three months, or with fine which may extend to two lakh rupees, or with both.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.97; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 55<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Contravening the Code, rules, regulations or bye-laws, or an order regulating the employment of workers (including women, audio-visual workers, contract labour and, in mines, under-18s): penalty of <strong>₹50,000 to ₹1 lakh</strong>. Repeat: imprisonment up to three months, or a fine up to ₹2 lakh, or both.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Covers, for example, breaches of employment restrictions and orders; repeat breaches become criminal.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 177 — Central sphere: enquiry by the officer under s.111; appeal within sixty days.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Penalty of ₹50,000 to ₹1 lakh for contraventions, including employment orders (s.97(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Repeat: imprisonment up to three months or fine up to ₹2 lakh, or both (s.97(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\">Section 94 — General penalty for offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\">Section 111 — Power of officers of appropriate Government to impose penalty in certain cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s98","title":"Section 98 — Punishment for falsification of records, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 98 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">98. (1) Whoever—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) produces false records or counterfeits or knowingly makes or produces or uses a false statement, declaration or evidence regarding any document in connection with compliance of any of the provisions of this Code or any rules, regulations or bye-laws or any order made thereunder; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) falsifies any plan or section, the maintenance of which is required by or under this Code or produces before any authority such plan or section, knowing the same to be false; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) makes, gives or delivers knowingly a false plan, section, return, notice, record or report containing a statement, entry or detail,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to three months, or with fine which may extend to one lakh rupees, or with both.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where any person convicted of an offence punishable under sub-section (1) is again convicted of an offence under the same provision, then, he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to six months, or with fine which shall not be less than one lakh rupees but which may extend to two lakh rupees, or with both.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.98; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 55<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Producing false records or evidence, falsifying plans or sections, or knowingly giving false returns or reports: imprisonment up to <strong>three months</strong>, or a fine up to <strong>₹1 lakh</strong>, or both. Repeat: up to six months, or ₹1–2 lakh, or both.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Ensure records are accurate; falsification is a criminal offence.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.98. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Falsification: up to three months or fine up to ₹1 lakh, or both (s.98(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s33\">Section 33 — Maintenance of registers, records and filing of returns</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s99","title":"Section 99 — Penalty for omission to furnish plans, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 99 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">99. Any person who, without reasonable excuse the burden of proving which shall lies upon him, omits to make or furnish in the prescribed form or manner or at, or within, the prescribed time any plan, section, return, notice, register, record or report required by or under any provision of this Code to be made or furnished, he shall be liable to penalty which shall not be less than one lakh rupees but which may extend to two lakh rupees.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.99; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 55<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Omitting, without reasonable excuse (which the person must prove), to make or furnish any plan, section, return, notice, register, record or report as required: penalty of <strong>₹1 lakh to ₹2 lakh</strong>.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Late or missing filings can attract this penalty; document reasons for any delay.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 177 — Central sphere: enquiry by the officer under s.111; appeal within sixty days.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Penalty of ₹1 lakh to ₹2 lakh for omitting to furnish required plans, returns, notices or reports (s.99).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s96\">Section 96 — Penalty for non-maintenance of register, records and non-filing of returns, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\">Section 111 — Power of officers of appropriate Government to impose penalty in certain cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s100","title":"Section 100 — Punishment for disclosure of information","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 100 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">100. (1) Whoever being the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator or any other person referred to in section 39 or section 121 discloses, contrary to the provisions of that section, any such information as is referred to in that section without the consent of the appropriate Government, he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to three months, or with fine which may extend to one lakh rupees, or with both.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) No court shall proceed with the trial of any offence under this section except with the previous sanction of the appropriate Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.100; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 55<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Inspectors and others who disclose confidential information contrary to s.39 or s.121: imprisonment up to three months, or a fine up to ₹1 lakh, or both. Prosecution needs prior sanction.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Protects employer information obtained in inspections.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.100. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Unauthorised disclosure by inspectors: up to three months or fine up to ₹1 lakh, or both (s.100(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s39\">Section 39 — Secrecy of information by Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s121\">Section 121 — Power of appropriate Government to direct inquiry in certain cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s101","title":"Section 101 — Punishment for wrongfully disclosing results of analysis","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 101 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">101. Whoever, except in so far as it may be necessary for the purposes of a prosecution for any offence punishable under this Code, publishes or discloses to any person the results of an analysis, of a sample of substance used or intended to be used in any process under this Code, shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term, which may extend to six months, or with fine, which may extend to fifty thousand rupees, or with both.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.101; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 56<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Publishing or disclosing results of analysis of a sample, except for a prosecution: imprisonment up to six months, or a fine up to ₹50,000, or both.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Treat sampling results as confidential.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.101. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Disclosure of sample analysis results: up to six months or fine up to ₹50,000, or both (s.101).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s35\">Section 35 — Powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s102","title":"Section 102 — Punishment for contravention of provisions of duties relating to hazardous processes","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 102 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">102. (1) Whoever fails to comply with or contravenes any of his duties specified under—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) clauses (a) to (h) of sub-section (1) or sub-section (2) of section 6 or clause (d) of section 13 in so far as such duty relates to hazardous processes; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) section 80,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">shall, in respect of such failure or contravention, be punishable with an imprisonment for a term which may extend to two years and with fine which may extend to five lakh rupees, and in case the failure or contravention continues, with additional fine which may extend to twenty-five thousand rupees for every day during which such failure or contravention continues, after the conviction for the first such failure or contravention.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) If the failure or contravention referred to in sub-section (1) continues beyond a period of one year after the date of conviction, the offender shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to three years or with a fine of twenty lakh rupees, or with both.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.102; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 56<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Failing in employer duties under s.6, or the employee reporting duty under s.13(d), <strong>in relation to hazardous processes</strong>, or in duties under s.80 (common facilities in multi-tenant factories): imprisonment up to <strong>two years</strong> and a fine up to <strong>₹5 lakh</strong>, plus up to ₹25,000 a day if continued after conviction. If it continues more than a year after conviction: up to three years, or a fine of ₹20 lakh, or both.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Hazardous-process sites face the heaviest continuing-breach penalties; prioritise s.6 compliance there.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.102. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Hazardous-process duty breaches: up to two years and fine up to ₹5 lakh, plus ₹25,000 a day for continuing breach (s.102(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s80\">Section 80 — Liability of owner of premises in certain circumstances</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\">Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s103","title":"Section 103 — Punishment for contravention of provisions of duties relating to safety provisions resulting in an accident","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 103 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">103. (1) If a person fails to comply with or contravenes any duties under this Code or the regulations, rules, bye-laws or orders made thereunder and such non-compliance or contravention has resulted in an accident or dangerous occurrences causing—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) death, he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to two years, or with a fine which shall not be less than five lakh rupees, or with both; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) serious bodily injury to any person within the establishment, he shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to one year, or with a fine which shall not be less than two lakh rupees but not exceeding four lakh rupees, or with both:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that while imposing the fine under this section, the court may direct that a portion of the fine, which shall not be less than fifty per cent. thereof, shall be given as compensation to the victim or to the legal heirs of the victim, in the case of his death.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where a person having been convicted under sub-section (1) is again convicted thereunder, shall be punishable with double the punishment provided under that sub-section for first conviction.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.103; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 56<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Where a breach of duty causes an accident or dangerous occurrence resulting in:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>death:</strong> imprisonment up to <strong>two years</strong>, or a fine of <strong>at least ₹5 lakh</strong>, or both;</li><li><strong>serious bodily injury:</strong> imprisonment up to <strong>one year</strong>, or a fine of <strong>₹2–4 lakh</strong>, or both.</li></ul><p>The court may direct that at least <strong>50% of the fine</strong> goes to the victim or his heirs. Repeat convictions carry double punishment.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Accident-linked breaches carry minimum fines and victim compensation. There is no s.110 compliance opportunity in accident cases.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.103. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Breach causing death: up to two years, or fine not less than ₹5 lakh, or both (s.103(1)(a)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Breach causing serious bodily injury: up to one year, or fine of ₹2–4 lakh, or both (s.103(1)(b)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">At least 50% of the fine may be directed to the victim or heirs (proviso to s.103(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\">Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s104","title":"Section 104 — Special provision for contravention of order under section 38","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 104 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">104. Whoever continues to work in contravention of any general or special order issued under the provisions of section 38, shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to two years and shall also be liable to fine which may extend to five lakh rupees:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the court shall not impose a fine under this section which shall be less than two lakh rupees without recording in the judgment the reasons for imposing such fine.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.104; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 56<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Continuing to work in contravention of a s.38 prohibition order: imprisonment up to <strong>two years</strong> and a fine up to <strong>₹5 lakh</strong>. A fine below ₹2 lakh needs recorded reasons.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Comply with prohibition orders immediately.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.104. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Working in breach of a s.38 order: up to two years and fine up to ₹5 lakh (s.104).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s38\">Section 38 — Special powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitator in respect of factory, mines, dock work and building or other construction work</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s105","title":"Section 105 — Failure to appoint manager in mine","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 105 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">105. Whoever in compliance of the provisions of section 67, fails to appoint a manager shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to three months, or with fine which may extend to one lakh rupees, or with both.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.105; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 56<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Failing to appoint a mine manager under s.67: imprisonment up to three months, or a fine up to ₹1 lakh, or both.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mines only.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.105. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Failure to appoint a mine manager: up to three months or fine up to ₹1 lakh, or both (s.105).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s67\">Section 67 — Managers</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s106","title":"Section 106 — Offences by employees","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 106 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">106. (1) Subject to the provisions of section 13, except clause (d) thereof, if any employee employed in a workplace contravenes any provision of this Code or any rules or orders made thereunder, imposing any duty or liability on employee, he shall be punishable with penalty which may extend to ten thousand rupees.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where an employee is convicted of an offence punishable under sub-section (1), the employer of the establishment shall not be deemed to be guilty of an offence in respect of that contravention, unless it is proved that he failed to take all reasonable measures for its prevention.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.106; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 57<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>An employee who contravenes a duty imposed on employees (other than the reporting duty in s.13(d)) is liable to a penalty up to <strong>₹10,000</strong>. The employer is not guilty for that contravention unless it failed to take all reasonable preventive measures.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Document safety training and enforcement; it protects the employer when an employee breaches a duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 177 — Central sphere: enquiry by the officer under s.111; appeal within sixty days.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Employees contravening their duties are liable to a penalty up to ₹10,000 (s.106(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s13\">Section 13 — Duties of employee</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s107","title":"Section 107 — Prosecution of owner, agent or manager of mine","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 107 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">107. No prosecution shall be instituted against any owner, agent or manager of a mine for any offence under this Code except at the instance of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or of the District Magistrate or of Inspector-cum-Facilitator authorised in this behalf by general or special order in writing by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or the District Magistrate or the Inspector-cum-Facilitator as so authorised shall before instituting such prosecution satisfy himself that the owner, agent or manager of a mine had failed to exercise due diligence to prevent the commission of such offence:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that in respect of an offence committed in the course of the technical direction and management of a mine, the District Magistrate shall not institute any prosecution against an owner, agent or manager of a mine without the previous approval of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.107; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 57<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Prosecution of a mine owner, agent or manager may be instituted only by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, the District Magistrate or an authorised Inspector-cum-Facilitator, after satisfying himself that due diligence was not exercised. Prosecution by the District Magistrate for technical offences needs the Chief Inspector&#39;s approval.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mines only.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.107. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Prosecution of mine owners, agents or managers requires the prescribed authority and a due-diligence finding (s.107).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s7\">Section 7 — Duties and responsibilities of owner, agent and manager in relation to mine</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s108","title":"Section 108 — Exemption of owner, agent or manager of mine or occupier of factory from liability in certain cases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 108 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">108. Where the owner, agent or manager of the mine or employer or occupier of the factory is charged with an offence punishable under this Code he shall be entitled, upon complaint duly made by him and on giving to the prosecutor not less than three clear days&#39; notice in writing of his intention so to do, to have any other person whom he charges as the actual offender brought before the Court at the time appointed for hearing the charge; and if, after the commission of the offence has been proved, the owner, agent or manager of the mine or occupier or manager of the factory, as the case may be, proves to the satisfaction of the Court—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) that he has exercised due diligence to enforce the execution of this Code, or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) that the said other person committed the offence in question without his knowledge, consent or connivance,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">that other person shall be convicted of the offence and shall be liable to the like punishment as if he was the owner, agent or manager of the mine or occupier or manager of the factory, as the case may be, and the owner, agent or manager of a mine or the occupier or the manager of the factory shall be, discharged from any liability under this Code in respect of such offence:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that in seeking to prove as aforesaid the owner, agent or manager of a mine or the occupier or manager of the factory, as the case may be, may be examined on oath, and his evidence and that of any witness whom he calls in his support, shall be subject to cross-examination on behalf of the person he charges as the actual offender and by the prosecutor:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that, if the person charged as the actual offender by the owner, agent or manager of the mine or occupier or manager of the factory, as the case may be, cannot be brought before the court at the time appointed for hearing the charge, the court shall adjourn the hearing from time to time for a period not exceeding three months and if by the end of the said period the person charged as the actual offender cannot still be brought before the court, the court shall proceed to hear the charge against the owner, agent or manager of the mine or occupier or manager of the factory, as the case may be, and shall, if the offence be proved, convict him.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.108; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 57<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>A charged mine owner, agent or manager, or factory occupier or employer, may on three clear days&#39; notice bring the actual offender before the court. If he proves due diligence, or that the offence was committed without his knowledge, consent or connivance, the other person is convicted and he is discharged.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Keep evidence of due diligence and delegation of responsibility.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.108. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">An owner, agent, manager or occupier may be discharged by proving due diligence and bringing the actual offender before the court (s.108).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s109\">Section 109 — Offences by companies, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s109","title":"Section 109 — Offences by companies, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 109 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">109. (1) Where an offence under this Code has been committed by a company, every person who, at the time the offence was committed, was in charge of, and was responsible to, the company for the conduct of the business of the company, as well as the company, shall be deemed to be guilty of the offence and shall be liable to be proceeded against and punished accordingly:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that nothing contained in this sub-section shall render any such person liable to any punishment, if he proves that the offence was committed without his knowledge or that he had exercised all due diligence to prevent the commission of such offence.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Notwithstanding anything contained in sub-section (1) where any offence under this Code has been committed by a company and it is proved that the offence has been committed with the consent or connivance of, or is attributable to any neglect on the part of any director, manager, company secretary or other officer of the company, such director, manager, company secretary or other officer shall be deemed to be guilty of that offence and shall be liable to be proceeded against and punished accordingly.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this section,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) &quot;company&quot; means any body corporate and includes a firm or other association of individuals; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) &quot;director&quot; means,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) in relation to a firm a partner thereof; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) the owner of a mine being a firm or other association of individuals or a company; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) in case of association of individuals other than specified in sub-clause (ii), any of its members.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.109; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 58<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Where a company (including a firm or association) commits an offence, the company and every person in charge of and responsible for its business are liable, unless they prove lack of knowledge or due diligence. Directors, managers, company secretaries and officers are liable where the offence involved their consent, connivance or neglect.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Nominate and document responsible persons and their compliance oversight.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.109. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Persons in charge of the company&#39;s business are deemed guilty unless they prove lack of knowledge or due diligence (s.109(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s108\">Section 108 — Exemption of owner, agent or manager of mine or occupier of factory from liability in certain cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s110","title":"Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 110 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">110. (1) Notwithstanding anything contained in this Chapter, the Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall, not initiate prosecution proceeding against an employer for any offence under this Chapter, give an opportunity to comply with relevant provisions of this Act within a period of thirty days from the date of notice giving opportunity, and, if the employer complies with such provisions within such period, then, no such proceeding shall be initiated against such employer:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that no such opportunity shall be accorded to an employer in case of an accident and if the violation of the same nature of the provisions under this Code is repeated within a period of three years from the date on which such first violation was committed and in such case the prosecution shall be initiated in accordance with provisions of sub-section (2).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) No court shall take cognizance of any offence punishable under this Code, unless a complaint in respect thereof is made within six months of the date on which the alleged commission of the offence came to the knowledge of the Inspector-cum-Facilitator and a complaint is filed in that regard by him.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) No court inferior to that of a Metropolitan Magistrate or a Judicial Magistrate of the First Class shall try any offence punishable under this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this section,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) in the case of a continuing offence, the period of limitation shall be computed with reference to every point of time during which the offence continues;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) where for the performance of any act, time is granted or extended on an application made by the employer of an establishment, the period of limitation shall be computed from the date on which the time so granted or extended expired.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.110; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 58<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Opportunity to comply.</strong> Before prosecuting an employer, the Inspector-cum-Facilitator must give <strong>30 days</strong> to comply; compliance bars prosecution. This does not apply in case of an <strong>accident</strong>, or where a violation of the same nature is repeated within <strong>three years</strong>.</p><p><strong>Limitation.</strong> A court may take cognizance only on the Inspector-cum-Facilitator&#39;s complaint made within <strong>six months</strong> of the offence coming to his knowledge (continuing offences are counted throughout).</p><p><strong>Court.</strong> Metropolitan Magistrate or Judicial Magistrate first class.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Treat a compliance notice as the last chance; comply within 30 days and keep proof.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Sub-section (1) refers to &#39;this Act&#39;; reproduced as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.110. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Before prosecution, the employer gets thirty days to comply, except after an accident or a repeat within three years (s.110(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Complaints must be made within six months of the offence coming to the inspector&#39;s knowledge (s.110(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\">Section 111 — Power of officers of appropriate Government to impose penalty in certain cases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s114\">Section 114 — Composition of certain offences</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s111","title":"Section 111 — Power of officers of appropriate Government to impose penalty in certain cases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 111 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">111. (1) Notwithstanding anything contained in section 110, for the purpose of imposing penalty under sub-section (3) of section 12 or sections 94, 96, 97, 99, 106 and sub-section (3) of section 114, the appropriate Government may appoint any officer not below the rank of Under Secretary to the Government of India or an officer of equivalent rank in the State Government, as the case may be, for holding enquiry in such manner, as may be prescribed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) While holding the enquiry, the officer referred to in sub-section (1) shall have the power to summon and enforce attendance of any person acquainted with the facts and circumstances of the case to give evidence or to produce any document, which in the opinion of such officer, may be useful for or relevant to the subject-matter of the enquiry and if, on such enquiry, he is satisfied that the person has committed any offence under the provisions referred to in sub-section (1), he may impose such penalty as he thinks fit in accordance with the provisions of that sub-section.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Any person aggrieved by an order made by the officer under sub-section (2) may prefer an appeal, in such form and manner and accompanied by such fee as may be prescribed, to the appellate authority to be appointed by the appropriate Government from amongst officers not below the rank of Deputy Secretary to the Government of India or an officer of equivalent rank in the State Government, as the case may be, within sixty days from the date on which the copy of the order made by the officer referred in sub-section (1) is received by the aggrieved person.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) The appellate authority may, after giving the parties to the appeal an opportunity of being heard, pass such order as he thinks fit, confirming, modifying or setting aside the order appealed against, within a period of sixty days from the date of receipt of appeal.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Where a person fails to pay the penalty so imposed within a period of ninety days from the date of receipt of the copy of the order, he shall be punishable with fine which shall not be less than twenty-five thousand rupees but which may extend up to two lakh rupees.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) The amount of penalty imposed and received under this section shall be credited to the fund established under sub-section (1) of section 115.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.111; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 59<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>For penalties under ss.12(3), 94, 96, 97, 99, 106 and 114(3), the appropriate Government may appoint an officer (at least Under Secretary rank) to hold an <strong>enquiry</strong> and impose the penalty. Appeals lie within <strong>60 days</strong> to an officer of at least Deputy Secretary rank, decided within 60 days. Failing to pay a penalty within <strong>90 days</strong> is an offence (fine ₹25,000–₹2 lakh). Penalties go to the social security fund.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 177): the enquiry starts on the Inspector-cum-Facilitator&#39;s complaint, with summons, evidence and cross-examination; ex parte after two non-appearances; at most three adjournments; appeal within 60 days.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 177 — Central sphere: enquiry by the officer under s.111; appeal within sixty days.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Penalties under specified sections are imposed by an officer after an enquiry, with appeal within sixty days (s.111(1)–(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Failure to pay a penalty within ninety days is punishable with a fine of ₹25,000 to ₹2 lakh (s.111(5)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\">Section 94 — General penalty for offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s96\">Section 96 — Penalty for non-maintenance of register, records and non-filing of returns, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s97\">Section 97 — Punishment for contravention of certain provisions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s115\">Section 115 — Social security fund</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s112","title":"Section 112 — Jurisdiction of court for entertaining proceedings, etc., for offence","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 112 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">112. For the purposes of conferring jurisdiction on any court in relation to an offence under this Code or the rules, regulation or bye-laws made thereunder in connection with an establishment, the place where the establishment is for the time being situated, shall be deemed to be the place where such offence has been committed.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.112; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 59<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>An offence is deemed committed where the establishment is situated, for court jurisdiction.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Proceedings are local to the establishment.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.112. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Jurisdiction lies where the establishment is situated (s.112).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\">Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s113","title":"Section 113 — Power of court to make orders","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 113 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">113. (1) Where the employer of a mine or a factory or a dock is convicted of an offence punishable under this Code, the court may, in addition to awarding him any punishment, by order in writing, require him within the period specified in the order (which may be extended by the court from time to time on application made in this behalf) to take such measures as may be specified in the order for remedying the matters in respect of which the offence was committed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where an order is made under sub-section (1), the employer of the mine or the factory shall not be liable under this Code in respect of the continuance of the offence during the period or extended period, if any, but if on the expiry of such period or extended period the order of the court has not been fully complied with, employer shall be deemed to have committed a further offence and shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to six months, or with fine which may extend to one hundred rupees for every day after such expiry on which the order has not been complied with, or with both.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.113; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 59<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>On convicting the employer of a mine, factory or dock, the court may order specified remedial measures within a set time. During that time the employer is not liable for continuance; non-compliance after it is a further offence (up to six months, or ₹100 a day, or both).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Comply with court remedial orders within the time set.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.113. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Courts may order remedial measures; non-compliance is a further offence (s.113).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\">Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s114","title":"Section 114 — Composition of certain offences","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 114 · Chapter XII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">114. (1) Notwithstanding anything contained in the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, any penalty under sub-section (3) of section 12 or section 94 or section 96 or sub-section (1) of section 97 or section 99 or section 106 or sub-section (3) or any offence under sub-section (2) of section 97 or sub-section (1) of section 100 or section 101 or clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 103 or section 105 or sub-section (2) of section 113 may either before or after the holding the enquiry or, as the case may be, of institution of prosecution may be compounded by such officer of the appropriate Government as may be notified by that Government in the manner as may be prescribed by it—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) in a case of penalty for a sum of fifty per cent. of the maximum penalty provided for such penalty; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) in a case of offence for a sum of seventy-five per cent. of the maximum fine provided for such offence.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Where a penalty or an offence has been compounded under sub-section (1), the person liable for penalty or the offender, as the case may be, shall be discharged of the penalty or offence and there shall be no further proceedings against him in respect of such penalty or offence.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Any person who fails to comply with an order made by the officer referred to in sub-section (1), shall be liable to pay a penalty equivalent to twenty per cent. of the maximum penalty or fine provided for the penalty or the offence, as the case may be, in addition to the penalty or fine.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) The amount of composition received under sub-section (1) shall be credited to the fund established under sub-section (1) of section 115 for the unorganised workers.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Nothing contained in sub-section (1) shall apply to a penalty or an offence committed by a person for a second or subsequent time within a period of three years from the date of penalty or offence, as the case may be,—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) which was earlier compounded; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) for which such person was earlier convicted.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.114; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 59<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Compoundable:</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>penalties under ss.12(3), 94, 96, 97(1), 99 and 106, at <strong>50% of the maximum penalty</strong>;</li><li>offences under ss.97(2), 100(1), 101, 103(1)(b), 105 and 113(2), at <strong>75% of the maximum fine</strong>.</li></ul><p>Compounding is possible before or after enquiry or prosecution, and discharges the person. Failure to comply with a compounding order costs a further 20% of the maximum. Compounding is not available for a second offence within <strong>three years</strong>. Amounts go to the social security fund.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rule 182): apply in <strong>Form XXVI</strong>; compounding notice in <strong>Form XXVII</strong>; pay within <strong>15 days</strong>; certificate within 10 days; prosecution follows one month after any default.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Death-causing breaches (s.103(1)(a)) and hazardous-process breaches (s.102) are not listed as compoundable.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 182 — Central sphere: compounding procedure.</li></ul><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Compounding at 50% of the maximum penalty, or 75% of the maximum fine (s.114(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No compounding for a second offence within three years (s.114(5)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: Form XXVI application; Form XXVII notice; payment within fifteen days (rule 182).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\">Section 94 — General penalty for offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s103\">Section 103 — Punishment for contravention of provisions of duties relating to safety provisions resulting in an accident</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s115\">Section 115 — Social security fund</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-xiii","title":"Chapter XIII — Social Security Fund (Sections 115–115)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s115","title":"Section 115 — Social security fund","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 115 · Chapter XIII <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">115. (1) There shall be established by the appropriate Government a social security fund for the welfare of the unorganised workers to which there shall be credited the amount received from composition of the offence as specified in sub-section (4) of section 114 and the amount of the penalty as specified in sub-section (6) of section 111.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The fund may also be funded by such other sources as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The fund shall be administered and expended for welfare of the unorganised workers in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government including the transfer of the amount in the fund to any fund established under any other law for the time being in force for the welfare of the unorganised workers.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purpose of this section the expression &quot;unorganised worker&quot; shall have the same meaning as is assigned to it under clause (m) of section 2 of the Unorganised Workers Social Security Act, 2008.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.115; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 60<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government establishes a <strong>social security fund</strong> for unorganised workers, credited with compounding amounts (s.114(4)) and penalties (s.111(6)), and other prescribed sources, and administered for their welfare.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Central sphere (rule 183): the Social Security Fund under s.141 of the Code on Social Security is deemed to be the fund under this section.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The Explanation refers to the Unorganised Workers&#39; Social Security Act, 2008, which is repealed by the Code on Social Security; reproduced as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 183 — Social Security Fund.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Compounding amounts and penalties are credited to a social security fund for unorganised workers (s.115(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central sphere: the SS Code s.141 fund is deemed set up under s.115 (rule 183).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Rules</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\">Section 111 — Power of officers of appropriate Government to impose penalty in certain cases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s114\">Section 114 — Composition of certain offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s141\">Section 141 — Social Security Fund</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-xiv","title":"Chapter XIV — Miscellaneous (Sections 116–143)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.s116","title":"Section 116 — Delegation of powers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 116 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">116. The Central Government may, by notification, direct that any power exercisable by it under this Code or rules made thereunder shall, in relation to such matters and subject to such conditions, if any, as may be specified in the notification, be exercisable also by the State Government or by such officer or authority subordinate to the State Government as may be specified in the said notification.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.116; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 60<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may delegate its powers to State Governments or their officers by notification.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.116. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central powers may be delegated to State Governments by notification (s.116).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s123\">Section 123 — Powers of Central Government to give directions</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s117","title":"Section 117 — Onus as to age","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 117 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">117. (1) When any offence is committed under this Code involving an issue of a certain age of a person and such person is in the opinion of the court prima facie under such age, the burden shall be on the accused to prove that such person is not under such age.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The medical authority prescribed by the Central Government shall, while examining a worker for issuing the certificate of age for the purposes of this Code, take into account the Aadhaar card of the worker, and in the absence thereof, the date of birth certificate from school or the matriculation or equivalent certificate from the concerned examination Board of the worker, if available, and in the absence thereof, the birth certificate of the worker given by a corporation or a municipal authority or a Panchayat, and only in the absence of any of the methods specified in this sub-section, the age shall be determined by such medical authority through an ossification test or any other latest medical age determination test.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.117; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 61<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Where a person&#39;s age is in issue and he appears under that age, the accused must prove otherwise. Age certificates are based, in order, on Aadhaar, school or matriculation certificate, or a municipal or panchayat birth certificate; only if none exists, on an ossification or other medical test.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Keep age-proof documents on file for young workers (Aadhaar first).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 178 — Medical authority for age certificates.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The accused bears the onus of proving that a person is not under the relevant age (s.117(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Age is certified from Aadhaar, then school certificates, then birth certificates, and only then by medical test (s.117(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s70\">Section 70 — Employment of persons below eighteen years of age</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s118","title":"Section 118 — Onus of proving limits of what is practicable, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 118 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">118. In any proceeding for an offence for the contravention of any provision of this Code or regulations or bye-laws or rules made thereunder consisting of a failure to comply with a duty or requirement to do something, it shall be for the person who is alleged to have failed to comply with such duty or requirement, to prove that it was not reasonably practicable or all practicable measures were taken to satisfy the duty or requirement.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.118; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 61<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>In prosecutions for failing to do something, the accused must prove that it was not reasonably practicable, or that all practicable measures were taken.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Document risk assessments and the reasoning behind &#39;reasonably practicable&#39; decisions.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.118. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The accused must prove that compliance was not reasonably practicable or all practicable measures were taken (s.118).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s119","title":"Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 119 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">119. (1) Notwithstanding anything contained in this Code, any person desirous of obtaining common licence in respect of a factory, industrial premises for beedi and cigar work and for engaging contract workers or any combination thereof or single licence for any one of them under this Code shall make an application electronically or otherwise to such authority as may be designated, by notification, by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The application under sub-section (1),—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) shall be in such form and filed in such manner and accompanied by such fee and contain such information as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) shall, in so far as it relates to the licence for engaging contract labours, contain the number of inter-State migrant workers employed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) On receipt of an application under sub-section (1), the authority referred to in that sub-section shall take such actions in such manner and make such inquiry as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Where the authority referred to in sub-section (1) is satisfied that the common licence may be issued in respect of a factory, industrial premises for beedi and cigar work and for engaging contract workers or any combination thereof or single licence for any one of them under this Code, such authority shall issue a licence electronically within forty-five days of the receipt of application failing which the licence shall be deemed to be issued and shall be auto generated and the responsibility of such failure shall be on such authority:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that where the licence is deemed to be issued, no further inquiry shall be made:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that the form of licence shall, as far as practicable, be similar throughout India:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided also that where such authority rejects the application he shall assign the reason for such rejection.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Notwithstanding anything contained in this Code, any licence in respect of a factory, industrial premises for beedi and cigar work and for engaging contract labour has been obtained under any Central labour law before the commencement of this Code, in respect of any establishment shall be deemed to have been obtained under the provisions of this Code and shall be valid for the period for which it was issued and shall have to be obtained afresh after its expiration.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) Any person aggrieved by an order passed under this section by the authority referred to in sub-section (1) may file, within thirty days from the date of the order, an appeal in such form, accompanied with such fee to such appellate authority as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government and the appeal shall be disposed of electronically within thirty days of the filing of the appeal.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.119; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 61<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Common licence.</strong> A single electronic application may be made for a common licence covering any combination of a <strong>factory</strong>, <strong>beedi and cigar industrial premises</strong> and <strong>engaging contract labour</strong>, or a single licence for one of them, to the designated authority.</p><p><strong>Deemed licence.</strong> The licence is issued within <strong>45 days</strong>, otherwise it is <strong>deemed issued</strong> and auto-generated, with no further inquiry. Reasons must be given for rejection.</p><p><strong>Existing licences</strong> under Central labour laws continue for their period, then must be obtained afresh.</p><p><strong>Appeal</strong> within 30 days, decided electronically within 30 days.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Combine factory and contract-labour licensing where possible. Track the 45-day deemed-licence date; renew pre-Code licences on expiry under the Code.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>For factories and beedi premises, the form, fees and inquiry are prescribed by the State Government (appropriate Government); Central Rules cover contract labour licences in the Central sphere (rules 85–96).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 85–96 — Central sphere: contract labour licensing.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">A common licence may be obtained for a factory, beedi and cigar premises and engaging contract labour (s.119(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Licences are issued within forty-five days, or deemed issued and auto-generated (s.119(4)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Licences obtained under Central labour laws before the Code remain valid for their period (s.119(5)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s74\">Section 74 — Licence to industrial premises and person</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s79\">Section 79 — Approval and licensing of factories</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s120","title":"Section 120 — Effect of law and agreements inconsistent with Code","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 120 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">120. (1) The provisions of this Code shall have effect notwithstanding anything inconsistent therewith contained in any other law for the time being in force or in the terms of any award, agreement or contract of service whether made before or after the commencement of this Code:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that where under any such award, agreement, contract of service or otherwise an employee is entitled to benefits in respect of any matters which are more favourable to him than those to which he will be entitled to under this Code, the employee shall continue to get the former notwithstanding that he receives benefits in respect of other matters under this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Nothing contained in this Code shall be construed as precluding any employee from entering into an agreement with an employer for granting him rights or privileges in respect of any matter which are more favourable to him than those to which he would be entitled under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.120; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 62<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Code overrides inconsistent laws, awards, agreements and contracts, but employees keep more favourable benefits, and may agree more favourable terms.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Existing more-favourable terms (for example, more leave) survive.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.120. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Code prevails over inconsistent laws and agreements (s.120(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">More favourable benefits continue (proviso to s.120(1); s.120(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s161\">Section 161 — Effect of laws and agreements inconsistent with this Code</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s121","title":"Section 121 — Power of appropriate Government to direct inquiry in certain cases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 121 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">121. (1) The appropriate Government may, in the event of the occurrence of an accident in an establishment which has caused or had the potentiality to cause serious danger to employees and other persons within, and in the vicinity of the workplace or whether immediate or delayed, or any occupational disease as specified in the Third Schedule, which has been or is suspected to have been contracted, in epidemic proportions, appoint one or more persons possessing legal or special knowledge to act as assessors or competent persons in such inquiry in order to inquire into the causes of the accident and disease, fix responsibilities and suggest a plan of action for the future to prevent such accidents or diseases and submit the report to the appropriate Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) The appropriate Government may direct a Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or any other officer under the control of the Government concerned or appoint a committee to undertake a survey in such manner as may be prescribed by the appropriate Government on the situation relating to safety or health at work at any workplace or class of workplaces or into the effect of work activity on the health of the employees and other persons within and in the vicinity of the workplace.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The officer directed or committee appointed, under sub-section (1) or sub-section (2), to hold an inquiry, shall have the powers of a civil court under the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908, for the purposes of enforcing the attendance of witnesses and compelling the production of documents and material objects, and may also so far as may be necessary for the purposes of the inquiry, exercise such powers of an Inspector-cum-Facilitator under this Code as may be necessary.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) The Central Government may make rules for regulating the procedure of inquiry and survey and other related matters under this section.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.121; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 62<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>After a serious accident, or a Third Schedule disease in epidemic proportions, the appropriate Government may appoint competent persons to inquire into causes, fix responsibility and recommend prevention. It may also order safety and health surveys. Inquirers have civil court powers.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p><strong>Central sphere</strong> (rules 179–181): competent persons inquire into causes and responsibility; dock work inquiries need at least 30 days&#39; notice; employers&#39; associations, unions and injured persons may appear.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The CPC reference is reproduced as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 179–181 — Central sphere: inquiry procedure.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Inquiries into serious accidents and epidemic occupational diseases, with civil court powers (s.121).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s20\">Section 20 — Safety and occupational health surveys</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s122","title":"Section 122 — Publication of reports","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 122 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">122. The appropriate Government may, if it thinks fit, cause to be published any report submitted to it by the National Board or State Advisory Board or any extracts from any report submitted to it under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.122; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 62<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Reports of the National or State Boards, or extracts from reports under the Code, may be published.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.122. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Reports may be published (s.122).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s16\">Section 16 — National Occupational Safety and Health Advisory Board</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s123","title":"Section 123 — Powers of Central Government to give directions","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 123 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">123. The Central Government may give directions to a State Government for the implementation of the provisions of this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.123; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 62<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may direct State Governments on implementation.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.123. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central directions to States (s.123).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s116\">Section 116 — Delegation of powers</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s124","title":"Section 124 — General restriction on disclosure of information","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 124 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">124. (1) No person shall in respect of the establishment, disclose any information relating to any manufacturing or commercial business or any working process which may come to his knowledge in the course of his official duties.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Nothing in sub-section (1) shall apply to any disclosure of information made with the previous consent in writing of the owner of the business or process or for the purposes of any legal proceeding (including adjudication or arbitration), pursuant to any of the relevant statutory provisions or of any criminal proceeding under this Code which may be taken, whether pursuant to any of the relevant statutory provisions or otherwise, or for the purposes of any report of any such proceedings.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.124; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 62<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No person may disclose business or process information learnt in official duties, except with the owner&#39;s written consent or for legal proceedings.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Protects trade secrets disclosed during inspections.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.124. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Officials may not disclose business information except with consent or for proceedings (s.124).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s39\">Section 39 — Secrecy of information by Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s100\">Section 100 — Punishment for disclosure of information</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s125","title":"Section 125 — Jurisdiction of civil courts barred","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 125 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">125. No civil court shall have jurisdiction in respect of any matter to which any provision of this Code applies and no injunction shall be granted by any civil court in respect of anything which is done or intended to be done by or under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.125; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 63<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Civil courts have no jurisdiction over matters under the Code, and may not grant injunctions against action under it.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Challenges go through statutory appeals or writ jurisdiction.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.125. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Civil court jurisdiction is barred (s.125).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s38\">Section 38 — Special powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitator in respect of factory, mines, dock work and building or other construction work</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s126","title":"Section 126 — Protection of action taken in good faith","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 126 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">126. (1) No suit, prosecution or other legal proceeding shall lie against any person for anything which is in good faith done or intended to be done in pursuance of this Code or any rule or regulation or bye-laws or order made thereunder.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) No prosecution or other legal proceeding shall lie against the Government, any Board or committees constituted under this Code or any member of such Board or any officer or employee of the Government or the Board or any other person authorised by the Government or any Board or committee, for any damage caused or likely to be caused by anything which is in good faith done or intended to be done in pursuance of this Code or any rule or regulation or bye-laws or order made or issued thereunder.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.126; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 63<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>No proceeding lies against any person, the Government, Boards or officers for anything done in good faith under the Code.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.126. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Good-faith acts are protected (s.126).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s125\">Section 125 — Jurisdiction of civil courts barred</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s127","title":"Section 127 — Power to exempt in special cases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 127 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">127. (1) The appropriate Government may, by notification and subject to such conditions and restrictions, if any, and for such period or periods as may be specified in the notification, direct that all or any of the provisions of this Code or the rules or the regulations made thereunder shall not apply to or in relation to any establishment or class of establishments.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Without prejudice to the generality of sub-section (1), where the State Government is satisfied in the public interest that it is necessary to create more economic activities and employment opportunities, it may, by notification, exempt, subject to such conditions as it may think fit, any new factory or class or description of new factories from all or any of the provisions of this Code for such period from the date on which such commercial production starts, as may be specified in the notification:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that any notification issued by a State Government under the Factories Act, 1948 for the time being in force in the State prior to the commencement of this Code to achieve the same purpose as is specified in this sub-section, shall remain in force after such commencement for its remaining period as if the provisions of this Code, to the extent they defeat any purpose to be achieved by such notification issued by the State Government, were not in force.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purpose of this sub-section, the expression &quot;new factory or class or description of new Factories&quot; means such factory or class or description of Factories which are established and whose commercial production start within such period as may be specified in the notification.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.127; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 63<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The appropriate Government may exempt establishments from the Code by notification. A State may exempt <strong>new factories</strong> for a period from the start of commercial production, to promote economic activity and employment; earlier Factories Act notifications for the same purpose continue for their remaining period.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check for State exemption notifications for new factories.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The reference to the Factories Act, 1948 is reproduced as printed; it is repealed by s.143.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.127. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">States may exempt new factories for a period from the start of commercial production (s.127(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s128\">Section 128 — Power to exempt during public emergency</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s129\">Section 129 — Power to exempt public institution</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s128","title":"Section 128 — Power to exempt during public emergency","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 128 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">128. In case of a public emergency or disaster or pandemic in whole of India or part thereof, the appropriate Government may, by notification, exempt any workplace or work activity or class thereof from all or any of the provisions of this Code for such period and subject to such conditions as it may think fit:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that no such notification shall be made for a period exceeding one year at a time.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Explanation.—For the purposes of this section “public emergency” means a grave emergency whereby the security of India or any part of the territory thereof is threatened, whether by war or external aggression or internal disturbance.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.128; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 63<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>In a public emergency, disaster or pandemic, the appropriate Government may exempt workplaces from the Code, for up to one year at a time.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Watch for emergency exemptions.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.128. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Emergency exemptions last at most one year at a time (proviso to s.128).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s127\">Section 127 — Power to exempt in special cases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s58\">Section 58 — Power to exempt in special cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s129","title":"Section 129 — Power to exempt public institution","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 129 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">129. The appropriate Government may exempt, subject to such conditions as it may consider necessary, any workshop or workplace where a manufacturing process is carried on and which is attached to a public institution maintained for the purposes of education, training, research or information, from all or any of the provisions of this Code:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that no such exemption shall be granted from the provisions relating to hours of work and holidays unless the persons having the control of the institution submit, for the approval of the appropriate Government, a scheme of the regulation of the hours of employment, intervals for meals, and holidays of the persons employed in or attending the institution or who are inmates for the institution, and the appropriate Government is satisfied that the provisions of the scheme are not less favourable than the corresponding provisions of this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.129; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 64<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Workshops attached to public educational, training or research institutions may be exempted; exemption from hours and holidays requires an approved scheme at least as favourable as the Code.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Institutions should file a scheme before relying on an hours exemption.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.129. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Public institution workshops may be exempted subject to conditions (s.129).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s127\">Section 127 — Power to exempt in special cases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s130","title":"Section 130 — Persons required to give notice, etc., legally bound to do so","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 130 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">130. Every person required to give any notice or to furnish any information to any authority in relation to the provisions of this Code shall be legally bound to do so within the meaning of section 176 of the Indian Penal Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.130; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 64<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Every person required to give notice or information to an authority under the Code is legally bound to do so within the meaning of s.176 of the IPC.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Failure to give required notices may also be an IPC offence.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The IPC reference is not updated for the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (interpretation).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.130. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Persons required to give notices are legally bound to do so (s.130).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s131","title":"Section 131 — Power of Central Government to amend Schedule","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 131 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">131. The Central Government may, by notification, amend any Schedule by way of addition, alteration or omission therein and on any such notification being issued, the Schedule shall be deemed to be amended accordingly.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.131; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 64<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may amend any Schedule by notification.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check for Schedule amendments (hazardous processes, standards, diseases).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.131. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Central Government may amend any Schedule by notification (s.131).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch1\">The First Schedule — List of industries involving hazardous processes</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch2\">The Second Schedule — Matters for occupational safety and health standards</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch3\">The Third Schedule — List of notifiable diseases</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s132","title":"Section 132 — Power to remove difficulties","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 132 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">132. (1) If any difficulty arises in giving effect to the provisions of this Code, the Central Government may, by order published in the Official Gazette, make such provisions not inconsistent with the provisions of this Code, as appears to it to be necessary or expedient for removing the difficulty:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that no such order shall be made after the expiry of two years from the date on which this Code comes into force.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Every order made under this section shall, as soon as may be after it is made, be laid before each House of Parliament.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.132; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 64<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may remove difficulties by order within two years of the Code coming into force; orders are laid before Parliament.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Watch for removal-of-difficulty orders until about November 2027.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.132. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">No removal-of-difficulty order after two years from commencement (proviso to s.132(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s143\">Section 143 — Repeal and Savings</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s133","title":"Section 133 — Power of appropriate Government to make rules","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 133 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">133. (1) The appropriate Government may, subject to the condition of previous publication and by notification, make rules for carrying out the purposes of this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) In particular and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing power, such rules may provide for all or any of the following matters, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) income from the sources under Explanation to clause (x) of sub-section (1) of section 2;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) substance or quantity of substance under clause (zb) of sub-section (1) of section 2;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the late fee under the proviso to sub-section (1) of section 3;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) the manner of submitting application under sub-section (2) of section 3 and the form of such application and the particulars to be contained therein and the fees to be accompanied therewith;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) the form and manner of sending the notice and the authority to whom the notice shall be sent and the manner of intimating the authority under sub-section (1) of section 5;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) annual health examination or test free of costs, age of employees or class of employees or establishment or class of establishments under clause (c) of sub-section (1) of section 6;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) the information to be included in the letter of appointment and the form of such letter under clause (f) of sub-section (1) of section 6;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) the nature of bodily injury and the manner of notice and the time within which the notice shall be sent and the authority to which notice shall be sent under sub-section (1) of section 10;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) nature of dangerous occurrence and the form of notice, the time within which and the authority to which notice shall be sent under section 11;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) the form of notice related to certain diseases and the time within which the notice shall be sent and the authority to which the notice shall be sent under sub-section (1) of section 12;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) the form and manner of the report and the time within which such report shall be sent to the office of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-section (2) of section 12;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) manner of making report by employee under clause (d) and other duties of employees under clause (g) of section 13;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) manner of sending report of action taken under sub-section (3) of section 14;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(n) the manner of constituting a safety committee and the manner and the purpose for choosing the representative of the workers in the Safety Committee under sub-section (1) of section 22;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(o) the qualifications, duties and number of safety officers under sub-section (2) of section 22;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(p) conditions for exemption of workers from weekly and compensatory holidays under sub-section (2) of section 26;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(q) the total number of overtime under second proviso to section 27;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(r) circumstances for exemption from restriction on double employment in factory and mine under section 30;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(s) the form of notice and manner of display of such notice and the manner in which such notice shall be sent to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-section (2) of section 31;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(t) the form of register and particulars of workers under clause (a) of section 33;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(u) the manner and form of displaying notices under clause (b) of section 33;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) return, manner of filing the return and periods of filing return to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator under clause (d) of section 33;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(w) the qualification and experience of Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-section (5) of section 34;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(x) the manner of taking samples of any article or substance found in any premises and air of atmosphere under clause (x) of sub-section (1) of section 35;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(y) the other powers and duties under clause (xiv) of sub-section (1) of section 35;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(z) the specialised qualification and experience, duties and responsibilities of experts to be empanelled under section 37;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(za) the manner of providing alternative employment under sub-clause (d) of clause (A) of sub-section (1) of section 38;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zb) the qualification for the appointment of medical practitioner and other establishment under sub-section (1) of section 42;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zc) other establishment engaged in the dangerous occupation or processes under clause (a) of sub-section (2) of section 42;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zd) medical supervision and other establishment under clause (b) of sub-section (2) of section 42;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ze) other establishment under clause (c) of sub-section (2) of section 42;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zf) conditions relating to safety, holidays and working hours or any other condition to be observed by the employer under section 43;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zg) the manner of requiring the employer to provide the adequate safeguards under section 44;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zh) conditions including, in particular, conditions as to hours of work, fixation of wages and other essential amenities in respect of contract labour under clause (a) of sub-section (3) of section 47;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zi) the form and manner of application and the particulars which such application shall contain regarding the number of contract labour, nature of work for which contract labour is to be employed and other particulars including the information relating to the employment of inter-State migrant workers under sub-section (1) of section 48;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zj) the procedure under sub-section (2) of section 48;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zk) manner of applying for the renewal of licence and the manner of renewal of licence under sub-section (3) of section 48;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zl) responsibility of the contractor under sub-section (4) of section 48;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zm) the manner of intimation of work order and time-limit for such intimation under sub-section (1) of section 50;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zn) the manner of suspending or cancelling the licence under sub-section (2) of section 50;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zo) the period before which the wages shall be paid under sub-section (1) of section 55;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zp) the mode of payment of wages under proviso to sub-section (2) of section 55;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zq) the manner of payment of wages from security deposit under sub-section (4) of section 55;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zr) the form of issuing experience certificate under section 56;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zs) the form and manner of making application under clause (b) of sub-section (2) of section 57;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zt) period of making report and the period of deciding the question under clause (c) of sub-section (2) of section 57;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zu) minimum service for entitlement, class of travel and other matters under section 61;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zv) manner of providing facility of toll free helpline under section 63;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zw) manner of providing for study on inter-State migrant workers under section 64;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zx) authority to whom a copy of the agreement shall be forwarded by the producer under sub-section (3) of section 66;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zy) details under clause (vii) of sub-section (4) of section 66;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zz) rules in respect of factory or class or description of factories under sub-section (1) of section 79;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zza) mode of submission of application under sub-section (2) of section 79;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzb) common facilities and services for joint liability of owner of premises and occupiers of the factories under section 80;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzc) rules under section 82;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzd) purposes under sub-section (1) of section 83;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zze) form of application under sub-section (2) of section 83;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzf) the appellate authority for appeal against the order of Inspector-cum-Facilitator of factory and the manner of appeal under section 90;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzg) rules under section 91;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzh) manner of holding enquiry under sub-section (1) of section 111;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzi) form and manner of preferring appeal and the fee to accompany such appeal under sub-section (3) of section 111;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzj) manner of compounding under sub-section (1) of section 114;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzk) other sources of fund under sub-section (2) of section 115;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzl) the manner of administering and expending the Fund under sub-section (3) of section 115;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzm) the form of application, manner of filing the application and the fee to be accompanied therewith including the information relating to the employment of inter-State migrant workers under sub-section (2) of section 119;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzn) actions, manner of taking actions and inquiry under sub-section (3) of section 119;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzo) the form of appeal, the fee to be accompanied therewith and the appellate authority under sub-section (6) of section 119;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzp) the manner of survey under sub-section (2) of section 121;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzq) any other matter which is required to be, or may be, prescribed under this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.133; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 64<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Rule-making power of the appropriate Government, with a long list of matters (only the opening and closing clauses are summarised here; the full text is reproduced).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>This lists where the detail lives. For the Central sphere, see the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026; State rules are outside scope.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.133. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Power of the appropriate Government to make rules (s.133).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s137\">Section 137 — Prior publication of rules, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s134","title":"Section 134 — Power of Central Government to make rules","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 134 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">134. (1) The Central Government may, subject to the condition of previous publication and by notification, make rules for carrying out the purposes of this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) In particular, and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing power, such rules may provide for all or any of the following matters, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the other authority under sub-clause (iii) of clause (zs) of sub-section (1) of section 2;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the matters which are directly related to the condition of ship under the proviso to sub-clause (iii) of clause (zs) of sub-section (1) of section 2;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) other period under clause (a) of sub-section (1) of section 3;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) the form of certificate of registration, the time within which and the conditions subject to which such certificate shall be issued under sub-section (3) of section 3;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) the form of intimation by the employer electronically and the manner of amendment in the certificate electronically under sub-section (4) of section 3;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) the manner of informing closing of establishment and certifying payment to the registering officer under sub-section (5) of section 3;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) procedure for nomination and discharge of functions of Members of National Board under sub-section (3) of section 16;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) the terms and conditions of service of officers and employees of the National Board under sub-section (4) of section 16;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) the number of members of technical committees or advisory committees and their qualifications under sub-section (5) of section 16;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) the form and manner of collecting, compiling and analyzing occupational safety and health statistics under sub-section (1) of section 21;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) the form and manner of maintaining database electronically or otherwise and the documents to be produced under sub-section (2) of section 21;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) health and working conditions under sub-section (1) of section 23;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) regarding matters specified in sub-section (2) of section 23;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(n) welfare facilities for the employees under sub-section (1) of section 24;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(o) regarding matters specified in sub-section (2) of section 24;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(p) facility of crèche under sub-section (3) of section 24;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(q) definition of &quot;running time&quot; in relation to a working day under clause (a) of the Explanation to sub-section (1) of section 25;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(r) the hours of work for working journalist under sub-section (2) of section 25;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(s) other kinds of leave under clause (i) of sub-section (3) of section 25;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(t) the maximum period of accumulating leave under clause (ii) of sub-section (3) of section 25;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(u) the limit up to which the earned leave may be availed of at a time and the reasons for which such leave may be exceeding under clause (iii) of sub-section (3) of section 25;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) conditions and restrictions for entitlement of cash compensation under clause (iv) of sub-section (3) of section 25;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(w) powers and duties of District Magistrate under section 36;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(x) requisite qualifications or criteria under sub-section (1) of section 47;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(y) period of renewal of licence under sub-section (2) of section 47;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(z) procedure under clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 51;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(za) form of agreement under clause (a), and the name and other particulars under clause (b) of sub-section (2) of section 66;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zb) the matter which may be saved and the qualifications of sole manager under sub-section (1) of section 67;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zc) the conditions relating to number of employees, depth of excavation and other matters under clause (a) of sub-section (1) of section 68;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zd) conditions relating to workings, opencast workings and explosives under clause (b) of sub-section (1) of section 68;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ze) to declare the mines and part thereof for the purpose of applicability of the provisions of this Code under sub-section (2) of section 68;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zf) the authority, the manner of informing such authority and the time limit for making such information under sub-section (3) of section 68;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zg) to provide for medical examination of apprentice, other trainee or employee under sub-section (3) of section 70;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zh) to exempt certain persons or category of persons holding positions of supervision or management and the persons employed in mine and the persons employed therein under section 71;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zi) to provide for vocational training and rescue and recovery services to the persons employed in a mine under section 72;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zj) medical authority under sub-section (2) of section 117;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zk) rules under sub-section (4) of section 121;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zl) the language of the bye-laws under sub-section (7) of section 139;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zm) any other matter which is required to be, or may be prescribed.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.134; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 67<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Rule-making power of the Central Government, with a long list of matters (only the opening and closing clauses are summarised here; the full text is reproduced).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>This lists where the detail lives. For the Central sphere, see the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026; State rules are outside scope.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.134. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Power of the Central Government to make rules (s.134).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s137\">Section 137 — Prior publication of rules, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s135","title":"Section 135 — Power of State Government to make rules","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 135 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">135. (1) The State Government may, subject to the condition of previous publication and by notification, make rules for the carrying out the provisions of this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) In particular and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing power, such rules may provide for all or any of the following matters, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the constitution, procedure and other matters relating to State Advisory Board under sub-section (2) of section 17;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) the number of members and their qualifications under sub-section (3) of section 17;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) the form of application and the payment of fees under sub-section (2) of section 74;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) the manner of preparing the plan of the place or premises under sub-section (3) of section 74;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) other matters under clause (e) of sub-section (4) of section 74;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) fees under sub-section (6) of section 74;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) period under the second proviso to sub-section (6) of section 74;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) the time of filing appeal and fees under section 75;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) the form of application by the employee and conditions under sub-section (1) of section 76;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) form of maintaining the record of the work under sub-section (2) of section 76;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) the manner of disclosing information by occupier of a factory under sub-section (1) of section 84;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) the interval of informing Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator and the local authority about the policy with respect to the health and safety of the workers under sub-section (2) of section 84;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) the form and manner of informing Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator under sub-section (5) of section 84;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(n) the manner of publicising among the workers and the general public living in the vicinity of the factory the measures and disposal laid down under sub-section (7) of section 84;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(o) the conditions for accessibility to the record by the workers under clause (a) of section 85;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(p) the qualification and experience of persons handling hazardous substance and manner of providing necessary facilities for protecting the workers under clause (b) of section 85;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(q) the manner of providing for medical examination of a worker under sub-clause (ii) of clause (c) of section 85;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(r) the measures or standards under sub-section (1) of section 86;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(s) the value of the maximum permissible limit of exposure of chemical and toxic substances in manufacturing process in any factory under section 88;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(t) requiring every employer to make in his plantation provisions in respect of as specified in clauses (a) to (d) of sub-section (1) of section 92;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(u) for prohibiting or, restricting employment of women or adolescents under sub-section (2) of section 93;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) qualifications under sub-section (3) of section 93;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(w) other matters under sub-section (4) of section 93;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(x) manner of periodical medical examination of worker under sub-section (5) of section 93;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(y) the manner of providing facilities, clothing and equipment under sub-section (7) of section 93;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(z) precautionary notices under sub-section (9) of section 93;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(za) any other matter which is required to be, or may be, prescribed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) The Central Government may, by notification and in consultation with the State Government, make rules for the purposes of bringing uniformity, throughout the country, in occupational safety, health or such other matters as it considers necessary in respect of factories.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.135; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 69<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Rule-making power of the State Government, with a long list of matters (only the opening and closing clauses are summarised here; the full text is reproduced).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>This lists where the detail lives. For the Central sphere, see the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026; State rules are outside scope.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.135. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Power of the State Government to make rules (s.135).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s137\">Section 137 — Prior publication of rules, etc.</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s136","title":"Section 136 — Power of Central Government to make regulations in relation to mines and dock work","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 136 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">136. The Central Government may, by notification, make regulations consistent with this Code for all or any of the following purposes, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) for specifying the qualifications required for appointment as Inspector-cum-Facilitator;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) for specifying and regulating the duties and powers of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator and of Inspector-cum-Facilitators in regard to the inspection of mines under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) for specifying the duties of owners, agents and managers of mines and of persons acting under them, and for specifying the qualifications (including age) of agents and managers of mines and of persons acting under them;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) for requiring facilities to be provided for enabling managers of mines and other persons acting under them to efficiently discharge their duties;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) for regulating the manner of ascertaining, by examination or otherwise, the qualifications of managers of mines and persons acting under them, and the granting and renewal of certificates of competency;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) for fixing the fees, if any, to be paid in respect of such examinations and of the grant and renewal of such certificates;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) for determining the circumstances in which and the conditions subject to which it shall be lawful for more mines than one to be under a single manager, or for any mines to be under a manager not having the specified qualifications;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) for providing for inquiries to be made under this Code, including any inquiry relating to misconduct or incompetence on the part of any person holding a certificate under this Code and for the suspension or cancellation of any such certificate and for providing, wherever necessary, that the person appointed to hold an inquiry shall have all the powers of a civil court under the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908, for the purpose of enforcing the attendance of witnesses and compelling the production of documents and material objects;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) for regulating, subject to the provisions of the Indian Explosives Act, 1884, and of any rules made thereunder, the storage, conveyance and use of explosives;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) for prohibiting, restricting or regulating the employment of women in mines or in any class of mines or on particular kinds of labour which are attended by danger to the life, safety or health of such persons and for limiting the weight of any single load that may be carried by any such person;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) for providing for the safety of the persons employed in a mine, their means of entrance there into and exit therefrom, the number of shafts or outlets to be furnished, and the fencing of shafts, pits, outlets, pathways and subsidences;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) for prohibiting the employment in a mine either as manager or in any other specified capacity of any person except persons paid by the owner of the mine and directly answerable to the owner or manager of the mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) for providing for the safety of the roads and working places in mines, including the siting, maintenance and extraction or reduction of pillars or blocks of minerals and the maintenance of sufficient barriers between mine and mine;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(n) for the inspection of workings and sealed off fire- areas in a mine, and for the restriction of workings in the vicinity of the sea or any lake or river or any other body of surface water, whether natural or artificial, or of any public road or building, and for requiring due precaution to be taken against the irruption or inrush of water or other liquid matter into, outbreak of fire in or premature collapse of, any workings;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(o) for providing for the ventilation of mines and the action to be taken in respect of dust, fire, and inflammable and noxious gases, including precautions against spontaneous combustion, underground fire and coal dust;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(p) for regulating, subject to the provisions of the Electricity Act, 2003, and of any rules made thereunder, the generation, storage, transformation, transmission and use of electricity in mines and for providing for the care and the regulation of the use of all electrical apparatus and electrical cables in mines and of all other machinery and plant therein;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(q) &quot;for regulating the use of machinery in mines, for providing for the safety of persons employed on or near such machinery and on haulage roads and for restricting the use of certain classes of locomotives underground;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(r) for providing for proper lighting of mines and regulating the use of safety lamps therein and for the search of persons entering a mine in which safety lamps are in use;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(s) for providing against explosions or ignitions of inflammable gas or dust or irruptions of or accumulations of water in mines and against danger arising therefrom and for prohibiting, restricting or regulating the extraction of minerals in circumstances likely to result in the premature collapse of workings or to result in or to aggravate the collapse of workings or irruptions of water or ignitions in mines;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(t) for specifying type of accidents for the purposes of notice under section 10 and for specifying the notices of accidents and dangerous occurrences, and the notices, reports and returns of mineral output, persons employed and other matters provided for by regulations, to be furnished by owners, agents and managers of mines, and for specifying the forms of such notices, returns and reports, the persons and authorities to whom they are to be furnished, the particulars to be contained in them, and the time within which they are to be submitted;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(u) for requiring owners, agents and managers of mines to have fixed boundaries for the mines, for specifying the plans and sections and field notes connected therewith to be kept by them and the manner and places in which such plans, sections and field notes are to be kept for purposes of record and for the submission of copies thereof to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, and for requiring the making of fresh surveys and plans by them, and in the event of non-compliance, for having the survey made and plans prepared through any other agency and for the recovery of expenses thereof in the same manner as an arrear of land revenue;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(v) for regulating the procedure on the occurrence of accidents or accidental explosions or ignitions in or about, mines; for dealing effectively with the situation;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(w) for specifying the form of, and the particulars to be contained in, the notice to be given by the owner, agent or manager of a mine under section 5;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(x) for specifying the notice to be given by the owner, agent or manager of a mine before mining operations are commenced at or extended to any point within forty-five meters of any railway subject to the provisions of the Indian Railways Act, 1989 or of any public roads or other works as the case may be, which are maintained by the Government or any local authority;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(y) for the protection from injury, in respect of any mine when the workings are discontinued, of property vested in the Government or any local authority or railway company as defined in the Indian Railways Act, 1989;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(z) for requiring protective works to be constructed by the owner, agent or manager of a mine before the mine is closed, and in the event of non-compliance, for getting such works executed by any other agency and for recovering the expenses thereof from such owner in the same manner as an arrear of land revenue;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(za) for requiring the fencing of any mine or part of a mine or any quarry, incline, shaft, pit or outlet, whether the same is being worked or not, or any dangerous or prohibited area, subsidence, haulage, tramline or pathway, where such fencing is necessary for the protection of the public;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zb) for specifying the number of officials to be appointed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zc) for specifying the qualifications of the officials to be appointed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zd) for specifying the qualifications and experience of the agents;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ze) for specifying the period during which the agent shall be resident in India;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zf) for specifying duties and responsibilities of suppliers, designers, importer and contractors for safety in mines;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zg) for requiring the owners, agents and managers of mines to formulate, maintain and enforce safety management plan in their mines;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zh) for requiring the managers of mines to formulate and implement codes of practice or standard operating procedure in respect of any machinery or operation used in the mines;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zi) for providing for the safety in opencast mines and associated operations and machineries used therein;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zj) for regulating the extraction of methane from working or abandoned coal mines or from virgin coal seam;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zk) for specifying the forms of returns which shall be filed by the establishments or the class of establishments under this Code;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zl) for the general requirement relating to the construction, equipping and maintenance for the safety of working places on shore, ship, dock, structure and other places at which any dock work is carried on;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zm) for the safety of any regular approaches over a dock, wharf, quay or other places which dock workers have to use for going for work and for fencing of such places and projects;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zn) for the efficient lighting of all areas of dock, ship, any other vessel, dock structure or working places where any dock work is carried on and of all approaches to such places to which dock workers are required to go in the course of their employment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zo) providing and maintaining adequate ventilation and suitable temperature in every building or an enclosure on ship where dock workers are employed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zp) providing for the fire and explosion prevention and protection;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zq) providing for safe means of access to ships, holds, stagings, equipment, lifting appliances and other working places;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zr) providing for the safety of workers engaged in the opening and closing of hatches, protection of ways and other openings in the docks which may be dangerous to them;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zs) providing for the safety of workers on docks from the risk of falling overboard being struck by cargo during loading or unloading operations;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zt) providing for the construction, maintenance and use of lifting and other cargo handling appliances and services, such as, pallets containing or supporting loads and provision of safety appliances on them, if necessary;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zu) providing for the safety of workers employed in freight container terminals or other terminals for handling unitised cargo;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zv) providing for the fencing of machinery, live electrical conductors, steam pipes and hazardous openings;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zw) providing for the construction, maintenance and use of staging;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zx) providing for the rigging and use of ship&#39;s derricks;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zy) providing for the testing, examination, inspection and certification as appropriate of loose gears including chains and ropes and of slings and other lifting devices used in the dock work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zz) providing for the precautions to be taken to facilitate escape of workers when employed in a hold, bin, hopper or the like or between decks of a hold while handling coal or other bulk cargo;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zza) providing for the measures to be taken in order to prevent dangerous methods of working in the stacking, unstacking, stowing and unstowing of cargo or handling in connection therewith;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzb) providing for the handling of dangerous substances and working in dangerous or harmful environments and the precautions to be taken in connection with such handling;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzc) providing for the work in connection with cleaning, chipping, painting, operations and precautions to be taken in connection with such work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzd) providing for the employment of persons for handling cargo, handling appliances, power operated hatch covers or other power operated ship&#39;s equipment, such as, door in the hull of a ship, ramp, retraceable car deck or similar equipment or to give signals to the drivers of such machinery;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zze) providing for the transport of dock workers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzf) providing for the precautions to be taken to protect dock workers against harmful effects of excessive noise, vibrations and air pollution at the workplace;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzg) providing for protective equipment or protective clothing;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzh) providing for the sanitary, washing and welfare facilities;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzi) providing for—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) the medical supervision;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) the ambulance rooms, first aid and rescue facilities and arrangements for the removal of dock workers to the nearest place of treatment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iii) the safety and health organisation; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(iv) the training of dock workers and for the obligations and rights of the dock workers for their safety and health at the workplace;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzj) providing for the investigation of occupational accidents, dangerous occurrences and diseases, specifying such diseases and the forms of notices, the persons and authorities to whom, they are to be furnished, the particulars to be contained in them and the time within which they are to be submitted;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzk) providing for the submission of statement of accidents, man-days lost, volume of cargo handled and particulars of dock workers; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(zzl) any other matter which is required to be or may be specified by regulation.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.136; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 70<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may make <strong>regulations for mines and dock work</strong> on a long list of matters (inspector qualifications, mine safety, docks and others).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mines and docks: check the regulations in force (not reviewed).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Regulations under this section were not reviewed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.136. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Central Government makes regulations for mines and dock work (s.136).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s138\">Section 138 — Power to make regulation without previous publication</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s137","title":"Section 137 — Prior publication of rules, etc.","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 137 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">137. The power to make rules, regulations, and bye-laws under this Code shall be subject to the condition of the previous publication of the same being made, in the following manner, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) the date to be specified after a draft of rule, regulation, and bye-laws proposed to be made will be taken under consideration, shall not be less than forty-five days from the date on which the draft of the proposed rule, regulation and bye-laws is published for general information;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) rule, regulation and bye-laws shall be published in the Official Gazette and on such publication, shall have effect as if enacted in this Code.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.137; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 74<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Rules, regulations and bye-laws must be published in draft with at least <strong>45 days</strong> before being taken into consideration, and take effect on final publication.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.137. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Drafts are open for at least forty-five days (s.137(a)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s133\">Section 133 — Power of appropriate Government to make rules</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s138","title":"Section 138 — Power to make regulation without previous publication","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 138 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">138. Notwithstanding anything contained in section 137, regulations under section 136 may be made without previous publication and without reference to the National Occupational Safety Health Advisory Board constituted under sub-section (1) of section 16, if the Central Government is satisfied that for the prevention of apprehended danger or the speedy remedy of conditions likely to cause danger and to avoid delay it is necessary to dispense with from such publication and reference.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.138; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 74<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Mines and docks regulations may be made without previous publication, or reference to the National Board, to prevent apprehended danger.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.138. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Urgent regulations may dispense with previous publication (s.138).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s136\">Section 136 — Power of Central Government to make regulations in relation to mines and dock work</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s139","title":"Section 139 — Bye-laws","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 139 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">139. (1) The employer of a mine may, and shall, if called upon to do so by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, frame and submit to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator a draft of such bye-laws, not being inconsistent with this Code or any rules or regulations or standards for the time being in force, governing the use of any particular machinery or the adoption of a particular method of working in the mine, as the employer may deem necessary to prevent accidents and provide for the safety, convenience and discipline of the persons employed in the mine.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) If any such employer—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) fails to submit within two months a draft of bye-laws after being called upon to do so by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) submits a draft of bye-laws which is not in the opinion of the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator sufficient, the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator may—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(i) propose a draft of such bye-laws as appear to him to be sufficient; or</p><p class=\"hr-st-l2\">(ii) propose such amendments in any draft submitted to him by the employer as will, in his opinion, render it sufficient, and shall send such draft bye-laws or draft amendments to the employer for consideration.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) If within a period of two months from the date on which any draft bye-laws or draft amendments are sent by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator to the employer under the provisions of sub-section (2), the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator and the employer are unable to agree as to the terms of the bye-laws to be made under sub-section (1), the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator shall refer the draft bye-laws for settlement to the technical committee constituted under sub-section (5) of section 16 in respect of mines.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) When such draft bye-laws have been agreed to by the employer and the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator, or, when they are unable to agree, have been settled by the technical Committee constituted under sub-section (5) of section 16 in respect of mines, a copy of the draft bye-laws shall be sent by the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Inspector-cum-Facilitator to the Central Government for approval:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided that the Central Government may make such modification of the draft bye-laws as it thinks fit:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">Provided further that before the Central Government approves the draft bye-laws, whether with or without modifications, there shall be published, in such manner as the Central Government may think best adapted for informing the persons affected, a notice of the proposal to make the bye-laws and of the place where copies of the draft bye-laws may be obtained, and of the time (which shall not be less than thirty days) within which any objections with reference to the draft bye-laws, made by or on behalf of persons affected should be sent to the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(5) Every objection under second proviso to sub-section (4) shall be in writing and shall state—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) the specific grounds of objections, and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(ii) the omissions, additions or modifications asked for.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(6) The Central Government shall consider any objection made within the required time by or on behalf of persons appearing to it to be affected, and may approve the bye-laws either in the form in which they were published or after making such amendments thereto as it thinks fit.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(7) The employer shall cause a copy of the bye-laws, in English and in such other language or languages as may be prescribed by the Central Government, to be pasted up in some conspicuous place at or near the mine, where the bye-laws may be conveniently read or seen by the persons employed; and, as and when the same become defaced, obliterated or destroyed, shall cause them to be pasted again.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(8) The Central Government may, by order in writing rescind, in whole or in part, any bye-law so made, and thereupon such bye-law shall cease to have effect accordingly.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.139; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 74<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>A mine employer may (and, if called on, must within two months) frame <strong>bye-laws</strong> on machinery use and methods of working for safety and discipline. Approved bye-laws must be posted in English and prescribed languages near the mine.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Mines only.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.139. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Mine employers frame bye-laws when called upon, within two months (s.139(1)–(2)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s136\">Section 136 — Power of Central Government to make regulations in relation to mines and dock work</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s140","title":"Section 140 — Powers to regulate general safety and health","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 140 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">140. Notwithstanding any law for the time being in force, the Central Government may make rules to regulate general safety and health of the persons residing in whole or part of India, in the event of declaration of an epidemic, pandemic or disaster, for such period as may be notified by the Central Government.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.140; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 76<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>The Central Government may make rules on general safety and health of persons in India during a declared epidemic, pandemic or disaster.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Watch for emergency safety rules.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.140. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central rules on general safety and health during epidemics or disasters (s.140).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s128\">Section 128 — Power to exempt during public emergency</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s141","title":"Section 141 — Laying of regulations, rules, bye-laws, etc., before Parliament","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 141 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">141. Every rule, regulation, standard and bye-laws notified or made by the Central Government under this Code shall be laid, as soon as may be after it is notified or made,</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">before each House of Parliament, while it is in session, for a total period of thirty days which may be comprised in one session or in two or more successive sessions, and if, before the expiry of the session immediately following the session or the successive sessions aforesaid, both Houses agree in making any modification in the rule, regulation, standard or bye-law or both Houses agree that the rule, regulation, standard or bye-law should not be made, the rule, regulation, standard or bye-law shall thereafter have effect only in such modified form or be of no effect, as the case may be; so, however, that any such modification or annulment shall be without prejudice to the validity of anything previously done under that rule, regulation, standard or bye-law, as the case may be.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.141; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 76<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Central rules, regulations, standards and bye-laws are laid before Parliament for 30 days.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.141. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Central instruments are laid before Parliament (s.141).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s142\">Section 142 — Laying of rules made by State Government</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s142","title":"Section 142 — Laying of rules made by State Government","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 142 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">142. Every rule made by the State Government under this Code shall be laid, as soon as may be, after it is made, before the State Legislature.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.142; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 76<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>State rules are laid before the State Legislature.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Administrative provision; no direct employer duty.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.142. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">State rules are laid before the State Legislature (s.142).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s141\">Section 141 — Laying of regulations, rules, bye-laws, etc., before Parliament</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.s143","title":"Section 143 — Repeal and Savings","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">Section 143 · Chapter XIV <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">143. (1) The following enactments shall stand repealed on and from the dates the notification referred to in sub-section (2) of section 1 is issued, namely:—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) The Factories Act, 1948;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) The Plantations Labour Act, 1951;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) The Mines Act, 1952;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) The Working Journalists and other Newspaper Employees (Conditions of Service) and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1955;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) The Working Journalists (Fixation of Rates of Wages) Act, 1958;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) The Motor Transport Workers Act, 1961;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) The Beedi and Cigar Workers (Conditions of Employment) Act, 1966;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) The Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) The Sales Promotion Employees (Conditions of Service) Act, 1976;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) The Inter-State Migrant Workmen (Regulation of Employment and Conditions of Service) Act, 1979;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) The Cine-Workers and Cinema Theatre Workers (Regulation of Employment) Act, 1981;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) The Dock Workers (Safety, Health and Welfare) Act, 1986;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(m) The Building and Other Construction Workers (Regulation of Employment and Conditions of Service) Act, 1996.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(2) Every Chief Inspector, Additional Chief Inspector, Joint Chief Inspector, Deputy Chief Inspector, Inspector and every other officer appointed for the purposes under any of the provisions of the enactments repealed by this Code, shall be deemed to have been appointed under this Code for such purposes under this Code.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(3) Notwithstanding repeal under sub-section (1), anything done or any action taken under the enactments so repealed (including any rule, regulation, bye-laws, notification, nomination, appointment, order or direction made thereunder) shall be deemed to have been done or taken under the corresponding provisions of this Code and shall remain in force to the extent they are not contrary to the provisions of this Code till they are repealed by the Central Government.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">(4) Without prejudice to the provisions of sub-section (2), provisions of section 6 of the General Clauses Act, 1897 shall apply to the repeal of such enactments.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.143; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 76<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p><strong>Repeal.</strong> Thirteen Acts are repealed:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>the Factories Act, 1948;</li><li>the Plantations Labour Act, 1951;</li><li>the Mines Act, 1952;</li><li>the Working Journalists Acts, 1955 and 1958;</li><li>the Motor Transport Workers Act, 1961;</li><li>the Beedi and Cigar Workers Act, 1966;</li><li>the <strong>Contract Labour (R&amp;A) Act, 1970</strong>;</li><li>the Sales Promotion Employees Act, 1976;</li><li>the Inter-State Migrant Workmen Act, 1979;</li><li>the Cine-Workers Act, 1981;</li><li>the Dock Workers Act, 1986;</li><li>the BOCW (RE&amp;CS) Act, 1996.</li></ul><p><strong>Savings.</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Inspectors under those Acts are deemed appointed under the Code.</li><li>Things done under them (rules, regulations, notifications, orders) continue so far as not contrary to the Code, until repealed by the Central Government.</li><li>Section 6 of the General Clauses Act applies.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Existing rules, notifications and licences under the old Acts continue so far as consistent. Pre-Code licences run until expiry (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\" data-ref=\"osh.s119\">s.119(5)</a>).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The repeal takes effect &#39;on and from the dates the notification referred to in sub-section (2) of section 1 is issued&#39;. S.O. 5321(E) brought the whole Code into force from 21 November 2025.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.143. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Thirteen Acts, including the Factories Act, 1948 and the CLRA Act, 1970, are repealed (s.143(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Things done under the repealed Acts continue so far as not contrary to the Code (s.143(3)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s1\">Section 1 — Short title, commencement and application</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s132\">Section 132 — Power to remove difficulties</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.sections.ch-sch","title":"Schedules","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.sch1","title":"The First Schedule — List of industries involving hazardous processes","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">The First Schedule <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">[See section 2(za)]</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">List of Industries involving hazardous processes:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">1. Ferrous Metallurgical Industries</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Integrated Iron and Steel</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Ferro-alloys</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Special Steels.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">2. Non-ferrous metallurgical Industries</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Primary Metallurgical Industries, namely, zinc, lead, copper, manganese and aluminium.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">3. Foundries (ferrous and non-ferrous)</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Castings and forgings including cleaning or smoothening/roughening by sand and shot blasting.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">4. Coal (including coke) industries</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Coal, Lignite, Coke and like other substances</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Fuel Gases (including Coal Gas, Producer Gas, Water Gas).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">5. Power Generating Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">6. Pulp and paper (including paper products) industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">7. Fertiliser Industries</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Nitrogenous</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Phosphatic</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Mixed.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">8. Cement Industries</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Portland Cement (including slag cement, puzzolona cement and their products).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">9. Petroleum Industries</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Oil Refining</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Lubricating Oils and Greases.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">10. Petro-chemical Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">11. Drugs and Pharmaceutical Industries</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">— Narcotics, Drugs and Pharmaceuticals.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">12. Fermentation Industries (Distilleries and Breweries).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">13. Rubber (Synthetic) Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">14. Paints and Pigment Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">15. Leather Tanning Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">16. Electro-plating Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">17. Chemical Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) Coke Oven by-products and Coaltar Distillation products:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) Industrial Gases (nitrogen, oxygen, acetylene, argon, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, sulphur dioxide, nitrous oxide, halogenated hydrocarbon, ozone, or any like gases);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(c) Industrial Carbon;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(d) Alkalies and Acids;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(e) Chromates and dichromates;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(f) Lead and its compounds;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(g) Electrochemicals (metallic sodium, potassium and magnesium, chlorates, perchlorates and peroxides);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(h) Electrothermal produces (artificial abrasive, calcium carbide);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(i) Nitrogenous compounds (cyanides, cyanamides and other nitrogenous compounds);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(j) Phosphorous and its compounds;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(k) Halogens and Halogenated compounds (Chlorine, Fluorine, Bromine and Iodine);</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(l) Explosives (including industrial explosives and detonators and fuses).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">18. Insecticides, Fungicides, Herbicides and other Pesticides Industries.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">19. Synthetic Resin and plastics.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">20. Man made Fibre (Cellulosic and non-cellulosic) industry.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">21. Manufacture and repair of electrical accumulators.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">22. Glass and Ceramics.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">23. Grinding or glazing of metals.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">24. Manufacture, handling and processing of asbestos and its products.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">25. Extraction of oils and fats from vegetable and animal sources.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">26. Manufacture, handling and use of benzene and substances containing benzene.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">27. Manufacturing processes and operations involving carbon disulphide.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">28. Dyes and Dyestuff including their intermediates.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">29. Highly flammable liquids and gases.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">30. Printing and dyeing on fabrics in textiles and plywood and laminate manufacturing process.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">31. Process involving usage of radium or Radioactive Substances.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">32. Stone Crushing industry.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">33. Extraction of Oil and Raw material from the scrap tyres.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">34. Cigarette manufacturing industry.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">35. Ship breaking industry.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">36. Hazardous waste and e-waste processing plants.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">37. Semiconductor manufacturing industry.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">38. Styrene manufacturing, handling and processing industry.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">39. Nano-particles utilising industry.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">40. Manufacturing, processing, preparation and utilisation of Mercury or Compounds of Mercury, Lead Tetra-ethyl, Manganese, Arsenic, Chrome, Aliphatic series, Beryllium, Phosgene and Isocyanates.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, Schedule 1; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 78<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Lists <strong>40 industries involving hazardous processes</strong> (s.2(1)(za)), including ferrous and non-ferrous metallurgy, foundries, coal and coke, power generation, pulp and paper, fertilisers, cement, petroleum and petrochemicals, pharmaceuticals, fermentation, rubber, paints, tanning, electroplating, chemicals (with sub-categories), pesticides, plastics, glass, asbestos, benzene, dyes, flammable substances, radioactive substances, stone crushing, ship breaking, e-waste, semiconductors, nanoparticles and mercury and lead compounds.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Check whether your process falls in the list: hazardous-process duties under ss.82–89 and the s.102 penalties follow.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Item 17 sub-clause labels are spaced irregularly in the print (&#39;( f )&#39;, &#39;( j)&#39;); normalised to &#39;(f)&#39;, &#39;(j)&#39; (the transcription check is unaffected).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.Sch1. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The First Schedule lists industries involving hazardous processes (First Schedule; s.2(1)(za)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\">Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s85\">Section 85 — Specific responsibility of the occupier in relation to hazardous processes</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s131\">Section 131 — Power of Central Government to amend Schedule</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.sch2","title":"The Second Schedule — Matters for occupational safety and health standards","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">The Second Schedule <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">[See section 18(2)(f)]</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">List of matters:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(1) fencing of machinery;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(2) work on or near machinery in motion;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(3) employment of adolescents on dangerous machines;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(4) striking gear and devices for cutting off power;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(5) self acting machines;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(6) casing of new machinery;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(7) prohibition of employment of women, children and adolescent near cotton openers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(8) hoists and lifts;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(9) lifting machines, chains, ropes and lifting tackles;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(10) revolving machinery;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(11) pressure plant;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(12) floors, stairs and means of access;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(13) pits, sumps, openings in floors and other similar indentation of area;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(14) safety officers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(15) protection of eyes;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(16) precautions against dangerous fumes, gases, etc.;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(17) precautions regarding the use of portable electric light;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(18) explosive or inflammable dust, gas, and other like dusts or gases;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(19) safety committee;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(20) power to require specifications of defective parts or tests of stability;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(21) safety of buildings and machinery;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(22) maintenance of buildings;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(23) prohibition in certain cases of danger;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(24) notice in respect of accidents;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(25) court of inquiry in case of accidents;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(26) safety management in plantation;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(27) the general requirement relating to the construction, equipments and maintenance for the safety of working places on shore, ship, dock, structure and other places at which any dock work is carried on;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(28) the safety of any regular approaches over a dock, wharf, quay or other places which dock worker have to use for going for work and for fencing of such places and projects;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(29) the efficient lighting of all areas of dock, ship, any other vessel, dock structure or working places where any dock work is carried on and of all approaches to such places to which dock workers are required to go in the course of their employment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(30) adequate ventilation and suitable temperature in every building or an enclosure on ship where dock workers are employed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(31) the fire and explosion preventions and protection;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(32) safe means of access to ships, holds, stagings, equipment, appliances and other working places;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(33) the construction, maintenance and use of lifting and other cargo handling appliances and services, such as, pallets containing or supporting loads and provision of safety appliances on them, if necessary;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(34) the safety of workers employed in freight container terminals of other terminals for handing unitized cargo;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(35) the fencing of machinery, live electrical conductors, steam pipes and hazardous openings;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(36) the construction, maintenance and use of staging;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(37) the rigging and use of ship&#39;s derricks;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(38) the testing, examination, inspection and certification as appropriate of&#39; loose gears including chains and ropes and of slings and other lifting devices used in the dock work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(39) the precautions to be taken to facilitate escape of workers when employed in a hold, bin, hopper or the like or between decks of a hold while handing coal of other bulk cargo;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(40) the measures to be taken in order to prevent dangerous methods of&#39; working in the stacking, unstacking, stowing and unstowing of cargo or handling in connection therewith;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(41) the handling of dangerous substances and working, in dangerous or harmful environments and the precautions to be taken in connection with such handling;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(42) the work in connection with cleaning, chipping, painting, operations and precautions to be taken in connection with such work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(43) the employment of persons for handling cargo, handling appliances, power operated batch covers or other power operated ship&#39;s equipment such as, door in the hull of a ship, ramp, retraceable car deck or similar equipment or to give signals to the drivers of such machinery;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(44) the transport of dock workers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(45) the precautions to be taken to protect dock workers against harmful effects of excessive noise, vibration and air pollution at the work place;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(46) protective equipment and protective clothing;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(47) the sanitary, washing and welfare facilities;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(48) the medical supervision;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(49) the ambulance rooms, first aid and rescue facilities and arrangements for the removal of dock workers to the nearest place of treatment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(50) the investigation of occupational accidents, dangerous occurrences and diseases, specifying such diseases and the forms of&#39; notices, the persons and authorities to whom, they are to be furnished, the particulars to be contained in them and the time within which they are to be submitted;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(51) the submission of statement of accidents, man-days lost, volume of cargo handled and particulars of dock workers.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(52) the safe means of access to, and the safety of, any working place, including the provision of suitable and sufficient scaffolding at various stages when work cannot be safely done from the ground or from any part of a building or from a ladder or such other means of support;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(53) the precautions to be taken in connection with the demolition of the whole or any substantial part of a building or other structure under the supervision of a competent person for the avoidance of danger from collapse of any building or other structure while removing any part of the framed building or other structure by shoring or otherwise;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(54) the handling or use of explosive under the control of competent persons so that there is no exposure to the risk of injury from explosion or from flying material;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(55) the erection installation, use and maintenance of transporting equipment, such as locomotives, trucks, wagons and other vehicles and trailers and appointment of competent persons to drive or operate such equipment;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(56) the erection, installation, use and maintenance of hoists, lifting appliances and lifting gear including periodical testing and examination and heat treatment where necessary, precautions to be taken while raising or lowering loads, restrictions on carriage of persons and appointment of competent persons on hoists or other lifting appliances;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(57) the adequate and suitable lighting of every workplace and approach thereto, of every place where raising or lowering operations with the use of hoists, lifting appliances or lifting gears are in progress and of all openings dangerous to building workers employed;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(58) the precautions to be taken to prevent inhalation of dust, fumes, gases or vapours during any grinding, cleaning, spraying or manipulation of any material and steps to be taken to secure and maintain adequate ventilation of every working place or confined space;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(59) the measures to be taken during stacking or unstacking, stowing or unstowing of materials or goods or handling in connection therewith;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(60) the safeguarding of machinery including the fencing of every fly-wheel and every moving part of prime mover and every part of transmission or other machinery, unless it is in such a position or of such construction as to be safe to every worker working only of the operations and as if it were securely fenced;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(61) the safe handling and use of plant, including tools and equipment operated by compressed air;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(62) the precaution to be taken in case of fire;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(63) the limits of weight to be lifted or moved by workers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(64) the safe transport of workers to or from any workplace by water and provision of means for rescue from drowning;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(65) the steps to be taken to prevent danger to workers from live electric wires or apparatus including electrical machinery and tools and from overhead wires;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(66) the keeping of safety nets, safety sheets and safety belts where the special nature or the circumstances of work render them necessary for the safety of the workers;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(67) the standards to be complied with regard to scaffolding, ladders and stairs, lifting appliances, ropes, chains and accessories, earth moving equipment and floating operational equipments;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(68) the precautions to be taken with regard to pile driving, concrete work, work with hot asphalt, tar or other similar things, insulation work, demolition operations, excavation, underground construction and handling materials;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(69) the safety policy, that is to say, a policy relating to steps to be taken to ensure the safety and health of the building workers, the administrative arrangements therefore and the matters connected therewith, to be framed by the employers and contractors for tile operations to be carried on in a building or other construction work;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(70) emergency standards for enforcement of suitable standards in respect of hazardous processes in a factory;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(71) the maximum permissible threshold limits of exposure of chemical and toxic substances in manufacturing processes (whether hazardous or otherwise) in any factory;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(72) lightning; and</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(73) any other matter which the Central Government considers under the circumstance for better working condition for safety at the workplace.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, Schedule 2; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 81<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Lists <strong>73 matters</strong> that occupational safety and health standards under s.18(2)(f) cover: machinery guarding, lifts and lifting gear, pressure plant, floors and openings, safety officers and committees, dangerous fumes and dust, building and machinery safety, accident notices and inquiries, plantation safety, extensive dock-work safety matters, construction safety (scaffolding, excavation, demolition, explosives, hoists, lighting, fire, weight limits, electrical safety), safety policy, emergency standards, exposure limits and lightning.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>The Second Schedule maps the topics on which standards may be declared; check notified standards for each.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.Sch2. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">Standards under s.18 relate to the matters in the Second Schedule (Second Schedule; s.18(2)(f)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\">Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s131\">Section 131 — Power of Central Government to amend Schedule</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"osh.sch3","title":"The Third Schedule — List of notifiable diseases","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":false,"aliases":[],"html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\">The Third Schedule <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In force from 2025-11-21</span> </div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Statutory text</h4><div class=\"hr-statute\"><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">[See section 12(1)]</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">List of Notifiable Diseases:</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">1. Lead poisoning, including poisoning by any preparation or compound of lead or their sequelae.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">2. Lead-tetra-ethyle poisoning.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">3. Phosphorus poisoning or its sequelae.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">4. Mercury poisoning or its sequelae.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">5. Manganese poisoning or its sequelae.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">6. Arsenic poisoning or its sequelae.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">7. Poisoning by nitrous fumes.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">8. Carbon bisulphide poisoning.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">9. Benzene poisoning, including poisoning by any of its homologues, their nitro or amido derivatives or its sequelae.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">10. Chrome ulceration or its sequelae.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">11. Anthrax.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">12. Silicosis.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">13. Poisoning by halogens or halogen derivatives of the hydrocarbons of the aliphatic series.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">14. Pathological manifestations due to—</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(a) radium or other radio-active substances;</p><p class=\"hr-st-l1\">(b) X-rays.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">15. Primary epitheliomatous cancer of the skin.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">16. Toxic anaemia.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">17. Toxic jaundice due to poisonous substances.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">18. Oil acne or dermatitis due to mineral oils and compounds containing mineral oil</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">base.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">19. Byssionosis.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">20. Asbestosis.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">21. Occupational or contact dermatitis caused by direct contact with chemicals and paints. These are of two types, that is, primary irritants and allergic sensitizers.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">22. Noise induced hearing loss (exposure to high noise levels).</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">23. Beriyllium poisoning.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">24. Carbon monoxide poisoning.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">25. Coal miners&#39; pneumoconiosis.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">26. Phosgene poisoning.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">27. Occupational cancer.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">28. Isocyanates poisoning.</p><p class=\"hr-st-l0\">29. Toxic nephritis.</p></div><div class=\"hr-cite\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, Schedule 3; Gazette of India Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, p. 85<br><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Transcription check: exact match with project Gazette copy (automated)</span>  <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Amendments check: Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">India Code consolidated copy &quot;as on 28 November 2025&quot; could be read only up to s.37 by the web-fetch tool, so amendment footnotes for this provision were not seen. No amending Act has been identified. Human sign-off pending.</div></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">What it means</h4><p>Lists <strong>29 notifiable diseases</strong> (s.12): lead, phosphorus, mercury, manganese, arsenic, benzene and other poisonings; chrome ulceration; anthrax; silicosis; radiation effects; skin cancer; toxic anaemia and jaundice; oil acne; byssinosis; asbestosis; contact dermatitis; noise-induced hearing loss; beryllium and carbon monoxide poisoning; coal miners&#39; pneumoconiosis; phosgene poisoning; occupational cancer; isocyanate poisoning; toxic nephritis.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Practical implications</h4><p>Notify any listed disease (Central sphere: forthwith, rule 8). Medical practitioners must also report.</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Qualifications and exceptions</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Spellings such as &#39;Lead-tetra-ethyle&#39;, &#39;Byssionosis&#39; and &#39;Beriyllium&#39; are reproduced as printed.</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Rules</h4><p>No Central Rule deals specifically with s.Sch3. <span class=\"hr-muted\">(searched: The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E)))</span></p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Basis of key statements</h4><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Basis</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">The Third Schedule lists twenty-nine notifiable diseases (Third Schedule; s.12(1)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Statute</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Statement\">In force from 21 November 2025 (S.O. 5321(E)).</td><td data-label=\"Basis\">Notification</td><td data-label=\"Source\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-commencement\">S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (S.O. 5321(E))</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s12\">Section 12 — Notice of certain diseases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s121\">Section 121 — Power of appropriate Government to direct inquiry in certain cases</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s131\">Section 131 — Power of Central Government to amend Schedule</a></li></ul>"}]}],"hrGuide":[{"id":"osh.gen.guide.applicability","title":"1. Who and what the Code covers","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.applicability","title":"Who and what the Code covers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.applicability"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">The Code applies to &#39;establishments&#39; (generally 10 or more workers), with extra regimes for factories, mines, docks, building work, plantations, contract labour and migrant workers. The State is the appropriate Government for all factories.</p><p><strong>Establishment</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(v)</a>). Any place of business with <strong>10 or more workers</strong>, including motor transport, newspapers, audio-visual production, building work and plantations; every mine and dock. Notified hazardous activities are covered at any headcount.</p><p><strong>Factory</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(w)</a>). 20 or more workers with power, or 40 or more without; earlier State thresholds continue until amended (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.factory-definition\" data-ref=\"osh.cmp.factory-definition\">Meaning of &#39;factory&#39;</a>).</p><p><strong>Worker vs employee.</strong> Most duties protect &#39;workers&#39; (excluding managers and supervisors earning above ₹18,000). Appointment letters and some rights extend to all &#39;employees&#39;.</p><p><strong>Appropriate Government</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(d)</a>). The Centre for its own undertakings, central PSUs, railways, mines, oil fields, major ports, air transport, telecom, banking and insurance, and their contractors; the <strong>State for every factory</strong> and everything else. Health and welfare rules under ss.23–24 are made by the Central Government.</p><p><strong>Registration</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\" data-ref=\"osh.s3\">Section 3</a>). One electronic registration within 60 days (deemed if not processed). Existing registrations count, with particulars updated (Central sphere: within six months of the Rules).</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Does the OSH Code apply, and which parts?\"><figcaption><strong>Does the OSH Code apply, and which parts?</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Headcounts are &#39;workers&#39; unless stated. Check State thresholds saved by the proviso to s.2(1)(w).</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">1. Government office?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Code does not apply (except contract labour where the Government is principal employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.1(3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">2. 10 or more workers (or mine, dock, notified hazardous activity)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">An &#39;establishment&#39;: registration, employer duties, health, welfare, hours, leave, registers</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.2(1)(v); ss.3, 6, 23–33</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">3. 20+ workers with power / 40+ without</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">A &#39;factory&#39;: Part VII and factory-specific rules (State rules)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.2(1)(w); ss.79–91</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">4. 50+ contract labour</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Part I (contract labour): contractor licences, core-activity bar</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.45</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">5. 10+ inter-State migrant workers</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Part II: journey fare, both-State accident reporting</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.59</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">6. Sector Parts</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Mines (Part IV), beedi and cigar (Part V), building work (Part VI), plantations (Part VIII), audio-visual workers (Part III)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">Chapter XI</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">7. Whose rules?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Central Government for central undertakings, mines, major ports and listed sectors; State for factories and the rest; ss.23–24 rules are made by the Centre</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.2(1)(d); ss.23–24</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.1, 2, 45, 59</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s1\">Section 1 — Short title, commencement and application</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\">Section 3 — Registration of certain establishments</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.employer-duties","title":"2. Core employer duties","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.employer-duties","title":"Core employer duties","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.appointment-letter"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">A general duty to keep the workplace free of hazards, plus specific duties: appointment letters, free health check-ups as prescribed, no charge for safety, waste disposal, notices of accidents and diseases.</p><p><strong>General duties</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">Section 6</a>). A hazard-free workplace; compliance with standards; a safe working environment; safe disposal of hazardous waste and e-waste; no charge to employees for safety measures. In factories, mines, docks, building work and plantations, responsibility extends to everyone on the premises.</p><p><strong>Appointment letters</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(f)</a>). For every employee; existing staff by about 21 February 2026. Central sphere format in rule 6.</p><p><strong>Health examinations</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(c)</a>). Free annual examination as prescribed. Central sphere: dock and building workers aged 40+ (rule 5). Hazardous-process workers: before assignment and at least every 12 months (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s85\" data-ref=\"osh.s85\">Section 85</a>).</p><p><strong>Other duty-holders.</strong> Mine owners and agents (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s7\" data-ref=\"osh.s7\">Section 7</a>); designers, manufacturers, importers and suppliers (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s8\" data-ref=\"osh.s8\">Section 8</a>); architects and project engineers (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s9\" data-ref=\"osh.s9\">Section 9</a>).</p><p><strong>Employees</strong> must take care, comply and report hazards (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s13\" data-ref=\"osh.s13\">Section 13</a>), and may report imminent danger to the inspector (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s14\" data-ref=\"osh.s14\">Section 14</a>).</p><p><strong>Defence.</strong> In a prosecution, the accused must show that more was not reasonably practicable (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s118\" data-ref=\"osh.s118\">Section 118</a>); keep risk assessments.</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Appointment letter: what it must contain (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Appointment letter: what it must contain (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rule 6 format. The duty in s.6(1)(f) applies to every establishment; the format is prescribed by the appropriate Government.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Identity</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Name; date of birth; father&#39;s or mother&#39;s name; Aadhaar number (with consent)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (i)–(iv)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employer</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Labour Identification Number of the establishment</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (v)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Social security IDs</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">UAN and/or ESI insurance number (if available)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (vi)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Job</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Designation; type of employment (regular, fixed term, contractual); skill category; date of joining; broad duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (vii)–(x), (xiv)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Pay</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Wages/basic pay and DA; other allowances including accommodation</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (xi)–(xii)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Benefits</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">EPF and ESI applicability; maternity benefits (women employees)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 6 (xiii), (xv)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Deadline for existing staff</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Within three months of commencement (about 21 February 2026)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.6(1)(f)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.6(1)(f); <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 6</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s8\">Section 8 — Duties of designers, manufacturers, importers or suppliers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s13\">Section 13 — Duties of employee</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s14\">Section 14 — Rights of employee</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s118\">Section 118 — Onus of proving limits of what is practicable, etc.</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.safety-systems","title":"3. Safety committees, safety officers, standards and incident reporting","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.safety-systems","title":"Safety committees, safety officers, standards and incident reporting","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.incident-reporting"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">Standards come from the Central Government; safety committees and officers depend on headcount; accidents, dangerous occurrences and notifiable diseases must be reported within tight time limits.</p><p><strong>Standards</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\" data-ref=\"osh.s18\">Section 18</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch2\" data-ref=\"osh.sch2\">Second Schedule</a>). Declared by the Central Government for factories, mines, docks, beedi, building work and others; States may amend with Central approval.</p><p><strong>Safety Committee</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\" data-ref=\"osh.s22\">Section 22</a>). On Government order. Central sphere: mandatory at 500+ workers; meets quarterly; recommendations acted on in 15 days (rule 14).</p><p><strong>Safety officers</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\" data-ref=\"osh.s22\">s.22(2)</a>). Factories 500+ (250+ hazardous), building work 250+, mines 100+. Central sphere scales for docks, building work and mines (rules 18–21).</p><p><strong>Incident reporting</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\" data-ref=\"osh.s10\">Section 10</a>–<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s12\" data-ref=\"osh.s12\">Section 12</a>; see the reporting clock).</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Death: forthwith, by Form XI and phone.</li><li>48-hour injuries: within 12 hours after the 48 hours.</li><li>Dangerous occurrences: within 12 hours.</li><li>Third Schedule diseases: forthwith.</li></ul><p><strong>Hazardous processes</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s82\" data-ref=\"osh.s82\">Section 82</a>–<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s89\" data-ref=\"osh.s89\">Section 89</a>; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch1\" data-ref=\"osh.sch1\">First Schedule</a>). Disclosure, safety policy, on-site emergency plan, 30 days&#39; notice of new processes, health records and examinations.</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rules 7–8. Also report to the competent authority under the SS Code within 7 days where applicable (SS s.73).</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-timeline\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: Form XI to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator; phone and electronic intimation to the Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator, District Magistrate/SDO, police station and family</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Dangerous occurrence (listed)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Within 12 hours: intimation to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator and District Magistrate/SDO, injury or not</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(3)–(4)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Injury, 48 hours off work</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Form XI electronically within 12 hours after the 48 hours are complete</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 7(2)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Later death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith by phone and electronically; written confirmation within 12 hours of the death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">proviso to rule 7(3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Third Schedule disease</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Forthwith: notice electronically or by speed post to the Inspector-cum-Facilitator or Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 8(1)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.10–12; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 7–8</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s18\">Section 18 — Occupational safety and health standards</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\">Section 22 — Safety Committee and safety officers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\">Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s85\">Section 85 — Specific responsibility of the occupier in relation to hazardous processes</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.health-welfare","title":"4. Health, working conditions and welfare facilities","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.health-welfare","title":"Health, working conditions and welfare facilities","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.welfare-thresholds"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">The Central Government prescribes health, working-condition and welfare standards. Facility thresholds: crèche above 50 workers, canteen at 100, welfare officer at 250, ambulance room above 500.</p><p><strong>Health and working conditions</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\" data-ref=\"osh.s23\">Section 23</a>). Cleanliness, ventilation, dust control, drinking water, space, lighting, separate toilets for men, women and transgender employees, waste treatment (Central Rules 22–48, by sector).</p><p><strong>Welfare</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>). Washing, bathing and lockers (separately for men, women and transgender employees), seating, first aid; and by headcount (see the threshold chart):</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>crèche: more than 50 workers (rule 58), with detailed child-safety standards;</li><li>lunchroom and rest room: more than 50 (factories and mines);</li><li>canteen: 100 or more including contract labour;</li><li>welfare officer: 250 or more (factories, mines, plantations);</li><li>ambulance room: more than 500.</li></ul><p><strong>Mock drills</strong> quarterly (annually in plantations) (rule 59).</p><p><strong>Contract labour.</strong> The principal employer provides these facilities to contract labour (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\" data-ref=\"osh.s53\">Section 53</a>).</p><p><strong>Who prescribes?</strong> The Central Government, even where a State is the appropriate Government (an interpretation, supported for docks by the Ministry&#39;s FAQ).</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Welfare and safety staffing thresholds\"><figcaption><strong>Welfare and safety staffing thresholds</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Counts are workers &#39;ordinarily employed&#39; (s.2(1)(zv)) unless stated. Central Rules figures apply to the establishment types the rule names.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">More than 50 workers</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Crèche (rule 58); lunchroom and rest room in factories and mines (rule 56)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(2)(iii), (3)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">100 or more workers (including contract labour)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Canteen</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(1)(v); rule 53</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">100 or more (mine)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Safety officer (one per 500)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(d); rule 20</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">250 or more</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Welfare officer (factories, mines, plantations); safety officer in hazardous-process factories and building work</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(b)–(c); s.24(2)(iv); rules 18, 57</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">500 or more</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Safety officer (factories, docks); Safety Committee (Central Rules)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.22(2)(a); rules 14, 18</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">More than 500</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Ambulance room (factories, mines, building work)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.24(2)(i); rule 55</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.22, 24; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 14–20, 53–58</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\">Section 53 — Liability of principal employer for welfare facilities</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s92\">Section 92 — Facilities for workers in plantation</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.hours-leave","title":"5. Working hours, overtime and leave","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.hours-leave","title":"Working hours, overtime and leave","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.annual-leave"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">8 hours a day, 6 days a week (48 hours a week in the Central Rules); overtime at twice the rate of wages with consent; annual leave after 180 days at 1 day per 20.</p><p><strong>Hours</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\" data-ref=\"osh.s25\">Section 25</a>). Maximum 8 hours a day, intervals and spread-over as notified; Central sphere: 48 hours a week (rule 64). Special rules for mines, motor transport and working journalists.</p><p><strong>Weekly holiday</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s26\" data-ref=\"osh.s26\">Section 26</a>). No more than six days a week; compensatory holidays within two months if lost under an exemption.</p><p><strong>Overtime</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\" data-ref=\"osh.s27\">Section 27</a>). Twice the rate of wages, daily or weekly as more favourable, <strong>with consent</strong>. Central sphere: rounding rules and a 144-hour quarterly cap (rule 69).</p><p><strong>Night shifts and overlaps</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s28\" data-ref=\"osh.s28\">Section 28</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s29\" data-ref=\"osh.s29\">Section 29</a>). Post-midnight hours count to the previous day; no overlapping relays without exemption.</p><p><strong>Annual leave</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\" data-ref=\"osh.s32\">Section 32</a>). After 180 days worked in a calendar year: 1 day per 20 (1 per 15 for adolescents and below-ground miners). Carry forward up to 30 days; encashment on demand at year-end; leave wages on exit within two working days (two months on superannuation or death). See the worked example.</p><p><strong>Notices.</strong> Display periods of work (Form XII) and change them no more than once a week without sanction (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s31\" data-ref=\"osh.s31\">Section 31</a>).</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Annual leave: worked example\"><figcaption><strong>Annual leave: worked example</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Illustrative application of s.32(1). Figures are hypothetical.</span></figcaption><dl class=\"hr-formula\"><dt>Days worked in the calendar year</dt><dd>240 (plus 20 days of lay-off) <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(i), (iii)</span></dd><dt>Qualifies?</dt><dd>Yes: 240 + 20 = 260 ≥ 180 (lay-off counts towards 180) <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(iii)</span></dd><dt>Leave earned</dt><dd>240 ÷ 20 = 12 days (lay-off days do not earn leave) <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(ii)–(iii)</span></dd><dt>Below-ground mine worker</dt><dd>240 ÷ 15 = 16 days <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(ii)</span></dd><dt>Unused at year-end</dt><dd>Carry forward up to 30 days in total; encash on demand; days above 30 may be encashed <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(vii)–(ix)</span></dd><dt>Resigns mid-year</dt><dd>Leave wages for accrued leave within two working days <span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.32(1)(vi)(a)</span></dd></dl><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.32</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s26\">Section 26 — Weekly and compensatory holidays</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\">Section 27 — Extra wages for overtime</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\">Section 32 — Annual leave with wages, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.working-hours\">Daily and weekly working hours</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.women","title":"6. Women at work","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.women","title":"Women at work","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.women-night-work"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">Women may be employed in all establishments and all work, including at night with consent and prescribed safeguards; the Government may require safeguards for dangerous operations.</p><p><strong>All work, all establishments</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\" data-ref=\"osh.s43\">Section 43</a>). Including before 6 a.m. and after 7 p.m. with the woman&#39;s consent, subject to conditions.</p><p><strong>Central sphere conditions</strong> (rule 83; see the checklist): written consent; transport; lighting and CCTV; nearby facilities; emergency numbers; three women together below ground; POSH compliance.</p><p><strong>Dangerous operations</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s44\" data-ref=\"osh.s44\">Section 44</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s82\" data-ref=\"osh.s82\">Section 82</a>). Safeguards may be required before employing women; factory rules may restrict pregnant women in dangerous processes.</p><p><strong>Facilities.</strong> Separate washrooms, toilets and rest rooms; crèches (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>). Maternity protection is in the Code on Social Security (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s59\" data-ref=\"ssc.s59\">Section 59</a>).</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Women&#39;s night work: checklist (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>Women&#39;s night work: checklist (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Rule 83, for work before 6 a.m. or beyond 7 p.m. State rules may differ for State-sphere establishments.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Consent</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Written consent of the woman employee</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Maternity</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">No employment contrary to the maternity benefit provisions of the SS Code</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Transport</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Pick-up and drop at her residence</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(c)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Facilities</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Well-lit workplace and passages; toilets, washrooms and water nearby; CCTV on the way to facilities</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(d)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Emergency</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Dedicated phone numbers displayed at the workplace and in vehicles</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(f)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Mines (below ground)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">At least three women on duty at any place</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(g)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Harassment</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Comply with the POSH Act, 2013</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 83(h)</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.43; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 83</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\">Section 43 — Employment of women</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s44\">Section 44 — Adequate safety of employment of women in dangerous operation</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s82\">Section 82 — Dangerous operations</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.women-night-work\">Women&#39;s working hours</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.contract-labour","title":"7. Contract labour","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.contract-labour","title":"Contract labour","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.core-activity","osh.dg.contract-wage-default"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">At 50+ contract labour, contractors need a licence; contract labour in core activities is prohibited subject to exceptions; principal employers provide welfare and are the fallback payer of wages.</p><p><strong>Applicability</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\" data-ref=\"osh.s45\">Section 45</a>). 50 or more contract labour in an establishment, or with a manpower supply contractor, on any day in the last 12 months; not intermittent or casual work.</p><p><strong>Licensing</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\" data-ref=\"osh.s47\">Section 47</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s48\" data-ref=\"osh.s48\">Section 48</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\" data-ref=\"osh.s119\">Section 119</a>). Electronic licence for five years (pan-India available); deemed after 45 days. Central sphere: Form XXI, security of ₹1,000 per worker, renewal 30–90 days before expiry, work orders intimated within 15 days.</p><p><strong>Core activities</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\" data-ref=\"osh.s57\">Section 57</a>). Prohibited, unless ordinarily done through contractors, not needing full-time workers, or a sudden surge. See the decision aid.</p><p><strong>Principal employer&#39;s duties.</strong></p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Use only licensed contractors (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s54\" data-ref=\"osh.s54\">Section 54</a>).</li><li>Provide welfare facilities (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\" data-ref=\"osh.s53\">Section 53</a>).</li><li>Pay unpaid wages and recover from the contractor (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\" data-ref=\"osh.s55\">Section 55</a>; Central sphere: within 15 days).</li><li>Central sphere: grievance committee deciding contract labour grievances within 30 days (rule 184).</li></ul><p><strong>Contractor&#39;s duties.</strong> No fees from workers (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s49\" data-ref=\"osh.s49\">Section 49</a>); electronic wage payment (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\" data-ref=\"osh.s55\">Section 55</a>); experience certificates (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s56\" data-ref=\"osh.s56\">Section 56</a>). Central sphere: parity of hours and conditions with direct workers doing similar work (rule 93(2)); EPF and ESI membership as applicable (rule 93(4)).</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Can this activity be given to contract labour?\"><figcaption><strong>Can this activity be given to contract labour?</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Section 57 read with s.2(1)(p). A decision aid, not a substitute for the statutory test.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">1. Is it a core activity?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">The activity for which the establishment is set up, or one essential or necessary to it (s.2(1)(p))</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.2(1)(p)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">2. Listed support service?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Sanitation, security, canteen, loading and unloading, hospitals or schools, courier, civil works, gardening, housekeeping, transport, intermittent activity: not core unless the establishment is set up for it</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">proviso to s.2(1)(p)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">3. If core: does an exception apply?</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">(a) ordinarily done through contractors; (b) not needing full-time workers; (c) a sudden increase in volume to finish in a specified time</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.57(1) proviso</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">4. No exception</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Contract labour prohibited</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.57(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">5. Unsure</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Apply to the appropriate Government (Central sphere: rule 101; no adverse action while pending)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.57(2); rule 101</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(p), 57; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 101</div></figure><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"When a contractor does not pay wages (Central Rules)\"><figcaption><strong>When a contractor does not pay wages (Central Rules)</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">s.55 and rule 98. Wage period of one month assumed.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-timeline\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">End of wage period (day 0)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Wage period closes (no wage period may exceed one month)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 98(1)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">By day 7</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Contractor pays by bank transfer or electronically, and informs the principal employer</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.55(1)–(2); rule 98(2)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Day 7 passes unpaid</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Principal employer must act</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">rule 98(8)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Within 15 days</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Principal employer pays in full or the balance; recovers from the contractor (bills, debt or security)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.55(3); rule 98(8)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">If still unpaid</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">CLC(C) inquiry; payment ordered from the security deposit; contractor re-furnishes the security within 15 days</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.55(4); rule 99</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.55; <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 98–99</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\">Section 53 — Liability of principal employer for welfare facilities</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\">Section 55 — Responsibility for payment of wages</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\">Section 57 — Prohibition of employment of contract labour</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-prohibition\">Prohibiting contract labour</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.migrant-workers","title":"8. Inter-State migrant workers","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.migrant-workers","title":"Inter-State migrant workers","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":[],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">Part II applies at 10 or more inter-State migrant workers (wages up to ₹18,000): suitable conditions, parity of benefits, both-State accident reporting and an annual journey fare.</p><p><strong>Who</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(zf)</a>). Recruited in one State for work in another, or moved on their own, earning up to ₹18,000 a month (or a notified higher amount).</p><p><strong>Duties</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s60\" data-ref=\"osh.s60\">Section 60</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\" data-ref=\"osh.s61\">Section 61</a>). Suitable working conditions; report fatal and serious accidents to authorities in both States and to next of kin; extend all benefits; pay an annual to-and-fro fare (Central sphere: after 180 days in 12 months, rule 102).</p><p><strong>Government side.</strong> National portal with self-registration on Aadhaar (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s21\" data-ref=\"osh.s21\">Section 21</a>); PDS and BOCW-benefit portability (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s62\" data-ref=\"osh.s62\">Section 62</a>); helpline (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s63\" data-ref=\"osh.s63\">Section 63</a>).</p><p><strong>Debts</strong> of the worker to the contractor or principal employer are extinguished at the end of employment (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s65\" data-ref=\"osh.s65\">Section 65</a>).</p><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s21\">Section 21 — Collection of statistics and portal for inter-State migrant workers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s60\">Section 60 — Facilities to inter-State migrant workers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\">Section 61 — Journey allowance</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s65\">Section 65 — Past liabilities</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.ismw\">Coverage, allowances and portability</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.sectors","title":"9. Sector-specific Parts","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.sectors","title":"Sector-specific Parts","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":[],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">Chapter XI carries sector regimes: audio-visual workers, mines, beedi and cigar, building work, factories and plantations, most with their own rules.</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><strong>Audio-visual workers</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s66\" data-ref=\"osh.s66\">Section 66</a>): registered written agreements; producer liable for contractor defaults.</li><li><strong>Mines</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s67\" data-ref=\"osh.s67\">Section 67</a>–<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s73\" data-ref=\"osh.s73\">Section 73</a>): sole qualified manager; no one under 18 (trainees from 16); rescue services; Central rules and regulations.</li><li><strong>Beedi and cigar</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s74\" data-ref=\"osh.s74\">Section 74</a>–<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s77\" data-ref=\"osh.s77\">Section 77</a>): premises licences (State).</li><li><strong>Building work</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s78\" data-ref=\"osh.s78\">Section 78</a>): fitness for risky operations; safety officers at 250+ workers; design-stage duties (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s9\" data-ref=\"osh.s9\">Section 9</a>).</li><li><strong>Factories</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s79\" data-ref=\"osh.s79\">Section 79</a>–<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s91\" data-ref=\"osh.s91\">Section 91</a>): plans, site permission (deemed after 30 days), licences; hazardous-process duties; State rules.</li><li><strong>Plantations</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s92\" data-ref=\"osh.s92\">Section 92</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s93\" data-ref=\"osh.s93\">Section 93</a>): housing, crèche at 50+, education, health, pesticide safety (State rules).</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s66\">Section 66 — Prohibition of employment of audio-visual worker without agreement</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s67\">Section 67 — Managers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s74\">Section 74 — Licence to industrial premises and person</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s78\">Section 78 — Prohibition of employment of certain persons in certain building or construction</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s79\">Section 79 — Approval and licensing of factories</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s92\">Section 92 — Facilities for workers in plantation</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.guide.enforcement","title":"10. Inspection, penalties and transition","generated":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.c.enforcement","title":"Inspection, penalties and transition","generated":true,"test":false,"defaultOpen":true,"aliases":["osh.dg.penalty-matrix"],"html":"<p class=\"hr-summary\">Web-based inspections with an advisory role; prohibition orders for imminent danger; civil penalties after enquiry for most breaches; 30 days to comply before prosecution except after accidents. Thirteen old Acts are repealed.</p><p><strong>Inspection</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s34\" data-ref=\"osh.s34\">Section 34</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s35\" data-ref=\"osh.s35\">Section 35</a>). Randomised web-based scheme; wide powers to enter, sample and seize. Third-party audit for start-ups may be notified (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s37\" data-ref=\"osh.s37\">Section 37</a>).</p><p><strong>Prohibition orders</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s38\" data-ref=\"osh.s38\">Section 38</a>). Factories (3 days unless extended; appeal to the High Court), mines, docks and building sites; comply at once (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s104\" data-ref=\"osh.s104\">Section 104</a>).</p><p><strong>Penalties</strong> (see the table). General penalty ₹2–3 lakh (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\" data-ref=\"osh.s94\">Section 94</a>); records ₹50,000–1 lakh (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s96\" data-ref=\"osh.s96\">Section 96</a>); hazardous-process and accident-linked breaches carry imprisonment (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s102\" data-ref=\"osh.s102\">Section 102</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s103\" data-ref=\"osh.s103\">Section 103</a>). Penalties are imposed by an officer after enquiry, with appeal in 60 days (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\" data-ref=\"osh.s111\">Section 111</a>).</p><p><strong>Before prosecution</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\" data-ref=\"osh.s110\">Section 110</a>): 30 days to comply, except after an accident or a repeat within three years. Complaints within six months of knowledge.</p><p><strong>Compounding</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s114\" data-ref=\"osh.s114\">Section 114</a>): 50% of maximum penalty or 75% of maximum fine; not for repeats within three years.</p><p><strong>Transition</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s143\" data-ref=\"osh.s143\">Section 143</a>). Factories, Plantations, Mines, CLRA, ISMW, BOCW and seven other Acts repealed from 21 November 2025; rules, notifications and licences continue so far as consistent (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\" data-ref=\"osh.s119\">s.119(5)</a>).</p><figure class=\"hr-diagram\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"OSH Code penalties at a glance\"><figcaption><strong>OSH Code penalties at a glance</strong><br><span class=\"hr-muted\">Summary of ss.94–106 and 114. The statutory text governs.</span></figcaption><ol class=\"hr-flow\"><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Any contravention with no specific penalty (employer)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹2–3 lakh; ₹2,000 a day if continued (compoundable at 50% of max)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.94</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Registers, returns, production of records</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh (repeat up to ₹2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.96</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Contravention of provisions or employment orders</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹50,000–1 lakh; repeat: up to 3 months or ₹2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.97</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Obstruction / falsification</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (repeat: up to 6 months or ₹1–2 lakh)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">ss.95, 98</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Failure to furnish plans, returns, notices</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty ₹1–2 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.99</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Hazardous-process duties (s.6, s.80)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh; ₹25,000 a day continuing; beyond 1 year: up to 3 years or ₹20 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.102</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing death</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years, or fine ≥ ₹5 lakh, or both (not compoundable)</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(a)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Breach causing serious injury</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 1 year, or fine ₹2–4 lakh, or both; ≥50% of fine may go to the victim</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.103(1)(b)</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Working in breach of a prohibition order</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.104</span></li><li><span class=\"hr-tl-label\">Employee breach of duties</span><span class=\"hr-tl-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><span class=\"hr-tl-value\">Penalty up to ₹10,000</span><span class=\"hr-tl-ref\">s.106</span></li></ol><div class=\"hr-muted hr-diagram-src\">Source: <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94–106, 114</div></figure><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s34\">Section 34 — Appointment of Inspector-cum-Facilitators</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s38\">Section 38 — Special powers of Inspector-cum-Facilitator in respect of factory, mines, dock work and building or other construction work</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\">Section 94 — General penalty for offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\">Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s114\">Section 114 — Composition of certain offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s143\">Section 143 — Repeal and Savings</a></li></ul>"}]}],"checklist":[{"id":"osh.gen.compliance.summary","title":"At a glance — compliance matrix","generated":true,"defaultOpen":true,"children":[{"title":"At a glance","html":"<p>16 obligations. Open a row below for all ten fields.</p><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Obligation</th><th>Deadline / frequency</th><th>Legal basis</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.contract-wages-welfare\">Act as fallback payer and provide welfare to contract labour</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Monthly; grievances within 30 days.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.53, 55; rules 86, 98, 184</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.contractor-licence\">Engage only licensed contractors (50+ contract labour)</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Before engagement; at renewal.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.45, 47, 48, 54, 119; rules 85–96</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.core-activity\">Classify outsourced activities as core or non-core</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Before contracting; annual review.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.2(1)(p), 57; rule 101</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Employment documentation</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.appointment-letters\">Issue appointment letters to every employee</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">On appointment; existing staff by about 21 February 2026.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">s.6(1)(f); rule 6</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Enforcement</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.inspections\">Handle inspections, notices and prohibition orders</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">30 days (s.110); immediately (s.38).</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.38, 40, 95, 104, 110, 114; rules 177, 182</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Factories</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.hazardous-process\">Hazardous-process factory obligations</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">30 days before new processes; 12-monthly exams.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.84, 85, 102; First Schedule</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Health and safety</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.health-exams\">Provide free health examinations</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Annually or as prescribed.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.6(1)(c), 85; rules 5, 60, 109</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Health and safety</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.incident-reporting\">Report accidents, dangerous occurrences and notifiable diseases</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Forthwith / within 12 hours.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.10–12; rules 7–8, 75</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Health and safety</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.safety-committee\">Constitute the Safety Committee and appoint safety officers</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Ongoing; recommendations within 15 days.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">s.22; rules 14–21</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Health and welfare</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.welfare\">Provide welfare facilities by headcount</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Continuous.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.23, 24, 53; rules 22–59</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Hours and leave</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.annual-leave\">Grant and encash annual leave</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Year-end; on exit within two working days.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">s.32; rule 76</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Hours and leave</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.hours-overtime\">Control working hours and overtime</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Each wage period.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.25–31; rules 64–71</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Migrant workers</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.migrant-workers\">Meet inter-State migrant worker duties</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Annually; on accidents.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.59–61, 65; rules 13, 102</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Records and returns</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.registers-returns\">Maintain registers, wage slips, notices and annual returns</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Annual return by 28/29 February.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.33, 96, 99; rules 72–76</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Registration</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.registration\">Register the establishment (or update an existing registration)</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Within 60 days of applicability; changes within 30 days.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">ss.3, 5; rules 3–4</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Women</td><td data-label=\"Obligation\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.women-night\">Put safeguards in place before rostering women at night</a></td><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\">Before rostering.</td><td data-label=\"Legal basis\">s.43; rule 83</td><td data-label=\"Scope\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.contract-wages-welfare","title":"Contract labour — Act as fallback payer and provide welfare to contract labour","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Contract labour — Act as fallback payer and provide welfare to contract labour","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Procedure and forms from the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026 — apply where the Central Government is the appropriate Government (never for factories); State rules govern other establishments.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Provide ss.23–24 welfare to contract labour (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\" data-ref=\"osh.s53\">Section 53</a>); pay contract wages if the contractor fails, and recover (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\" data-ref=\"osh.s55\">Section 55</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Contract labour engaged (Part I).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Principal employer.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><p>Central sphere (rules 86, 98, 184):</p><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Provide toilets, washrooms, water, changing rooms, first aid, canteen and crèche at your premises.</li><li>Check contractor wage payment by the 7th day after the wage period; if unpaid, pay within 15 days and recover.</li><li>File the principal employer&#39;s annual return (Form XVII Part III) by end-February.</li><li>Constitute a grievance committee (principal employer chair, with contractor representative); decide grievances within 30 days, otherwise forward to the inspector.</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Monthly; grievances within 30 days.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Payment proofs; grievance register; welfare facility records.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Contract labour; inspector.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.53, 55</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 86, 98, 184</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\">Section 55 — Responsibility for payment of wages</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.contract-wage-default\">When a contractor does not pay wages (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.contractor-licence","title":"Contract labour — Engage only licensed contractors (50+ contract labour)","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Contract labour — Engage only licensed contractors (50+ contract labour)","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Where Part I applies, ensure each contractor holds a valid licence covering the headcount (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\" data-ref=\"osh.s47\">Section 47</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s54\" data-ref=\"osh.s54\">Section 54</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>50+ contract labour in the establishment or with the contractor.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Procurement / HR.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Count contract labour per establishment and per contractor (any day in 12 months).</li><li>Verify the licence, its validity and its headcount cap before work starts and at renewal (Central sphere: Form XXII; renewal 30–90 days before expiry).</li><li>Check the contractor intimated the work order within 15 days (rule 94).</li><li>Monitor contractor wage payments (see next record).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Before engagement; at renewal.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Licence copies; headcount reports.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Licensing authority (contractor).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>Engaging an unlicensed contractor is a contravention by the principal employer (s.54); General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.45, 47, 48, 54, 119</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 85–96</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.contract-labour\">Contract labour</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.core-activity","title":"Contract labour — Classify outsourced activities as core or non-core","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Contract labour — Classify outsourced activities as core or non-core","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Do not engage contract labour in core activities unless an exception applies (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\" data-ref=\"osh.s57\">Section 57</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Any outsourced activity.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>HR / legal.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>List each contracted activity; apply s.2(1)(p) and its excluded support services.</li><li>For core activities, record which exception applies (ordinarily contracted; not full-time; sudden surge).</li><li>Where unclear, apply to the appropriate Government (Central sphere: rule 101; no adverse action while pending).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Before contracting; annual review.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Activity classification register.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Internal; Government on application.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(p), 57</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 101</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\">Section 57 — Prohibition of employment of contract labour</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.core-activity\">Can this activity be given to contract labour?</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-prohibition\">Prohibiting contract labour</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.appointment-letters","title":"Employment documentation — Issue appointment letters to every employee","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Employment documentation","children":[{"title":"Employment documentation — Issue appointment letters to every employee","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Issue an appointment letter to every employee on appointment; existing employees within three months of commencement (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(f)</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Every employee (direct, fixed term; contractors for their contract labour).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>HR.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Use the prescribed format (Central sphere: rule 6 — identity, Aadhaar with consent, UAN/ESI number, employment type, skill, wages, allowances, social security, maternity benefits).</li><li>Back-fill letters for staff employed before 21 November 2025.</li><li>Contract terms: require contractors to issue letters to contract labour and share evidence (recommended control).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>On appointment; existing staff by about 21 February 2026.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Letters and acknowledgements.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Employee.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.6(1)(f)</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 6</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.appointment-letter\">Appointment letter: what it must contain (Central Rules)</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.appointment-letter\">Appointment letters</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.inspections","title":"Enforcement — Handle inspections, notices and prohibition orders","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Enforcement","children":[{"title":"Enforcement — Handle inspections, notices and prohibition orders","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Afford inspectors facilities (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s40\" data-ref=\"osh.s40\">Section 40</a>); comply with s.110 notices within 30 days; comply with prohibition orders at once (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s38\" data-ref=\"osh.s38\">Section 38</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Inspection, notice or order.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Site head / compliance.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Produce records; do not obstruct (s.95).</li><li>Comply with a s.110 notice within 30 days and keep proof (not available after accidents or repeats within 3 years).</li><li>Comply with any prohibition order immediately; continue paying affected factory workers; appeal (factories: High Court; building sites and docks: 15 days).</li><li>Consider compounding (Central sphere: Form XXVI; pay within 15 days, rule 182).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>30 days (s.110); immediately (s.38).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Inspection file; compliance proof; appeals.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Inspector; appellate authority.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>Obstruction: up to 3 months or ₹1 lakh (s.95); working in breach of a prohibition order: up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh (s.104).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.38, 40, 95, 104, 110, 114</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 177, 182</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\">Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.enforcement\">Inspection, penalties and transition</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.hazardous-process","title":"Factories — Hazardous-process factory obligations","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Factories","children":[{"title":"Factories — Hazardous-process factory obligations","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Occupiers of factories involving First Schedule hazardous processes must disclose hazards, adopt a safety policy and on-site emergency plan, give 30 days&#39; notice of new processes, keep health records and arrange examinations (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\" data-ref=\"osh.s84\">Section 84</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s85\" data-ref=\"osh.s85\">Section 85</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Factory carrying on a hazardous process.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Occupier.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Safety policy at registration; report changes.</li><li>Approved on-site emergency plan, communicated to workers and neighbours.</li><li>Notify new hazardous processes 30 days ahead.</li><li>Health records and examinations (before assignment, and at least every 12 months).</li><li>Appoint qualified supervisors; publicise handling and disposal measures.</li></ol><p>(Detail: State rules.)</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>30 days before new processes; 12-monthly exams.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Policy; emergency plan; health records.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Chief Inspector-cum-Facilitator; local authority; workers; public.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>Up to 2 years and ₹5 lakh, plus ₹25,000 a day continuing (s.102).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.84, 85, 102; First Schedule</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s84\">Section 84 — Compulsory disclosure of information by occupier</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.sch1\">The First Schedule — List of industries involving hazardous processes</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.health-exams","title":"Health and safety — Provide free health examinations","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Health and safety","children":[{"title":"Health and safety — Provide free health examinations","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Provide prescribed annual health examinations free of cost (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(c)</a>); in hazardous-process factories, examinations before assignment and at least every 12 months (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s85\" data-ref=\"osh.s85\">Section 85</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Prescribed classes of employees.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Employer / occupational health.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Central sphere: dock and building workers aged 40+, annually, Form VIII certificate (ESIC facilities may be used) (rule 5).</li><li>Mines: pre-employment and annual examinations (rules 60, 109).</li><li>Hazardous-process factories: State rules; the Ministry&#39;s FAQ says examinations apply regardless of age.</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Annually or as prescribed.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Certificates; health records.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Employee; employer records.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.6(1)(c), 85</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 5, 60, 109</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s85\">Section 85 — Specific responsibility of the occupier in relation to hazardous processes</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.incident-reporting","title":"Health and safety — Report accidents, dangerous occurrences and notifiable diseases","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Health and safety","children":[{"title":"Health and safety — Report accidents, dangerous occurrences and notifiable diseases","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Procedure and forms from the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026 — apply where the Central Government is the appropriate Government (never for factories); State rules govern other establishments.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Notify accidents causing death or 48+ hours&#39; incapacity, dangerous occurrences and Third Schedule diseases (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\" data-ref=\"osh.s10\">Section 10</a>–<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s12\" data-ref=\"osh.s12\">Section 12</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Any qualifying incident or disease.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Site head / safety officer.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><p>Central sphere (rules 7–8):</p><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Death: Form XI forthwith; phone and electronic intimation to the inspectorate, DM/SDO, police and family.</li><li>48-hour injury: Form XI within 12 hours after the 48 hours.</li><li>Dangerous occurrence: within 12 hours.</li><li>Disease: notice forthwith.</li><li>Record in the accident register (Form XIX); also report under SS Code s.73 where applicable.</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Forthwith / within 12 hours.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Form XI; accident register; disease notices.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Inspector-cum-Facilitator; DM/SDO; police; family.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>Failure to give notice: penalty (ss.94, 99); accident-linked breaches: s.103.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.10–12</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 7–8, 75</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.incident-reporting\">Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.safety-committee","title":"Health and safety — Constitute the Safety Committee and appoint safety officers","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Health and safety","children":[{"title":"Health and safety — Constitute the Safety Committee and appoint safety officers","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Procedure and forms from the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026 — apply where the Central Government is the appropriate Government (never for factories); State rules govern other establishments.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Constitute a Safety Committee where required, and appoint safety officers at the s.22(2) thresholds (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\" data-ref=\"osh.s22\">Section 22</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Central sphere: 500+ workers (committee); safety officer thresholds by sector.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Employer.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><p>Central sphere (rules 14–21):</p><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Committee: equal sides, at most 20; worker members via negotiating union or council, or chosen by workers; women proportionately; three-year term; quarterly meetings (monthly in mines); minutes.</li><li>Act on recommendations within 15 days.</li><li>Safety officers: building work 1 up to 500 workers and on a scale; docks from 500; mines from 100 (one per 500); qualified per rules 18 and 20.</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Ongoing; recommendations within 15 days.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Constitution record; minutes; appointment letters; safety officer log.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Workers; inspector on demand.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.22</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 14–21</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\">Section 22 — Safety Committee and safety officers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.safety-systems\">Safety committees, safety officers, standards and incident reporting</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.welfare","title":"Health and welfare — Provide welfare facilities by headcount","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Health and welfare","children":[{"title":"Health and welfare — Provide welfare facilities by headcount","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Rules under ss.23–24 are prescribed by the Central Government; whether they bind State-sphere establishments is an interpretation question (see osh.s23).</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Provide health conditions and welfare facilities as prescribed by the Central Government (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\" data-ref=\"osh.s23\">Section 23</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>), including for contract labour (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s53\" data-ref=\"osh.s53\">Section 53</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Establishments of the types named in the Central Rules.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Facilities / HR.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Baseline: drinking water, separate toilets and washrooms (men, women, transgender persons, persons with disabilities), lighting, first aid.</li><li>By headcount: crèche (&gt;50), lunchroom (&gt;50, factories and mines), canteen (≥100 including contract labour), welfare officer (≥250), ambulance room (&gt;500).</li><li>Quarterly mock drills (annual in plantations).</li><li>Crèche safety standards in rule 58 (CCTV, police verification, attendance, evacuation plan).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Continuous.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Facility register; drill records; welfare officer appointment.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Workers; inspector.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.23, 24, 53</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 22–59</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.welfare-thresholds\">Welfare and safety staffing thresholds</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.annual-leave","title":"Hours and leave — Grant and encash annual leave","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Hours and leave","children":[{"title":"Hours and leave — Grant and encash annual leave","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Grant annual leave after 180 days (1 day per 20), allow carry forward to 30 days, encash on demand at year-end, and pay leave wages on exit (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\" data-ref=\"osh.s32\">Section 32</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Workers with 180+ days in a calendar year.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>HR / payroll.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Accrue leave on days worked (lay-off and maternity count towards 180 but do not earn leave).</li><li>Year-end: carry forward up to 30 days; encash on demand; encash excess above 30.</li><li>Exit: pay leave wages within two working days (two months on superannuation or death).</li><li>Central sphere: leave register in Form XX; share each record once a year on demand (rule 76).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Year-end; on exit within two working days.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Leave register (Form XX).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Worker.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.32</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 76</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\">Section 32 — Annual leave with wages, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.annual-leave\">Annual leave: worked example</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.hours-overtime","title":"Hours and leave — Control working hours and overtime","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Hours and leave","children":[{"title":"Hours and leave — Control working hours and overtime","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Procedure and forms from the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026 — apply where the Central Government is the appropriate Government (never for factories); State rules govern other establishments.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Keep within 8 hours a day and six days a week; pay overtime at twice the rate of wages, only with consent (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\" data-ref=\"osh.s25\">Section 25</a>–<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\" data-ref=\"osh.s27\">Section 27</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>All workers.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>HR / plant management.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Central sphere: 48 hours a week (rule 64); overtime after 8 hours a day (daily wagers) or 48 a week; rounding rules; daily rate = monthly ÷ 26; 144 hours a quarter cap (rule 69).</li><li>Record consent for overtime (recommended control: written or system consent per instance or roster).</li><li>Display notice of periods of work (Form XII) and weekly holidays (rules 67, 71).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Each wage period.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Attendance register; overtime register; consent records; notices.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Workers; inspector.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.25–31</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 64–71</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\">Section 27 — Extra wages for overtime</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.hours-leave\">Working hours, overtime and leave</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.migrant-workers","title":"Migrant workers — Meet inter-State migrant worker duties","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Migrant workers","children":[{"title":"Migrant workers — Meet inter-State migrant worker duties","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>At 10+ inter-State migrant workers: suitable conditions, parity of benefits, both-State accident reporting and the annual journey fare (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s60\" data-ref=\"osh.s60\">Section 60</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\" data-ref=\"osh.s61\">Section 61</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>10+ inter-State migrant workers (wages up to ₹18,000).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Employer / contractor.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Identify migrant workers at hiring.</li><li>Central sphere: pay the annual to-and-fro fare after 180 days in 12 months (rule 102); share statistics on the portal (rule 13).</li><li>Report fatal or serious accidents to both States and next of kin.</li><li>Do not pursue debts after employment ends (s.65).</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Annually; on accidents.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Migrant worker register; fare payments; reports.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Workers; authorities of both States.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.59–61, 65</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 13, 102</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\">Section 61 — Journey allowance</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.migrant-workers\">Inter-State migrant workers</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.registers-returns","title":"Records and returns — Maintain registers, wage slips, notices and annual returns","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Records and returns","children":[{"title":"Records and returns — Maintain registers, wage slips, notices and annual returns","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Procedure and forms from the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026 — apply where the Central Government is the appropriate Government (never for factories); State rules govern other establishments.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Maintain registers, display notices, issue wage slips and file returns (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s33\" data-ref=\"osh.s33\">Section 33</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>All establishments.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>HR / payroll.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><p>Central sphere (rules 72–76):</p><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Registers in Forms XIII (employees), XIV (attendance-cum-muster roll), XV (wages, overtime, deductions) — Code on Wages registers count; Form XIX (accidents); Form XX (leave).</li><li>Wage slips electronically in Form XVI on or before payday.</li><li>Preserve five years; keep within 3 km.</li><li>Notice board details (rule 73).</li><li>Annual return in Forms XVII and XVIII by 28/29 February; further return on sale or discontinuance; EPF/ESI-covered establishments self-declare in Part IV of Form XVII.</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Annual return by 28/29 February.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Registers; wage slips; return acknowledgements.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Inspector-cum-Facilitator / portal.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>Records or returns failure: ₹50,000–1 lakh (s.96); omission to furnish returns: ₹1–2 lakh (s.99).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.33, 96, 99</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 72–76</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s33\">Section 33 — Maintenance of registers, records and filing of returns</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.registration","title":"Registration — Register the establishment (or update an existing registration)","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Registration","children":[{"title":"Registration — Register the establishment (or update an existing registration)","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Statutory requirement – applies irrespective of appropriate Government</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Statutory requirement; procedural detail (forms, authorities) depends on the appropriate Government&#39;s rules.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Register every establishment to which the Code applies electronically within 60 days (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\" data-ref=\"osh.s3\">Section 3</a>); no one may be employed in an unregistered establishment.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>New establishment; or existing registration under a Central labour law.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>Employer.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><ol class=\"hr-list\"><li>Central sphere: Form I on the Shram Suvidha Portal; certificate in Form III within 7 days or deemed (rule 3).</li><li>Existing registrations: update particulars in Form I within six months of the Rules coming into force (rule 3(6)).</li><li>Notify changes within 30 days; display the certificate.</li><li>On closure: Form II within 30 days with a certificate of dues paid.</li></ol></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Within 60 days of applicability; changes within 30 days.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Registration certificate; change filings.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Registering officer.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>Employing anyone in an unregistered establishment contravenes s.3(7); General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.3, 5</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 3–4</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\">Section 3 — Registration of certain establishments</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.applicability\">Who and what the Code covers</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cl.women-night","title":"Women — Put safeguards in place before rostering women at night","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Women","children":[{"title":"Women — Put safeguards in place before rostering women at night","html":"<div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Central sphere only (Central Rules)</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Procedure and forms from the OSH (Central) Rules, 2026 — apply where the Central Government is the appropriate Government (never for factories); State rules govern other establishments.</span></div><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-kv\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Requirement</th><td data-label=\"Requirement\"><p>Employ women before 6 a.m. or after 7 p.m. only with consent and prescribed safeguards (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\" data-ref=\"osh.s43\">Section 43</a>).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Applicability trigger</th><td data-label=\"Applicability trigger\"><p>Women on night or early shifts.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Responsible stakeholder</th><td data-label=\"Responsible stakeholder\"><p>HR / admin / security.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Required action</th><td data-label=\"Required action\"><p>Central sphere (rule 83): written consent; transport to and from residence; lighting and CCTV; nearby facilities; emergency numbers displayed (including in vehicles); three women together below ground in mines; POSH Act compliance.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deadline / frequency</th><td data-label=\"Deadline / frequency\"><p>Before rostering.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Documentation</th><td data-label=\"Documentation\"><p>Consent forms; transport logs; CCTV and facility checks.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recipient / authority</th><td data-label=\"Recipient / authority\"><p>Women employees.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequence of non-compliance</th><td data-label=\"Consequence of non-compliance\"><p>General penalty ₹2–3 lakh on the employer (s.94), after an enquiry (s.111); 30 days to comply before prosecution (s.110).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Legal basis</th><td data-label=\"Legal basis\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.43</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 83</li></ul></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Verification status</th><td data-label=\"Verification status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span><div class=\"hr-muted\">Drafted from the Gazette text of the Code and the Central Rules; not legally reviewed.</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\">Section 43 — Employment of women</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.women-night-work\">Women&#39;s night work: checklist (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]}],"comparison":[{"id":"osh.gen.comparison.summary","title":"At a glance — all changes","generated":true,"defaultOpen":true,"children":[{"title":"At a glance","html":"<p>13 changes that matter in practice. Open a row below for the full seven-column comparison.</p><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Topic</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Previous law</th><th>Code</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-labour-threshold\">When contract labour regulation applies</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Relaxed</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.1(4)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.45</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-licence\">Licensing of contractors and registration</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">ss.7(1), 12(1)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">ss.3, 47, 48, 119; rules 85–96</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-prohibition\">Prohibiting contract labour</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.10(1)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">ss.2(1)(p), 57; rule 101</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-wages\">Principal employer&#39;s liability for contract wages</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Consolidated</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.21(4)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.55; rule 98</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Coverage</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.factory-definition\">Meaning of &#39;factory&#39;</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Relaxed</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.2(m); s.2(m)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.2(1)(v), (w)</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Employment documentation</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.appointment-letter\">Appointment letters</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>New</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\"></td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.6(1)(f); rule 6</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Hours and leave</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.annual-leave\">Annual leave with wages</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.79(1)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.32</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Hours and leave</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.overtime\">Overtime</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.59(1)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.27; rule 69</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Hours and leave</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.working-hours\">Daily and weekly working hours</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">ss.51, 54</td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.25; rule 64</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Inter-State migrant workers</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.ismw\">Coverage, allowances and portability</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">ss.1(4)(a), 14, 15</td><td data-label=\"Code\">ss.21, 59–65; rules 13, 102</td><td data-label=\"Status\"></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Offences</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.penalties\">General penalty</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.92</td><td data-label=\"Code\">ss.94, 103, 110, 111</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Welfare and safety</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.welfare-thresholds\">Canteen, crèche, welfare officer and safety officer thresholds</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">ss.40B, 46, 48, 49</td><td data-label=\"Code\">ss.22, 24; rules 53, 57, 58</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Area\">Women</td><td data-label=\"Topic\"><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.women-night-work\">Women&#39;s working hours</a></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Relaxed</strong></td><td data-label=\"Previous law\">s.66(1)(b)</td><td data-label=\"Code\">s.43; rule 83</td><td data-label=\"Status\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.contract-labour-threshold","title":"Contract labour — When contract labour regulation applies","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Contract labour — When contract labour regulation applies","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">CLRA Act read from the Haryana Labour Department website copy (amendments to 2005); the India Code page could not be fetched. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">When contract labour regulation applies</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Contract Labour (R&amp;A) Act, 1970, s.1(4): every establishment, and every contractor, employing <strong>20 or more</strong> workmen as contract labour on any day of the preceding 12 months.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Part I applies at <strong>50 or more</strong> contract labour, for establishments and manpower supply contractors (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\" data-ref=\"osh.s45\">Section 45</a>). Intermittent or casual work is excluded (more than 120 days a year, or 60 if seasonal, is not intermittent).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Relaxed</strong><p>Licensing and related Part I duties start at 50 instead of 20.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Establishments with 20–49 contract labour no longer need contractor licences, but general duties (welfare, registration, safety) still apply.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.clra-1970-haryana\">The Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970 – copy on the Haryana Labour Department website (Act No. 37 of 1970)</a>, s.1(4) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.45</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Official FAQs describe the threshold change as administrative and say worker entitlements are unaffected.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.contract-labour\">Contract labour</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.contract-licence","title":"Contract labour — Licensing of contractors and registration","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Contract labour — Licensing of contractors and registration","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">CLRA Act read from the Haryana Labour Department website copy (amendments to 2005); the India Code page could not be fetched. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Licensing of contractors and registration</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Contract Labour (R&amp;A) Act, 1970: the <strong>principal employer registers</strong> the establishment (s.7), and each contractor needs a <strong>licence</strong> to undertake work through contract labour (s.12).</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>A contractor needs a licence valid for <strong>five years</strong>, issued <strong>electronically</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\" data-ref=\"osh.s47\">Section 47</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s48\" data-ref=\"osh.s48\">Section 48</a>). A single <strong>pan-India licence</strong> is available for work in several States. A work-specific licence is available where the criteria are not met. Licences are deemed issued after 45 days (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\" data-ref=\"osh.s119\">Section 119</a>). The establishment registers once under <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\" data-ref=\"osh.s3\">Section 3</a>.</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong><p>Five-year, pan-India, deemed licences replace work-specific licences; one registration for the establishment.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Multi-State contractors can consolidate licences. Principal employers should verify licence validity and headcount caps (s.54).</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.clra-1970-haryana\">The Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970 – copy on the Haryana Labour Department website (Act No. 37 of 1970)</a>, ss.7(1), 12(1) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.3, 47, 48, 119</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 85–96</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><span class=\"hr-muted\">None</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s47\">Section 47 — Licensing of contractors</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.contract-prohibition","title":"Contract labour — Prohibiting contract labour","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Contract labour — Prohibiting contract labour","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">CLRA Act read from the Haryana Labour Department website copy (amendments to 2005); the India Code page could not be fetched. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Prohibiting contract labour</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Contract Labour (R&amp;A) Act, 1970, s.10(1): the appropriate Government could prohibit contract labour in a process or work <strong>by notification</strong>, after consulting the Central or State Advisory Board.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Contract labour in <strong>core activities</strong> is prohibited by the Code itself, except where the activity is ordinarily done through contractors, does not need full-time workers, or meets a sudden surge (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\" data-ref=\"osh.s57\">Section 57</a>). Support services listed in s.2(1)(p) are not core.</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong><p>A statutory core-activity bar with exceptions replaces case-by-case notification.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Review all outsourced activities against the core-activity test; seek a decision under s.57(2) where unsure.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.clra-1970-haryana\">The Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970 – copy on the Haryana Labour Department website (Act No. 37 of 1970)</a>, s.10(1) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(p), 57</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 101</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>How existing s.10 notifications interact with s.57 (saved under s.143(3) so far as not contrary) is an interpretation question.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\">Section 57 — Prohibition of employment of contract labour</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.core-activity\">Can this activity be given to contract labour?</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.contract-wages","title":"Contract labour — Principal employer's liability for contract wages","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Contract labour — Principal employer's liability for contract wages","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">CLRA Act read from the Haryana Labour Department website copy (amendments to 2005); the India Code page could not be fetched. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Principal employer&#39;s liability for contract wages</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Contract Labour (R&amp;A) Act, 1970, s.21(4): if the contractor failed to pay on time or short-paid, the <strong>principal employer</strong> had to pay in full and could recover from the contractor.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Same liability (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\" data-ref=\"osh.s55\">s.55(3)</a>), with electronic payment by the contractor. Central sphere: wages by the 7th day after the wage period; the principal employer pays within 15 days of default (rule 98).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Consolidated</strong><p>Carried over, with payment timelines in the Central Rules.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Track contractor wage payments monthly; build the 15-day fallback into the contractor management process.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.clra-1970-haryana\">The Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970 – copy on the Haryana Labour Department website (Act No. 37 of 1970)</a>, s.21(4) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.55</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 98</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><span class=\"hr-muted\">None</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\">Section 55 — Responsibility for payment of wages</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.contract-wage-default\">When a contractor does not pay wages (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.factory-definition","title":"Coverage — Meaning of 'factory'","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Coverage","children":[{"title":"Coverage — Meaning of 'factory'","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Factories Act read from State Government website copies (Kerala, Haryana) and, for ss.59, 66, 79 and 92, a secondary reproduction. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Meaning of &#39;factory&#39;</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Factories Act, 1948, s.2(m): premises with <strong>10 or more workers</strong> with power, or <strong>20 or more</strong> without power, on any day of the preceding 12 months.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p><strong>20 or more workers</strong> with power, or <strong>40 or more</strong> without power (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(w)</a>). Thresholds under prior State law continue until amended. Separately, any place with <strong>10 or more workers</strong> is an &#39;establishment&#39; and gets the general safety, health and welfare duties (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(v)</a>).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Relaxed</strong><p>Factory-specific regulation starts at a higher headcount; general establishment duties start at 10.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Small units (10–19 workers with power) fall out of the factory regime but remain &#39;establishments&#39;. Check State thresholds preserved by the proviso to s.2(1)(w).</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-kerala\">The Factories Act, 1948 – copy on the Factories and Boilers Department, Kerala website (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, s.2(m) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-haryana\">The Factories Act, 1948 – copy on the Haryana Labour Department website (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, s.2(m) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.2(1)(v), (w)</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Official FAQs describe the same change (10/20 to 20/40) and state that welfare and safety duties still apply at 10 or more employees.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.applicability\">Who and what the Code covers</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.appointment-letter","title":"Employment documentation — Appointment letters","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Employment documentation","children":[{"title":"Employment documentation — Appointment letters","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">The absence of a general predecessor duty is based on the predecessor provisions read; not exhaustive.</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Appointment letters</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>No general statutory duty to issue appointment letters was found in the predecessor provisions read for this handbook.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Every employer must issue an <strong>appointment letter</strong> to every employee; existing employees within <strong>three months</strong> of commencement (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(f)</a>). Central sphere: prescribed format (rule 6).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>New</strong><p>A universal duty to issue appointment letters.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Issue letters to all employees, including fixed term staff, in the prescribed format and keep acknowledgements; require contractors to do the same for contract labour.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><p class=\"hr-muted\">None recorded.</p><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.6(1)(f)</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 6</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Some predecessor laws had sector-specific documentation duties (for example, certain State shops and establishments Acts); these were not reviewed.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.appointment-letter\">Appointment letter: what it must contain (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.annual-leave","title":"Hours and leave — Annual leave with wages","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Hours and leave","children":[{"title":"Hours and leave — Annual leave with wages","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Factories Act read from State Government website copies (Kerala, Haryana) and, for ss.59, 66, 79 and 92, a secondary reproduction. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Annual leave with wages</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Factories Act, 1948, s.79(1): leave for workers with <strong>240 days</strong> or more in a calendar year, at <strong>one day for every 20 days</strong> worked.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Eligibility at <strong>180 days</strong>; same rate of one day per 20 days (1 per 15 for adolescents and below-ground miners); carry forward up to 30 days; encashment on demand at year-end (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\" data-ref=\"osh.s32\">Section 32</a>).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong><p>More workers qualify (180 days instead of 240), and year-end encashment is available on demand.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Update leave eligibility rules and payroll for annual encashment.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-advocatekhoj\">The Factories Act, 1948 – advocatekhoj.com bare act pages (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, s.79(1) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.32</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><span class=\"hr-muted\">None</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\">Section 32 — Annual leave with wages, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.annual-leave\">Annual leave: worked example</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.overtime","title":"Hours and leave — Overtime","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Hours and leave","children":[{"title":"Hours and leave — Overtime","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Factories Act read from State Government website copies (Kerala, Haryana) and, for ss.59, 66, 79 and 92, a secondary reproduction. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Overtime</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Factories Act, 1948, s.59(1): beyond <strong>9 hours a day or 48 a week</strong>, overtime at <strong>twice the ordinary rate of wages</strong>.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Overtime at <strong>twice the rate of wages</strong>, beyond prescribed hours, computed daily or weekly as more favourable, and <strong>only with the worker&#39;s consent</strong> (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\" data-ref=\"osh.s27\">Section 27</a>). Central sphere: after 8 hours a day (daily wagers) or 48 a week; capped at 144 hours a quarter (rule 69).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong><p>Consent is required; the trigger moves with the 8-hour day.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Record worker consent to overtime; recompute overtime thresholds.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-advocatekhoj\">The Factories Act, 1948 – advocatekhoj.com bare act pages (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, s.59(1) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.27</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 69</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><span class=\"hr-muted\">None</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\">Section 27 — Extra wages for overtime</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/cow.s14\">Section 14 — Wages for overtime work</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.working-hours","title":"Hours and leave — Daily and weekly working hours","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Hours and leave","children":[{"title":"Hours and leave — Daily and weekly working hours","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Factories Act read from State Government website copies (Kerala, Haryana) and, for ss.59, 66, 79 and 92, a secondary reproduction. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Daily and weekly working hours</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Factories Act, 1948: no adult worker more than <strong>48 hours a week</strong> (s.51) or <strong>9 hours a day</strong> (s.54).</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>No worker more than <strong>8 hours a day</strong>, with intervals and spread-over as notified (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\" data-ref=\"osh.s25\">Section 25</a>). Central sphere: 48 hours a week (rule 64).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong><p>The daily limit falls from 9 to 8 hours; the weekly limit is in the Central Rules.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Rework shift patterns built on 9-hour days. Longer days depend on notified spread-over and overtime with consent.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-kerala\">The Factories Act, 1948 – copy on the Factories and Boilers Department, Kerala website (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, ss.51, 54 <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.25</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 64</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>An official FAQ describes flexibility to extend daily hours up to 12 with consent and overtime; that is guidance.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.hours-leave\">Working hours, overtime and leave</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.ismw","title":"Inter-State migrant workers — Coverage, allowances and portability","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Inter-State migrant workers","children":[{"title":"Inter-State migrant workers — Coverage, allowances and portability","html":"<div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Coverage, allowances and portability</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Inter-State Migrant Workmen Act, 1979:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>s.1(4)(a): applied at <strong>five or more</strong> inter-State migrant workmen;</li><li>s.14: <strong>displacement allowance</strong> at recruitment (50% of monthly wages or ₹75, whichever higher);</li><li>s.15: <strong>journey allowance</strong> by the contractor for outward and return journeys.</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Part II applies at <strong>10 or more</strong> inter-State migrant workers (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\" data-ref=\"osh.s59\">Section 59</a>). The employer pays an <strong>annual to-and-fro fare</strong> to the native place (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\" data-ref=\"osh.s61\">Section 61</a>; Central sphere: after 180 days in 12 months, rule 102). There is no displacement allowance in Part II. New: a national portal with self-registration (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s21\" data-ref=\"osh.s21\">Section 21</a>), PDS portability (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s62\" data-ref=\"osh.s62\">Section 62</a>) and extinguishment of unsettled debts (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s65\" data-ref=\"osh.s65\">Section 65</a>).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong><p>Higher threshold; annual journey fare; no displacement allowance; portal and portability.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Pay the annual journey fare to eligible migrant workers; register them on the portal (rule 13).</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.ismw-1979-indiacode\">The Inter-State Migrant Workmen (Regulation of Employment and Conditions of Service) Act, 1979 – India Code text (Act No. 30 of 1979)</a>, ss.1(4)(a), 14, 15</li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.21, 59–65</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 13, 102</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>The statement that Part II has no displacement allowance is based on reading ss.59–65 of the Code in full.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s59\">Section 59 — Applicability of Part II</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\">Section 61 — Journey allowance</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.penalties","title":"Offences — General penalty","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Offences","children":[{"title":"Offences — General penalty","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Factories Act read from State Government website copies (Kerala, Haryana) and, for ss.59, 66, 79 and 92, a secondary reproduction. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">General penalty</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Factories Act, 1948, s.92: occupier and manager punishable with up to <strong>two years</strong>, or a fine up to <strong>₹1 lakh</strong>, or both; up to ₹1,000 a day for continuing contravention.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>General <strong>penalty</strong> of <strong>₹2–3 lakh</strong> on the employer (imposed after an enquiry), plus up to ₹2,000 a day (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\" data-ref=\"osh.s94\">Section 94</a>). Criminal punishment is reserved for specified offences, such as breaches causing death (up to two years, or a fine of at least ₹5 lakh) (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s103\" data-ref=\"osh.s103\">Section 103</a>). A 30-day opportunity to comply precedes prosecution, except after an accident (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\" data-ref=\"osh.s110\">Section 110</a>).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Modified</strong><p>Civil penalties for general breaches; heavier, targeted punishment for accident-linked breaches.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Expect monetary penalties through enquiries; preserve evidence of due diligence for accident cases.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-advocatekhoj\">The Factories Act, 1948 – advocatekhoj.com bare act pages (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, s.92 <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.94, 103, 110, 111</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><span class=\"hr-muted\">None</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s94\">Section 94 — General penalty for offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.penalty-matrix\">OSH Code penalties at a glance</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.welfare-thresholds","title":"Welfare and safety — Canteen, crèche, welfare officer and safety officer thresholds","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Welfare and safety","children":[{"title":"Welfare and safety — Canteen, crèche, welfare officer and safety officer thresholds","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Factories Act read from State Government website copies (Kerala, Haryana) and, for ss.59, 66, 79 and 92, a secondary reproduction. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Canteen, crèche, welfare officer and safety officer thresholds</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Factories Act, 1948:</p><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>canteen: more than <strong>250</strong> workers (s.46);</li><li>crèche: more than <strong>30 women</strong> (s.48);</li><li>welfare officer: <strong>500</strong> or more (s.49);</li><li>safety officer: <strong>1,000</strong> or more (s.40B).</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>canteen: <strong>100</strong> or more workers including contract labour (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>);</li><li>crèche: more than <strong>50 workers</strong> (s.24(3); rule 58);</li><li>welfare officer: <strong>250</strong> or more (s.24(2)(iv));</li><li>safety officer: <strong>500</strong> or more, or <strong>250</strong> in hazardous-process factories (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\" data-ref=\"osh.s22\">Section 22</a>).</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Tightened</strong><p>Lower thresholds for canteens, welfare officers and safety officers; the crèche test moves from women to all workers.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Mid-sized factories (100–250 workers) may now need a canteen; 250–500 may need a welfare officer.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-kerala\">The Factories Act, 1948 – copy on the Factories and Boilers Department, Kerala website (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, ss.40B, 46, 48, 49 <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.22, 24</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 53, 57, 58</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>Factory welfare thresholds in the Code are &#39;as may be prescribed by the Central Government&#39; (s.24); see the interpretation note on <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\">Section 22 — Safety Committee and safety officers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.welfare-thresholds\">Welfare and safety staffing thresholds</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.cmp.women-night-work","title":"Women — Women's working hours","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Women","children":[{"title":"Women — Women's working hours","html":"<div class=\"hr-verify\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Row verification: Unverified</span> <span class=\"hr-muted\">Factories Act read from State Government website copies (Kerala, Haryana) and, for ss.59, 66, 79 and 92, a secondary reproduction. Confirmation against a current official text is outstanding (tracker: dep-osh-predecessor-texts).</span></div><div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table hr-table-cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Previous position</th><th>Position under the Code</th><th>Nature of change</th><th>Practical implications</th><th>Legal references</th><th>Qualifications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Topic\">Women&#39;s working hours</td><td data-label=\"Previous position\"><p>Factories Act, 1948, s.66(1)(b): women only between <strong>6 a.m. and 7 p.m.</strong>; States could vary the limits but never to allow work between 10 p.m. and 5 a.m.</p></td><td data-label=\"Position under the Code\"><p>Women may be employed in <strong>all establishments for all types of work</strong>, and before 6 a.m. or after 7 p.m. <strong>with their consent</strong>, subject to prescribed safety conditions (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\" data-ref=\"osh.s43\">Section 43</a>; Central sphere rule 83).</p></td><td data-label=\"Nature of change\"><strong>Relaxed</strong><p>Night work is permitted with consent and safeguards.</p></td><td data-label=\"Practical implications\"><p>Night-shift roles can be opened to women once consent, transport, lighting, CCTV and POSH arrangements are in place.</p></td><td data-label=\"Legal references\"><div class=\"hr-muted\">Previous law</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.fa-1948-advocatekhoj\">The Factories Act, 1948 – advocatekhoj.com bare act pages (Act No. 63 of 1948)</a>, s.66(1)(b) <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">unofficial copy</span></li></ul><div class=\"hr-muted\">Code</div><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.43</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 83</li></ul></td><td data-label=\"Qualifications\"><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li>State conditions for State-sphere establishments were not reviewed.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\">Section 43 — Employment of women</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.women-night-work\">Women&#39;s night work: checklist (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]}],"watchlist":[{"id":"osh.gen.watch.high","title":"High priority (9)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":true,"children":[{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-appointment-letter","title":"Appointment letters","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Every employee needs an appointment letter; existing staff needed one by about 21 February 2026 (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(f)</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> confirm issuance for all staff, and that contractors have issued letters to contract labour.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.appointment-letter\">Open: Appointment letters</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-contract-labour-threshold","title":"When contract labour regulation applies","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Contract labour licensing applies at 50 or more contract labour (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\" data-ref=\"osh.s45\">Section 45</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> recount contract labour per establishment and per contractor.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-labour-threshold\">Open: When contract labour regulation applies</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-contract-prohibition","title":"Prohibiting contract labour","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Contract labour in core activities is prohibited by statute, subject to three exceptions (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\" data-ref=\"osh.s57\">Section 57</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> classify every outsourced activity as core or non-core and record the basis.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-prohibition\">Open: Prohibiting contract labour</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-factory-definition","title":"Meaning of 'factory'","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Factory thresholds rose to 20 (with power) and 40 (without), subject to State law (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(w)</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> re-test factory status per site and check the State&#39;s saved thresholds.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.factory-definition\">Open: Meaning of &#39;factory&#39;</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-working-hours","title":"Daily and weekly working hours","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>The daily limit is 8 hours (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\" data-ref=\"osh.s25\">Section 25</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> review shift designs and rosters, especially 9-hour and compressed-week patterns.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.working-hours\">Open: Daily and weekly working hours</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-appointment-letters","title":"Issue appointment letters to every employee","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Appointment letters are mandatory for every employee (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(f)</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> audit coverage, including back-filled letters for existing staff.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.appointment-letters\">Open: Issue appointment letters to every employee</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-contractor-licence","title":"Engage only licensed contractors (50+ contract labour)","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Principal employers are in breach if a contractor requiring a licence has none (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s54\" data-ref=\"osh.s54\">Section 54</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> set up licence verification for all contractors at 50+ contract labour.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.contractor-licence\">Open: Engage only licensed contractors (50+ contract labour)</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-hours-overtime","title":"Control working hours and overtime","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Daily hours are capped at 8 and overtime needs consent (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\" data-ref=\"osh.s25\">Section 25</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\" data-ref=\"osh.s27\">Section 27</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> review rosters and overtime approvals.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.hours-overtime\">Open: Control working hours and overtime</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-incident-reporting","title":"Report accidents, dangerous occurrences and notifiable diseases","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">HIGH</span><p>Fatal accidents must be reported forthwith and 48-hour injuries within 12 hours of the 48 hours (rule 7). <strong>Action:</strong> align the incident protocol with these clocks.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.incident-reporting\">Open: Report accidents, dangerous occurrences and notifiable diseases</a></p>"}]},{"id":"osh.gen.watch.medium","title":"Medium priority (9)","generated":true,"defaultOpen":false,"children":[{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-annual-leave","title":"Annual leave with wages","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Annual leave eligibility falls to 180 days worked (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\" data-ref=\"osh.s32\">Section 32</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> update leave policy and HRMS accrual rules.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.annual-leave\">Open: Annual leave with wages</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-ismw","title":"Coverage, allowances and portability","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Migrant workers get an annual to-and-fro fare after 180 days (Central sphere) (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s61\" data-ref=\"osh.s61\">Section 61</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> identify inter-State migrant workers (wages up to ₹18,000) and set up the fare payment.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.ismw\">Open: Coverage, allowances and portability</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-overtime","title":"Overtime","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Overtime now needs the worker&#39;s consent (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\" data-ref=\"osh.s27\">Section 27</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> add consent capture to overtime approval.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.overtime\">Open: Overtime</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-welfare-thresholds","title":"Canteen, crèche, welfare officer and safety officer thresholds","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Canteen (100), welfare officer (250) and safety officer (500, or 250 hazardous) thresholds are lower (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\" data-ref=\"osh.s22\">Section 22</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> re-test each site against the new thresholds.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.welfare-thresholds\">Open: Canteen, crèche, welfare officer and safety officer thresholds</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cmp-women-night-work","title":"Women's working hours","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Women may work at night with consent and prescribed safeguards (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\" data-ref=\"osh.s43\">Section 43</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> document consent and the rule 83 safeguards before rostering.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.women-night-work\">Open: Women&#39;s working hours</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-annual-leave","title":"Grant and encash annual leave","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Annual leave is earned after 180 days and can be encashed on demand at year-end (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\" data-ref=\"osh.s32\">Section 32</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> update leave policy and payroll.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.annual-leave\">Open: Grant and encash annual leave</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-registers-returns","title":"Maintain registers, wage slips, notices and annual returns","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>The OSH annual return (Forms XVII and XVIII) is due by 28/29 February (rule 72(5)). <strong>Action:</strong> add to the compliance calendar alongside the SS Code return.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.registers-returns\">Open: Maintain registers, wage slips, notices and annual returns</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-registration","title":"Register the establishment (or update an existing registration)","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Existing establishments had to update registration particulars within six months of the Central Rules (rule 3(6)). <strong>Action:</strong> confirm the update was filed for each Central-sphere establishment.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.registration\">Open: Register the establishment (or update an existing registration)</a></p>"},{"id":"osh.watch.cl-welfare","title":"Provide welfare facilities by headcount","generated":true,"html":"<span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">MEDIUM</span><p>Welfare thresholds are lower than under the Factories Act (canteen 100, welfare officer 250) (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>). <strong>Action:</strong> re-test each site.</p><p><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.welfare\">Open: Provide welfare facilities by headcount</a></p>"}]}],"faqs":[{"id":"osh.faq.contract-licence-threshold","title":"We use 30 contract workers. Does our contractor need a licence?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"We use 30 contract workers. Does our contractor need a licence?","html":"<p>Not under Part I, which applies at 50 or more contract labour in the establishment, or 50 or more with the contractor across establishments (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\" data-ref=\"osh.s45\">Section 45</a>). The contractor may still need a licence if it employs 50+ in total. Welfare, wage and safety duties under other provisions still apply; official FAQs make the same point.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.45</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-code\">FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</a>, Q2</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s45\">Section 45 — Applicability of this Part</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.contract-labour-threshold\">When contract labour regulation applies</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.contractor-default","title":"Our contractor has not paid wages. What must we do?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Our contractor has not paid wages. What must we do?","html":"<p>As principal employer, pay the unpaid wages (or the shortfall) and recover from the contractor (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\" data-ref=\"osh.s55\">s.55(3)</a>). In the Central sphere: wages are due by the 7th day after the wage period, and you must pay within 15 days of the default (rule 98).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.55</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 98</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s55\">Section 55 — Responsibility for payment of wages</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.contract-wage-default\">When a contractor does not pay wages (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.core-security","title":"Can we outsource security and housekeeping?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Contract labour","children":[{"title":"Can we outsource security and housekeeping?","html":"<p>Yes, unless your establishment exists to provide those services. Security, housekeeping, sanitation, canteen, loading and unloading, transport and similar support services are not &#39;core activities&#39; (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(p)</a>), so the s.57 bar does not apply to them.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(p), 57</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s57\">Section 57 — Prohibition of employment of contract labour</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.core-activity\">Can this activity be given to contract labour?</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.applies-10","title":"Does the OSH Code apply to an office with 15 employees?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Coverage","children":[{"title":"Does the OSH Code apply to an office with 15 employees?","html":"<p>Usually yes. An establishment with 10 or more workers is covered (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(v)</a>), so registration, appointment letters, hours, leave, registers and the general safety and welfare duties apply. It is not a &#39;factory&#39; (that needs 20+ workers with power), and Government offices are excluded (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s1\" data-ref=\"osh.s1\">s.1(3)</a>). Count &#39;workers&#39;, which excludes managerial staff and supervisors earning above ₹18,000.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.1(3), 2(1)(v), (zzl)</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.applicability\">Who and what the Code covers</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.audio-visual","title":"Are stunt performers and dubbing artists covered?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Coverage","children":[{"title":"Are stunt performers and dubbing artists covered?","html":"<p>Yes, if their remuneration is within the notified limit: &#39;audio-visual worker&#39; expressly includes dubbing artists and stunt persons (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">Section 2</a>). They may be employed only under a registered written agreement (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s66\" data-ref=\"osh.s66\">Section 66</a>).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(f), 66</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-code\">FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</a>, Q17</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s66\">Section 66 — Prohibition of employment of audio-visual worker without agreement</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.drivers","title":"Does the Code cover drivers and motor transport workers?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Coverage","children":[{"title":"Does the Code cover drivers and motor transport workers?","html":"<p>Yes. Motor transport undertakings with 10 or more workers are establishments (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(v)</a>). Working hours for motor transport workers include running time, subsidiary work and short attendance at terminals (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\" data-ref=\"osh.s25\">Section 25</a>), and medical facilities, uniforms and rain or cold protection may be prescribed (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">s.24(2)(ii)</a>).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(v), 24(2)(ii), 25</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-code\">FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</a>, Q15</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.fixed-term","title":"Are fixed term employees covered?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Coverage","children":[{"title":"Are fixed term employees covered?","html":"<p>Yes. &#39;Employee&#39; and &#39;worker&#39; do not depend on the type of contract, so fixed term staff get appointment letters, health examinations, hours and leave protections (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">Section 2</a>, <a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">Section 6</a>). The official FAQ says the same.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(t), (zzl), 6</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-code\">FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</a>, Q12</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.state-variation","title":"Will the rules differ between our sites in different States?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Coverage","children":[{"title":"Will the rules differ between our sites in different States?","html":"<p>Often yes. For factories, plantations, motor transport and most private establishments, the State is the appropriate Government (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(d)</a>) and makes rules on many matters, and pre-Code State factory thresholds continue until amended. Standards under s.18 and the ss.23–24 rules come from the Central Government. This handbook covers the Central Rules only.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(d), (w), 18, 23, 24, 133</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.faq.which-rules\">Do the Central OSH Rules apply to my factory?</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.which-rules","title":"Do the Central OSH Rules apply to my factory?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Coverage","children":[{"title":"Do the Central OSH Rules apply to my factory?","html":"<p>For most purposes, no: the State Government is the appropriate Government for every factory (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\" data-ref=\"osh.s2\">s.2(1)(d)</a>). The exception is health, working-condition and welfare matters under ss.23–24, which the Code says are prescribed by the Central Government; whether the Central Rules on those matters bind factories is an interpretation question (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\" data-ref=\"osh.s23\">Section 23</a>).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Interpretation</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.2(1)(d), 23, 24</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-2026\">FAQ on OSH&amp;WC Code, 2020 (as on 13.03.2026)</a>, item 1</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s2\">Section 2 — Definitions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.appointment-letter-existing","title":"Do existing employees need appointment letters?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Documentation","children":[{"title":"Do existing employees need appointment letters?","html":"<p>Yes. Employees without one at commencement had to be issued a letter within three months, that is by about 21 February 2026 (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\" data-ref=\"osh.s6\">s.6(1)(f)</a>). Central sphere: use the rule 6 format.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.6(1)(f)</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 6</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s6\">Section 6 — Duties of employer</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.appointment-letters\">Issue appointment letters to every employee</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.prosecution-first","title":"Will we be prosecuted immediately for a lapse?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Enforcement","children":[{"title":"Will we be prosecuted immediately for a lapse?","html":"<p>Usually not: the inspector must first give 30 days to comply, and compliance ends the matter (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\" data-ref=\"osh.s110\">Section 110</a>). This does not apply after an accident, or if the same kind of violation recurs within three years. Most general breaches attract a civil penalty after an enquiry (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s111\" data-ref=\"osh.s111\">Section 111</a>).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.110, 111</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s110\">Section 110 — Limitation of prosecution and cognizance of offences</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.c.enforcement\">Inspection, penalties and transition</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.overtime-consent","title":"Can we require workers to work overtime?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Hours","children":[{"title":"Can we require workers to work overtime?","html":"<p>Only with the worker&#39;s consent (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\" data-ref=\"osh.s27\">Section 27</a>). Overtime is paid at twice the rate of wages; in the Central sphere it is capped at 144 hours a quarter (rule 69).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.27</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 69</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\">Section 27 — Extra wages for overtime</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.twelve-hour-shifts","title":"Can we run 12-hour shifts or a four-day week?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Hours","children":[{"title":"Can we run 12-hour shifts or a four-day week?","html":"<p>The Code limits work to 8 hours a day, with intervals and spread-over as notified (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\" data-ref=\"osh.s25\">Section 25</a>); time beyond that is overtime at twice the rate of wages and needs consent (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\" data-ref=\"osh.s27\">Section 27</a>). An official FAQ refers to flexibility up to 12 hours with consent and overtime. A compressed week without overtime pay is not supported by the text as read; check the notified spread-over in your State.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Interpretation</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-red\">Disputed</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.25, 27</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-code\">FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</a>, Q3</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s25\">Section 25 — Daily and weekly working hours, leave, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s27\">Section 27 — Extra wages for overtime</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.leave-eligibility","title":"When does a worker become entitled to annual leave?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Leave","children":[{"title":"When does a worker become entitled to annual leave?","html":"<p>After working 180 days in a calendar year, at one day for every 20 days worked (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\" data-ref=\"osh.s32\">Section 32</a>). The official FAQ confirms the 180-day test. Leave can be carried forward up to 30 days and encashed on demand at year-end.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.32</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-code\">FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</a>, Q18</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s32\">Section 32 — Annual leave with wages, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.annual-leave\">Annual leave: worked example</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.accident-48","title":"How quickly must we report a workplace injury?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Safety","children":[{"title":"How quickly must we report a workplace injury?","html":"<p>If the injury keeps the person off work for 48 hours or more, report in Form XI within 12 hours after the 48 hours (Central sphere, rule 7). A death must be reported forthwith, including by phone to the authorities and the family.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Rules</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.10</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 7</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s10\">Section 10 — Notice of certain accident</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.incident-reporting\">Incident reporting clock (Central Rules)</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.old-licences","title":"Are our licences under the Factories Act and CLRA Act still valid?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Transition","children":[{"title":"Are our licences under the Factories Act and CLRA Act still valid?","html":"<p>Yes, for the period for which they were issued; they must then be obtained afresh under the Code (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\" data-ref=\"osh.s119\">s.119(5)</a>). Registrations under Central labour laws are deemed registrations, subject to updating particulars (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s3\" data-ref=\"osh.s3\">s.3(8)</a>).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.3(8), 119(5)</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s119\">Section 119 — Common licence for contractor, factories and to industrial premises, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s143\">Section 143 — Repeal and Savings</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.canteen","title":"At what headcount do we need a canteen?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Welfare","children":[{"title":"At what headcount do we need a canteen?","html":"<p>Where 100 or more workers, including contract labour, are ordinarily employed (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">s.24(1)(v)</a>; rule 53). Under the Factories Act it was more than 250.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.24(1)(v)</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 53</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cmp.welfare-thresholds\">Canteen, crèche, welfare officer and safety officer thresholds</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.creche-osh-ss","title":"Which crèche threshold applies — OSH Code or SS Code?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Welfare","children":[{"title":"Which crèche threshold applies — OSH Code or SS Code?","html":"<p>Both can apply. The SS Code requires a crèche at 50 employees (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s67\" data-ref=\"ssc.s67\">Section 67</a>); the OSH Code and Central Rules at more than 50 workers (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">s.24(3)</a>; rule 58). Apply the stricter threshold and the more detailed standards (rule 58 has child-safety requirements such as CCTV and police verification).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Interpretation</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.24(3)</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 58</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/ssc.s67\">Section 67 — Creche facility</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.safety-welfare-officer","title":"At what headcount do we need a safety officer or a welfare officer?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Welfare","children":[{"title":"At what headcount do we need a safety officer or a welfare officer?","html":"<p>Safety officers: factories with 500+ workers, hazardous-process factories and building work with 250+, mines with 100+ (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\" data-ref=\"osh.s22\">s.22(2)</a>); docks from 500 in the Central Rules (rule 18). Welfare officers: factories, mines and plantations with 250+ workers (s.24(2)(iv); rule 57), one more for every additional 500.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.22(2), 24(2)(iv)</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rules 18, 20, 57</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s22\">Section 22 — Safety Committee and safety officers</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.dg.welfare-thresholds\">Welfare and safety staffing thresholds</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.transgender-facilities","title":"Does the Code require facilities for transgender employees?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Welfare","children":[{"title":"Does the Code require facilities for transgender employees?","html":"<p>Yes. Latrines and urinals (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\" data-ref=\"osh.s23\">s.23(2)(viii)</a>), and bathing places, locker rooms and rest rooms (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\" data-ref=\"osh.s24\">Section 24</a>), must be provided separately for male, female and transgender employees.</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, ss.23(2)(viii), 24</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.faq-osh-code\">FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</a>, Q14</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s23\">Section 23 — Responsibility of employer for maintaining health, safety and working conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s24\">Section 24 — Welfare facilities in the establishment, etc.</a></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"osh.faq.women-night","title":"Can women work night shifts?","generated":true,"test":false,"category":"Women","children":[{"title":"Can women work night shifts?","html":"<p>Yes, with their consent and the prescribed safeguards (<a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\" data-ref=\"osh.s43\">Section 43</a>). In the Central sphere: written consent, transport, lighting, CCTV, emergency numbers and POSH compliance (rule 83).</p><div class=\"hr-rec-meta\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-grey\">Basis: Statute</span> <span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Unverified</span></div><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Sources</h4><ul class=\"hr-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-act-2020\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 (Act No. 37 of 2020)</a>, s.43</li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/src.osh-central-rules-2026\">The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026 (G.S.R. 345(E))</a>, rule 83</li></ul><h4 class=\"hr-rec-h\">Related</h4><ul class=\"hr-related-list\"><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.s43\">Section 43 — Employment of women</a></li><li><a class=\"hr-xref\" href=\"#/id/osh.cl.women-night\">Put safeguards in place before rostering women at night</a></li></ul>"}]}],"resources":[{"id":"osh.resources.the-code-as-enacted-amended","title":"The Code (as enacted / amended)","generated":true,"aliases":["src.osh-act-2020","src.osh-act-2020-indiacode"],"children":[{"title":"The Code (as enacted / amended)","html":"<div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Citation / date</th><th>Standing</th><th>Access</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr id=\"src.osh-act-2020\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: OSH CODE.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">Act No. 37 of 2020 · 2020-09-29 · Gazette of India, Extraordinary, Part II, Section 1, No. 62, 29 September 2020</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Gazette PDF in the project (86 pages). sources/text/osh-code-2020.gazette.txt is pdftotext -layout output of that PDF, used by the transcription check. Commencement: src.osh-commencement. India Code copy: src.osh-act-2020-indiacode.</td></tr><tr id=\"src.osh-act-2020-indiacode\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020 — India Code consolidated text</strong><div><a href=\"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/bitstream/123456789/22041/1/2020-37.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official copy</a></div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">Act No. 37 of 2020 · 2020-09-29</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Retrieved from official site</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Retrieved 2026-10-22 through an AI web-fetch tool (not stored here; the shell cannot reach the host). Header &#39;[As on the 28th November, 2025]&#39;. Only footnote seen: commencement (S.O. 5321(E)). The tool could read the text only up to s.37 (s.38 cut off); later provisions and the Schedules were not seen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},{"id":"osh.resources.rules-and-orders","title":"Rules and orders","generated":true,"aliases":["src.osh-central-rules-2026"],"children":[{"title":"Rules and orders","html":"<div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Citation / date</th><th>Standing</th><th>Access</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr id=\"src.osh-central-rules-2026\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (Central) Rules, 2026</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: Central OSH Rules.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">G.S.R. 345(E) · 2026-05-08 · Gazette of India, Extraordinary, Part II, Section 3, Sub-section (i), No. 311, 8 May 2026</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},{"id":"osh.resources.notifications","title":"Notifications","generated":true,"aliases":["src.osh-commencement"],"children":[{"title":"Notifications","html":"<div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Citation / date</th><th>Standing</th><th>Access</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr id=\"src.osh-commencement\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>S.O. 5321(E): appointment of 21 November 2025 for the provisions of the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020</strong><div><a href=\"https://labour.gov.in/sites/default/files/e-noti-osh-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official copy</a></div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">S.O. 5321(E) · 2025-11-21 · Gazette of India, Extraordinary, Part II, Section 3(ii), No. 5145, 21 November 2025</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Retrieved from official site</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Retrieved 2026-10-22 through an AI web-fetch tool (not stored here; the shell cannot reach the host). As reported: appoints 21 November 2025 as the date on which &#39;the provisions of the said Code&#39; come into force, with no exceptions. Matches the India Code commencement footnote.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},{"id":"osh.resources.official-faqs-and-guidance-not-law","title":"Official FAQs and guidance (not law)","generated":true,"aliases":["src.mole-compliance-handbook","src.mole-faq-2026-03-16","src.faq-labour-codes","src.faq-osh-code","src.faq-osh-2026"],"children":[{"title":"Official FAQs and guidance (not law)","html":"<div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Citation / date</th><th>Standing</th><th>Access</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr id=\"src.mole-compliance-handbook\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>Compliance Handbook for Employers under the Four Labour Codes (Central Government Sphere)</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: labor codes compliance handbook.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">—</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Official guidance – not law</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Self-declared non-legal reference: &#39;In the event of any discrepancy between the contents of this Handbook and the provisions of the new Labour Codes, the latter shall prevail.&#39; Contains at least one mis-citation observed so far (wage periods attributed to &#39;Section 6&#39;; the provision is s.16/s.17). Undated on its face.</td></tr><tr id=\"src.mole-faq-2026-03-16\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>Additional FAQs on Labour Codes (as on 16.03.2026)</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: FAQ Labor Codes.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">2026-03-16</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Official guidance – not law</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Issuer attribution to MoLE is inferred from content, not confirmed. FAQs are not law.</td></tr><tr id=\"src.faq-labour-codes\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>FAQs on Labour Codes (undated)</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: FAQ labourr code.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">—</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Official guidance – not law</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Issuer and date unconfirmed.</td></tr><tr id=\"src.faq-osh-code\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>FAQs on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (OSH) Code, 2020 (undated)</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: FAQ OSH code.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">—</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Official guidance – not law</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Undated 19-question FAQ in the project (read through the project&#39;s text extraction). Guidance only. Q1 describes the factory threshold change as from 10/20 to 20/40 workers; Q3 describes &#39;flexibility to extend daily hours with the consent of workers on payment of overtime&#39;.</td></tr><tr id=\"src.faq-osh-2026\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>FAQ on OSH&amp;WC Code, 2020 (as on 13.03.2026)</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: FAQ OSH.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">—</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Official guidance – not law</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">FAQ dated &#39;As on 13.03.2026&#39; in the project, with a disclaimer that the Code prevails. Item 1 states that Central rules under ss.23 and 24 apply to all ports and docks across India; item 2 states that hazardous-process and dangerous-operation workers get pre-employment and periodic examinations irrespective of age.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},{"id":"osh.resources.predecessor-acts-repealed","title":"Predecessor Acts (repealed)","generated":true,"aliases":["src.fa-1948-kerala","src.fa-1948-haryana","src.clra-1970-haryana","src.ismw-1979-indiacode"],"children":[{"title":"Predecessor Acts (repealed)","html":"<div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Citation / date</th><th>Standing</th><th>Access</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr id=\"src.fa-1948-kerala\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Factories Act, 1948 – copy on the Factories and Boilers Department, Kerala website</strong></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">Act No. 63 of 1948</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Copy from a non-authoritative site</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Retrieved 2026-10-22 through an AI web-fetch tool (not stored here; the shell cannot reach the host). Not an authoritative text; truncated after s.59(1). Read: s.2(m) (&#39;ten or more&#39; with power; &#39;twenty or more&#39; without), ss.40B(1) (one thousand), 46(1) (more than two hundred and fifty), 48(1) (more than thirty women), 49(1) (five hundred), 51 (48 hours a week), 54 (nine hours a day). An India Code copy (bitstream 6323) read in the same session shows &#39;twenty&#39;/&#39;forty&#39; in s.2(m) and cites a Jharkhand amendment; it appears to be a State-amended version and was not used. Retrieved from: https://www.fabkerala.gov.in/images/act_rules/act1948.pdf</td></tr><tr id=\"src.fa-1948-haryana\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Factories Act, 1948 – copy on the Haryana Labour Department website</strong></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">Act No. 63 of 1948</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Copy from a non-authoritative site</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Retrieved 2026-10-22 through an AI web-fetch tool (not stored here; the shell cannot reach the host). Not an authoritative text; latest amendment shown Act 20 of 1987. Read: s.2(m) (ten/twenty). Retrieved from: https://www.hrylabour.gov.in/staticdocs/labourActpdfdocs/The_FactoriesAct.pdf</td></tr><tr id=\"src.clra-1970-haryana\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970 – copy on the Haryana Labour Department website</strong></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">Act No. 37 of 1970</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Copy from a non-authoritative site</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Retrieved 2026-10-22 through an AI web-fetch tool (not stored here; the shell cannot reach the host). Not an authoritative text; latest amendment shown Act 4 of 2005. Read: ss.1(4) (twenty or more workmen), 7(1), 10(1), 12(1), 21(4), 23 (up to three months or ₹1,000). The India Code page for this Act could not be fetched (robots restriction). Retrieved from: https://audit.hrylabour.gov.in/staticdocs/labourActpdfdocs/Contract_Labour_Act.pdf</td></tr><tr id=\"src.ismw-1979-indiacode\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Inter-State Migrant Workmen (Regulation of Employment and Conditions of Service) Act, 1979 – India Code text</strong><div><a href=\"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/bitstream/123456789/15984/1/34.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official copy</a></div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">Act No. 30 of 1979</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Primary (law)</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">Retrieved from official site</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Retrieved 2026-10-22 through an AI web-fetch tool (not stored here; the shell cannot reach the host). No as-on date. Read: ss.1(4)(a) (five or more), 14 (displacement allowance: 50% of monthly wages or ₹75, whichever higher), 15 (journey allowance for outward and return journeys).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},{"id":"osh.resources.secondary-material-not-authority","title":"Secondary material (not authority)","generated":true,"aliases":["src.labour-codes-booklet","src.fa-1948-advocatekhoj"],"children":[{"title":"Secondary material (not authority)","html":"<div class=\"hr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"hr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Citation / date</th><th>Standing</th><th>Access</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr id=\"src.labour-codes-booklet\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>New Labour Code for New India (information booklet)</strong><div class=\"hr-muted\">File: labor code discussion.pdf</div></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">—</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Secondary – not authority</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-green\">In project files</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Promotional/explanatory material. Not to be used as the sole basis of any legal statement.</td></tr><tr id=\"src.fa-1948-advocatekhoj\"><td data-label=\"Document\"><strong>The Factories Act, 1948 – advocatekhoj.com bare act pages</strong></td><td data-label=\"Citation / date\">Act No. 63 of 1948</td><td data-label=\"Standing\">Secondary – not authority</td><td data-label=\"Access\"><span class=\"hr-badge hr-badge-amber\">Copy from a non-authoritative site</span></td><td data-label=\"Notes\">Retrieved 2026-10-22 through an AI web-fetch tool (not stored here; the shell cannot reach the host). Secondary reproduction. Read: ss.59(1) (twice the ordinary rate after nine hours a day or 48 a week), 66(1)(b) (women only between 6 a.m. and 7 p.m.; States may vary but not between 10 p.m. and 5 a.m.), 79(1) (240 days; one day for every twenty), 92 (up to two years or ₹1 lakh; ₹1,000 a day continuing). Retrieved from: https://www.advocatekhoj.com/library/bareacts/factories/</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]}]};
